INSERT INTO `review` VALUES (8,270,'1998-01-06 01:17:45',3215,'vader','<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/garage/letter/e.gif\" alt=\"E\">very\nnow and again you come across a kit that\'s such a bargain that you\ncan\'t pass it by.  AMT/ERTL\'s 1:6 Darth Vader kit is a great bet to fall in\nthat category.  For $25, you\'re buying a well sculpted and cast kit of one\nof film\'s great villains.\n\n<p>\nVader consists of 14 gray vinyl and styrene parts in a full color box\nwith instructions targeted at the beginning figure kit modeler.  Trimming\nthe parts is a snap with the help of a sharp knife and a sink full of hot\nwater.  All of the parts fit cleanly with some slight warming to get them\nto conform.  I did need to do some minor puttying around the waist seam and\nleft boot, but otherwise all of the breaklines joined neatly.\n\n<p>\nI either fared better than Steve Goodrich or I was more tolerant when it\ncame to the two cape pieces.  I noted only two small drips in the slush\ncast parts, both of which were hidden from view when attached to the\nfigure.  I did think the cape was a little thick, especially at the edges,\nso I cut the vinyl at an angle to make it appear slightly thinner.  The\nwaist section took a small amount of coaxing to fit properly, but shaped up\nnicely when all was said and done.\n\n<p>\nPainting Vader?  Can you say black?  It\'s actually a little more\ninteresting than that, as you get to work with flat black for the clothing\nand cape and semi-gloss black for the gloves, boots, and armor.  The only\nother colors you\'ll need are gunmetal, silver, red, green, and blue.  I\nalso used some Tamiya clear red to color Vader\'s eyes and light saber beam.\nSo we\'re looking at a kit that\'s probably a great idea for kids and people\njust breaking into the hobby with a limited selection of paints.\n\n<p>\nAs with some of the &quot;big company&quot; figures, the sculptor wasn\'t credited\nanywhere that I could see.  A shame, since the sculpting on the kit is\nquite nice.  The torso looked a bit flat from the side, but this might have\nbeen correctable by heating and warping the parts a bit.  Detailing under\nVader\'s helmet is there for folks who want to leave it unattached for an\noccasional cheap thrill.\n\n<p>\nFigure stability is just fine, and enhanced by filling the lower legs\nwith plaster.  The attacking light saber pose is suitable for placement\nwith some of the 1:6 scale Luke Skywalker pieces or even Grey Zone\'s way\ncool Jedi Master kit.  Basher\'s can go wild lighting Vader\'s chest panel or\neven his light saber by flush mounting a red LED between his hand and a\nsmall notch drilled in the pommel.\n\n<p>\nAMT/ERTL scores big with the best kit in their Star Wars line so far.  It\nstacks up favorably with Kaiyodo\'s 1:6 kit, and will cost you far less.\nBeginners will want to give this kit a serious look, as will adult kit\nbuilders who are hunting for a great project to share with their kids.'),(10,5066,'1998-04-22 13:36:03',4281,'flearider','The Flea Rider is a new fantasy-Sci Fi kit from CM Studio.\n A favorite of the model competition at the Mad Model Party in Pasedena,\n Calif last May, The Flea Rider is now available as a model kit.\n <p>\n   CM Studio is owned by master sculptor Charlie McGrady who has created this\nwonderful new creature that will stand out among all other kits on your\nshelf. Charlie has created detailed sculptures of wildlife, western art,\ndinosaurs and many other subjects for years. Based upon his vast knowledge of\nprehistoric and modern day animal life, the realism of Charlie\'s Flea Rider\ncreation gives you the impression it is about to move at any moment.\n<p>\n   A giant flea-like insect with raptor feet is the mount of a Rhinoceros\nbeetle-like alien in this original model. The beetle holds a weapon in one hand\nand the decapitated head of the alien it has defeated in the other. The\nheadless remains of the fallen creature lie below the victor.\n<p>\n   The completed piece stands about a foot tall and almost as long. Charlie\nwill sell the unbuilt kit &quot;as is&quot; or built and painted or even finished with\nyour customizing ideas. Please call Charlie for pricing and other\ninformation. Many other kits available as well.'),(11,330,'1998-01-22 16:27:20',3279,'dragon','<p>To my knowledge, Grant\'s one man operation has never been featured in &quot;Gremlins in the Garage&quot;. So, let\nme introduce you readers to Mr. Grant Biffin. Grant is located in the &quot;land down under&quot; (Australia,\nmates!). He is a celebrated f.x. man in film and local t.v. Grant is also one seriously cool dude and a\nprimo-great guy who finds time to produce his own sculpting work via his company; Cast from the\nDungeon. </p>\n<p>&quot;Dragon&quot; is Grant\'s fourth kit and is cast in four pieces. The shield is made of fiber- glass - it\'s quite thin for\na hollow cast, but a huge amount stronger than a resin counterpart would be. The dragon horns are solid\nresin and the actual head is a hollow cast, up to the buyer in resin at $130.00 or fiberglass at $110.00.\nResin-o-holics, swallow your pride and go for the cheaper and stronger fiberglass version! While fiberglass\nstrands flow on the surface of the backside of the shield and (one assumes) inside of the dragon head, the\nouter surface of said pieces are as slick as glass. Grant even fills in any stray air bubble spots and polishes\nthem off. The builder will find only serious actual hairline mold seam lines to sand off on horns and head.\nThis is a simple build up and even the anchor screws to attach head to shield are provided in the kit. As\nwell, a rubber or plastic coated hanging hook is included with each kit. This is a piece, by the by, you hang\non the wall. The shield is about 9&quot; by 12&quot; so scale may very well be 1/1 and you can either treat this kit as\na display kit or an actual trophy... tell your friends you bagged this one in your back yard, etc... </p>\n<p>Painting is all up to you. I did the review copy in wild metallic green, purple, and blues and that was just the\nbase coat! I would suggest, because of its naive charm, this would be a drop dead piece for the novice air\nbrush user to play with. By Grant\'s own admission, he\'s not the greatest one to finish a work of 3-D art;\nhence, he\'s done Dragon in a drab black on the cover of the box, giving it uninspired red eyes with a black\nslit, and solid yellow teeth. Well, what the hell is our chosen &quot;hobby&quot; all about if not to constantly learn and\nimprove our work on a day to day basis. Trust me dear readers, this is one serious kick ass kit that has an\namazing range of potential! </p>\n<p>A thumbs up kit all the way!! </p>\n<p>International money order only should be made out to Grant Biffin and sent to: </p>\n<dl><dd>\nCast from the Dungeon<br>\n74 Queen St.<br>\nNarellan 2567<br>\nN.S.W. Australia<br>\n</dl>\n<p>Above prices include air mail postage. </p>\n'),(12,270,'1999-09-28 21:42:05',3304,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/d.gif\" alt=\"D\" WIDTH=\"22\" HEIGHT=\"29\">ark Horse has finally released\nRandy Bowen\'s LONG awaited Frankenstein kit.  Frank was one\nof the first kits announced by Dark Horse models, and I\'ve heard several\nstories explaining why the kit was delayed for so many years.  (The most\ndisturbing placed the kit molds in the hands of an unscrupulous casting\ncompany who was selling the kit on the black market in the far east!)\nFortunately, Frank is finally available in what may be one of the most\ninteresting poses yet: chained to a stone chair, and struggling to\nescape.\n\n<p>\nThe first thing one notices when hefting (and I mean hefting) the kit\nis its weight.  Frank is cold cast entirely in porcelain, and weighs on\nthe order of 7 lbs.  This is one kit that you don\'t have to worry about\ntoppling or blowing over!  Dark Horse has apparently learned some valuable\nlessons about packaging their porcelain kits, as all of the major parts\nare snuggly fit in a custom made Styrofoam insert.  The entire package\nis neatly presented in a box featuring painted artwork by John Bolton,\na famed horror comic artist.\n\n<p>\nThis 1:8 scale Aurora-tribute kit is separated into 7 porcelain pieces.\n(Base, Chair/body, head, hands, and head blocks.)  Chains, eyelets, and eyehooks\nare also included for detailing.  I was a little unhappy to see that Frank\'s\nbody and chair were molded together.  The body is undercut a bit, but could\nhave been improved by deeper undercutting or complete separation from the\nchair.  Perhaps w/ painting, it will be much less obvious that the kit\nwas molded in this fashion.  Test fitting of the parts indicates that the\nbreak lines are quite natural (head -> shirt collar, arms -> sleeves,\nchair -> floor, head blocks -> chair), and will require little (if any)\nputtying.  There is no flash to speak of in this cold cast kit, but some\nadditional material inside Frank\'s collar will have to be removed for the\nhead to fit in w/ no noticeable gaps.\n\n<p>\nDetail in the kit is a mixed bag IMO.  The face is the most striking\nelement of the figure.  Frank\'s facial expression is perfect.  Clearly\nthe monster is straining against his bonds: mouth grimacing, eyes squinted,\nand neck tendons stretched to the breaking point.  Likewise, the hands are\nwell crafted w/ stitching, wounds, and tendons pronounced.  In contrast\nto these parts, the rest of the kit seems almost bland.  True, there\'s only\nso much detail you can put into Frank\'s plain outfit, or a chair and stone\nwork.  While detail in say, Frank\'s torn jacket, is good, it falls a bit\nshort of the wonderful attention Bowen has obviously paid to the face.\n\n<p>\nThe only obvious difficulty in assembling this kit is related to the\neyehooks which serve as hardpoints for the chains.  A 1/16th inch hole\nmust be drilled into each mounting point: Two in the base, three in the\nbottom of the chair, and in each wrist manacle.  While the base and chair\nholes should pose little problem, the porcelain into which the wrist holes\nmust be drilled is quite thin.  A high-speed drill (perhaps a Dremel tool)\nmay help prevent shattering of the delicate material.\n\n<p>\nThe instruction sheet is formatted in obvious homage to the Aurora-style\nthat so many of these kits mimic.  Assembly instructions are clear, and\nthe obvious danger of shattering porcelain during drilling is spelled out\nclearly.  A specific painting scheme is not spelled out, rather the\nbuilder is referred to the box art.  I would have appreciated at least some\ncolor suggestions in addition to the artwork.  The instruction sheet also\ncontains a wonderful section describing basic modeling/painting techniques.\nWashes and drybrushing are clearly spelled out for beginning modelers,\nand since I\'m not an expert by any stretch of the imagination, I learned\nsome things too! :)\n\n<p>\nOnce again, the question:  At a suggested retail price of $99.99 is\nDark Horse\'s Frankenstein worth it?  My answer is yes for several reasons.\nDespite my \"complaints,\" Bowen has produced a quality piece of work, at\na reasonable price.  (Compare Frank\'s price to other Dark Horse porcelain\nkits, and it\'ll look like a bargain.)  The second reason is somewhat more\npersonal.  Garage kit building is an expensive hobby.  I can\'t afford to\nbuy every mummy kit, every Frank kit, etc. available.  As such, I tend to\nselect ONE kit of a specific character that I find particularly dis-\ntinctive.  (In detail, quality, or pose.)  For my mummy kit, I chose\nThe Shape of Things \"Curse of Sekenenrah,\" a particularly interesting\nmummy kit IMO.  When compared to the traditional \"lumbering w/ arms\noutstretched\" poses of most other Frank kits, Dark Horse\'s offering hits\nthe mark in both pose distinctiveness as well as quality.  So from the\nstandpoint of producing an interesting collection, Bowen\'s Frankenstein\nis an excellent choice for me.  (That is NOT to say that the Horizon,\nBilliken, or any other Frank is uninteresting or without merit.)  Anyway,\nenough rambling.  The bottom line: Dark Horse\'s Frank is an excellent\n(but not perfect) kit.'),(13,270,'1999-09-28 21:41:51',3960,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/a.gif\" alt=\"A\" WIDTH=\"23\" HEIGHT=\"31\">s self-appointed net-herald of\nall new Dark Horse model releases, here\'s the scoop on their most recent\nrelease: The Mummy.\n\n<p>\n   Upon first glance, I thought to myself, WOW!  This box is nearly as\nbig as the colossal Bride of Frankenstein packaging!  I hadn\'t expected\nthe mummy kit to be very large, from the few pictures I\'d seen in the\nmagazine ads.  Opening the beautifully painted package revealed that\nShane Mahan has designed one elaborate kit!\n\n<p>\n   Like all of Dark Horse\'s offerings, the mummy is a cold-cast porcelain\nfigure kit in 1:8 scale.  The packaging, instruction sheet, and basic\nfigure/base design are all in homage to the classic Aurora monsters of the\n60\'s.  In this case, the classic Karloff mummy is presented on an elaborate\nsand-covered base.  A crumbling wall of frescoed stonework backs up the\nfigure who is shambling towards a squat pillar.  A full-sized sarcophagus\ncompletes the piece, and a \"papyrus\" scroll is included for detailing.\n\n<p>\n   First some comments on the figure.  Breaklines?  What breaklines?  I\ndon\'t anticipate the need for any puttying on this kit.  Dark Horse kits\nare generally free of pinholes and casting defects.  (Although I did\nnotice a wee bit of flashing on the base.)  The breaklines are at the neck\nand arms, and the parts are keyed to socket neatly into the bandages.\n\n<p>\n   The only word that comes to mind to describe the sculpting of the face is\nexquisite.  It actually looks like someone shrunk Karloff\'s mummified face\ndown to 1:8 scale!  Easily one of the best faces I\'ve seen since Bowen\'s\nFrankenstein or the Janus Lon Chaney kit.  The body is a slightly different\nstory.  I suspect that Mahan opted to go for a more realistic portrayal\nof a mummy\'s body - he\'s scrawny!  The bandages hug his thin body in some\nplaces and sag in others.  Certainly more realistic than the bandages\nwrapped around Karloff\'s frame in the film, but folks expecting the kit\nto duplicate the costume exactly may find it a little odd.  Myself?  I\nkind of like it.  The hands are also well detailed, and share the same\nthin, emaciated, \"look\" as the body.\n\n<p>\n   Perhaps my only complaint about the kit is the way the figure fits (or\nrather doesn\'t fit) on the base.  There are clear footprints pressed into\nthe sand where the figure is meant to stand, and the feet fit well into the\nindentations.  However, it seems the leg spacing shifted slightly after\nthe indentations were made.  The indentations are improperly spaced, perhaps\nby as much as 1/8\".  I plan to widen the rear indentation and fill in the far\nside with putty carved to look like sand or rock.\n\n<p>\n   The sarcophagus is a nice touch.  The casket and lid are cast separately,\nand may be placed as desired on the base.  It appears that Im-Ho-Tep was\nnot a wealthy individual, as the sarcophagus seems rather plain!  The\ntraditional Egyptian face and some hieroglyphs are present, but it could\'ve\ndone w/ additional detail.\n\n<p>\n   The base itself is gorgeous, and perhaps one of my favorite things about\nthe kit.  It measures roughly 10\" in diameter.  The sand and stonework cast\ncleanly, and are well detailed.  The pillar in the middle of the scene was\napparently \"boxed\" for shipping.  The mummy has splintered the enclosure, and\nbits of broken wood lay around and about the pillar and in the sand, another\nnice touch.  The pillar itself contains additional hieroglyphs, and like all\nthe stonework, will be fun to drybrush and weather.  The paper scroll that\nwas included with the kit is a simply collection of hieroglyphs printed on\nbrown paper.  A nice idea that I wish Dark Horse would have adopted for the\nbook pages in their invisible man kit.\n\n<p>\n   Clocking in at a retail price of $150, like many of the Dark Horse kits,\nI would only recommend this piece to Dark Horse completists or mummy nuts.\nIf you\'re after an Egyptian undead to add to your collection, you\'ll find\nmore bang for your buck with the Menagerie or Horizon Mummies, or even\nthe Curse of Sekenen-rah by The Shape of Things.  The base really makes\nthe Dark Horse kit something special, but I\'m not sure that it will prove\nincentive enough for many modelers to part w/ $150.'),(14,270,'1998-01-24 13:17:10',3310,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/d.gif\" alt=\"D\" WIDTH=\"22\" HEIGHT=\"29\">ark Horse Comics latest\n\"garage kit\" offering in their Universal movie monsters line is the Wolfman.\nThe kit is well packaged, with\nseveral Styrofoam inserts to protect some of the more delicate cold\ncast porcelain pieces, and features a beautiful painting of the subject.\n\n<p>\nLike most of the Dark Horse kits, the wolfman is sculpted in 1:8\nscale and features an elaborate base in homage to the old Aurora monster\nkits.  All of the pieces are cleanly cast with no obvious flashing or\nair bubbles.  The majority of the pieces are logs or sticks, and will\nultimately become part of the base.  The figure itself is cast in one\nlarge body section, two feet, two arms, and two hands.  Breaklines at\nthe hands and feet are at the cuffs, and should require no puttying.  The\nshoulder breaklines follow a \"pleat\" in the wolfman\'s shirt, and fit\nfairly closely.  Minimal puttying is needed.\n\n<p>\nThe first thing that strikes me as I look at the kit\'s pose, is how\nsimilar the finished piece will be to the old Aurora wolfman.  The figure\nis crouching slightly with both hands raised.  The Dark Horse base is\nmuch more elaborate however, and is festooned with logs, and a tall spindly\ntree.  I was a bit disappointed to see this similarity.  One aspect that\nI genuinely love about most Dark Horse kits is their distinctiveness:\nFrankenstein bound in a chair rather than lumbering with outstretched arms,\nthe gillman swimming along a lake bed rather than lurching forward, etc.\nWhen compared with some of their other kits, the wolfman falls short of\nthe mark.\n\n<p>\nMinor gripes with the pose aside, the detail in the kit is superlative.\nFolds and pleats in the fabric are all well defined, and begging for dry\nbrushing.  Likewise, detail in the fur, face, and hands is quite crisp.\nApparently, the \"logs and branches\" on the base were cast from sticks\nand twigs, so as you might expect, the detail is near perfect.\n\n<p>At a retail price of $150, I\'d have to suggest prospective buyers to\nseriously consider whether or not they want a wolfman kit that is QUITE\nsimilar to the Aurora (or inexpensive Luminator) kit.  Dark Horse complet-\nists, or those looking for a first wolfman to add to their figure kit\ncollections shouldn\'t be disappointed with the quality of the casting or\nsculpting.  Compared to some of the other kits in the Dark Horse line,\nthis one just doesn\'t seem as \"distinctive.\"'),(15,270,'1998-01-22 16:44:40',3311,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/j.gif\" alt=\"J\" WIDTH=\"20\" HEIGHT=\"29\">ust so folks don\'t get\nthe impression that I only buy and build &quot;mainstream&quot; figure kits, here\'s a\nreview of an honest to goodness garage kit produced by Deceptions Ltd. - Hybrid.\n\n<p>\n   Hybrid is a 1:5 scale resin kit of a hybrid alien/predator and his victim,\na nearly disemboweled predator.  No base is included.  The pose is dynamic to\nsay the least!  The predator lies on his belly, pinned beneath the foot of\nthe leering hybrid.  The hybrid is pulling the predator\'s head up by the\ndreadlocks, and is prepared to deliver the killing blow with his free hand.\n This predator is <b>not</b> having a good day!\n\n<p>\n   This is one of four hybrid alien/predator designs I\'ve seen, and is a\nfairly interesting amalgam of the two creatures.  The basic predator head is\nelongated slightly and adorned with dreadlocks.  As is typical with most\naliens, the creature has no obvious eyes.  The Predator\'s four-tooth inner\nmouth has been replaced with a grinning set of &quot;alien teeth.&quot;  In designing\nthe hybrid\'s body, sculptor Larry Cole has gone for a mixture of Giger\'s\nalien and the hunting armor often worn by the predators.  Ribs and boney\nplates cover the creatures body, and a spiked tail rounds out the monster.\n Body detail on the hybrid is a bit coarse.\n\n<p>\n   Cole has apparently cannibalized parts of the predator from his previous\nBatman vs. Predator kit, Final Conflict, in producing Hybrid.  The fallen\npredator appears to be decked out in the same armor, and there are places in\nthe casting where it was obvious that the sculpture had been cut and rebuilt.\n Taken as a whole, the predator is a bit too stocky.  While his face is nicely\nsculpted, the body armor lacks the detail needed to make it truly\ninteresting.  The predator\'s wounds have received a fair amount of sculpting\nattention.  Claw slashes line the predator\'s flank, and his throat has been\ntorn open to the point that might well confuse a face hugger!\n\n<p>\n   Overall casting quality was quite poor in my opinion.  The largest air\nbubble in the kit was nearly the size of a peanut!  Several chunks of the\nmold were trapped in the undercuts, and apparently tore as the casting was\nremoved.  It was little comfort to know that the fellow who got the kit cast\nafter mine would have <b>more</b> defects to fix than I did.  Sculpture\ncomponents\nwere also cut roughly prior to casting, resulting in mismatches when the kit\nwas assembled.  Get the putty out folks, this is a &quot;builder\'s kit.&quot;\n\n<p>\n   To provide some idea of the resculpting needed to finish the kit, consider\nthe predator who was cast in four major parts, torso/head, arms (2), and\nlower body.  (Each of the four outer teeth and several dreads were also cast\nseparately, but assembled cleanly.)  Numerous bubbles and voids must be\nfilled with putty and resculpted.  The predator\'s hide is fairly detailed, so\nscales must be sculpted back in.  The upper and lower body sections and arms\nwere then pinned and bonded together.  Gaps between the arms and torso and\naround the waist were as large as 1/8&quot; and needed to be filled, built up, and\nresculpted.  The hybrid required similar treatment, along with resculpting\ndreads and spikes which failed to fill out in the casting.\n\n<p>\n   After puttying and sanding, the figures were painted separately.  I\nhonestly do not consider the construction of a base for this kit a cosmetic\namenity.  Since the two figures are connected at the hybrid\'s foot and hand\nand predator\'s waist and hair, I feel a base is <b>needed</b> to bolster the\nstrength of the assembly.  Some scraps of wood and super sculpey turned into\na Gigeresque battleground.  After it was built and painted, it was time to\nmove on to the final stage of the assembly.\n\n<p>\n   An ambiguous instruction sheet was not included in my kit (I had to call\nand request one).  The sheet &quot;illustrates&quot; the proper placement of the\npredator\'s dreadlocks so that they will interweave with the hybrid\'s fingers.\n I did my best to follow the instructions, and decided that the result looked\ntwo &quot;plain&quot;.  I sculpted several additional dreads and wove them in to fill\nout the predator\'s head as well as to improve the appearance that the hybrid\nhas a good grip on him.\n\n<p>\n   All told, the project took me around 60-70 hours including all the\nresculpting, scratch building the base, painting, etc.  While I was content\nwith the finished product, I felt the process could have been much less\npainful (Should a hobby be painful?) had more care been taken in the\nseparation and casting phases of production.\n\n<p>\n   Hybrid is limited to a production run of 100 pieces and retails for a\nwhopping $295.  Once again, this is a 1:5 kit that contains two figures, and\nthere <b>is</b> a lot of resin in it.  Unfortunately, I cannot recommend this kit\ndue to the poor casting quality and lack of any kind of base (needed in my\nopinion for\nstructural soundness).  While the finished product is rather striking, the\namount of work required to get it there will prove excessive for most garage\nkitters.'),(16,270,'1998-01-22 16:45:41',3328,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/w.gif\" alt=\"W\" WIDTH=\"37\" HEIGHT=\"30\">ives seem to get a bum wrap\nin this hobby.  I don\'t know how many\narticles I\'ve read in Kitbuilders or elsewhere lamenting that &quot;wives just\ndon\'t understand our fascination with figure kits.&quot;  I think it\'s a little\nshortsighted to assume that women can\'t or don\'t enjoy modeling.  Sillier\nstill, is the assumption that women don\'t like scary critters.  While I can\'t\nsay that my wife aspires to become a master modeler, I\'m happy to say that\nshe likes monsters.  On several occasions, she\'s handpicked kits for OUR\ncollection.  One such recent &quot;pick&quot; is a character featured in one of her\nfavorite movies, &quot;Cat\'s Eye.&quot;\n\n<p>\n<a href=\"http://www.dimensionaldesigns.com/cat_troll.html\">Dimensional Designs\' &quot;Cat Troll&quot;</a>\nis a 1:1 scale limited edition kit of the\ntroll character featured in the final segment of the film &quot;Cat\'s Eye.&quot;  The\ntroll is roughly 10&quot; tall.  The movie character and figure kit both brandish\ntiny swords, and are decked out in what can only be described as a jester\'s\noutfit.  The bell laden jester\'s cap harshly contrasts with the troll\'s\nreptilian features and tiny razor-sharp teeth.  This parallels the fact that\nwhile the troll IS rather menacing in the last portion of the film, there\'s\nalso a sense of comic relief as he\'s first thwarted, then tormented, by the\nfilm\'s unlikely hero, the cat.\n\n<p>\n   The kit is cast in 16 white resin pieces and includes tiny bells and a\nlong feather to detail the troll\'s hat.  An instruction sheet and reference\nphoto are also included.  Casting quality is easily on par with other\ncompanies\' work.  Seam lines were not pronounced and cleaned up easily.  Only\na few air bubbles were present in areas that are visible once the kit is\nassembled.  The parts were well keyed and fit easily.\n\n<p>\n   The head is cast in two sections, the upper part of the head and the lower\nmandible.  Since the troll\'s mouth is quite wide and slightly open, the\ninside of the mouth is fairly detailed.  As such, these pieces must be primed\nand painted prior to final assembly on the body.  Once the head is in place,\nsome putty work is required to blend the sections of the hood that were cast\nseparately and on the body.  Some puttying is also needed on the &quot;tassels&quot;\nsticking out of the jester\'s cap, at the wrists, and at the waist.  Once this\nwas accomplished, I masked off the already painted mouth and primed the\nfigure.  All this information is irrelevant to modelers who prefer to paint\nsubassemblies.  However, I prefer to build and prime as much as possible to\navoid having to putty and repaint later.\n\n<p>\n   The likeness of the figure to the troll in the movie is quite good.  The\ndetail in the feet, hands, and face is blocked in a bit roughly, and could\nhave stood some additional refinement.  For example, wrinkles on the troll\'s\nface are much more exaggerated than those on the character in the film.  The\ntradeoff here is that a fairly dramatic color scheme for the troll\'s skin is\neasy to achieve with a bit of drybrushing.  The clothing is nicely textured.\n Once again, drybrushing is the obvious technique to achieve a nice &quot;burlap&quot;\nappearance.\n\n<p>\n   The only sore point in the kit?  The base clearly falls short of the mark\nwhen compared to the rest of the sculpture.  The base consists of a plain\npiece of resin with indentations for the troll\'s feet.  It does nothing for\nthe kit, and in my opinion would actually diminish the impact of the finished\npiece.  The base originally contained the film\'s title, but I still feel that\nthe figure deserves a more creative setting.  I\'ll leave my solution to this\n&quot;problem&quot; as a surprise for when I finish painting the figure and provide\nDenis with a picture for the web page.\n\n<p>\n   Another, exceedingly minor, nitpick seems to apply to a great many of\nDimensional Design\'s kits - who sculpted the piece?  This information should\nappear prominently on the packaging or instructions for every kit made.\n Sculptors and casters alike deserve equal billing for the garage kits they\nwork so hard on.\n\n<p>\n   At $100, I think the Cat Troll is a nicely executed kit of a charming\n(does that word apply to monsters?) character.  Aside from the lackluster\nbase, the kit is quite solid.  I had another project lined up when the Cat\nTroll arrived, but this was a case of a kit looking like so much fun that it\nHAD to be tackled immediately!  Is there any greater praise for a model kit?'),(17,316,'1998-01-22 16:47:25',3415,'tribute','<b><img src=\"../garage/letter/w.gif\" alt=\"W\" WIDTH=\"37\" HEIGHT=\"30\"></b>hen\nI first saw Fairbanx Models tribute kit advertised in a popular\nkit magazine I knew I had to have one, being a long time fan of\nOzzy Osbourne and the late great Randy Rhodes.  At first sight the\npackaging is very neat, with photo copied box art of the kit.  Also\nincluded is a detailed building and painting instruction sheet.\nThe kit is sculpted by the very talented Rick Wyatt and I have to say,\nhe did the likenesses very well considering they are both in a\nstraining/growling pose.  This eight peice (including base) beauty\nis casted very well with little to no mold lines on all parts.  Most\ndetail can be found in the face and hair, but lacks a little in the\nclothing.  To be fair, I looked at the CD cover and found little\nwrinkling or folds in the photo, so it is accurate.  The base on\nthe other hand is pretty boring, having a flat stage peice with\nfour stairs leading off in ghe background witha small amp in the\ncorner.  I\'d have to say the thing that really impressed me was\nthe etched outline of Ozzy\'s tattoos, even the O-Z-Z-Y on the\nfingers of his left hand.  Also worth mentioning is Randys detailed\nflying guitar with a packet that includes the vibrato arm, guitar\nstrap and cord.  The pieces of this kit fit together slightly loose,\nwhich is good considering you\'ll need a little play in interlocking\nthe two figures.\n<p>\nAll said and done, Fairbanx Models has one sharp kit to\noffer, definately worth paying TRIBUTE to!'),(18,4957,'1998-01-22 16:49:49',4088,'nefarious','<img src=\"../garage/letter/w.gif\" alt=\"W\" WIDTH=\"37\" HEIGHT=\"30\">hen I walked into the\n<a href=\"chiller_95.html\">October Chiller Theatre Show</a>, the first kit I\nspotted was Dr. Nefarious by a company called \n<a href=\"http://laddie.chem.pdx.edu/~melissa/fantasy.html\">Fantasy World Studios</a>.\nI walked\naround the whole show in search of a few kits I wanted, but the Doc had stuck in\nmy mind. I walked back through twice before I plunked down my cash, but I\'m glad\nI finally bit the bullet. According to the sculptor,  the kit is sort of loosely\nbased on Jack &quot;Dr. Death&quot; Kevorkian and depicts the results if he were to use\nsome of his tehniques on himself.\n<p>\nDr. Nefarious is a 1/6 scale resin kit in 6 pieces. The kit depicts a\ndecayed, disembowelled zombie wearing a medical lab coat. The animated pose has\nthe knock-kneed doc shuffling across a tile base dragging his right foot behind\nwith a trail of goo to mark his passing. He leans forward with his right arm\nbehind clutching a turkey baster sized enabalming syringe as it he is about to\ndeliver an uppercut blow to his victim. Surface detail abounds with his face\nfixed in an evil rictus, the right side of his skull cracked open to reveal his\ndecaying brains, his abdomen flayed open to reveal his visceral organs. The end\nof his small intestine hangs loose over his belt to drain the mush in his\ninnards. There are numerous other festering wounds on  the rest of his body.\n<p>\nAs for the kit, it lives up to the pose. The 6 pieces include the tile\nbase, The legs and lower abdomen, the upper torso head and upper arms, two\nforearms, and a syringe to detail the embalmer. All were well cast with only a\ntiny seam line to deal with. Best part is that all the joints are hidden, so no\nputty work is required! He fits into the base by a cast peg from the left foot.\nI pinned the major joints and pinned through the right foot to secure him. The\nneedle for the embalmer is ground down but is sill somewhat sharp. I ground down\na little more and covered the tip with 5 min. epoxy to simulate fluids oozing\nfrom the thing .I assembled and painted the doc with the usual zombie\narmementarium of colors. Cool thing is, the surface detail lends itself to\nembellishment. I mixed five minute epoxy with  billius yellow green to simulate\npurile body fluids and then trailed this from various wounds,the end of the\nintestine, and from the cracked open skull to depict the festering ooze that\nanimates the doc.I also added a meat thermometer to his intestines as depicted\nin the accompanying comic, fashioned from a model plane part and a piece of\nwire. I stuck mostly with a rotted flesh look for the kit. The one at the FWS\nbooth at Chiller was done  in blues and greens which lended to its more\ncartoonish appearance. You can paint it any way you want and really let your\nimagination run wild.\n<p>\nThis was the first kit I had seen from Fantasy World and I must say I\'m\nimpressed. The casting, the thoughtful part layout, the surface detail, the\npose, the full color artwork on the box, and the comic book that came with the\nkit make this one a winner in my book. If you like gore or living dead type\nkits, you should look this one up. FWS also makes a Rod Serling kit called &quot;Mr\nT-Zone&quot; and an undead &quot;Jester&quot;. If they are anything like the doc in terms of\nquality, they\'d be worth a look. I know FWS will be one of my first stops at the\nSpring Chiller show.'),(19,270,'1998-01-22 16:54:57',3421,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/i.gif\" alt=\"I\" WIDTH=\"11\" HEIGHT=\"29\">\'d long been tempted by the ads in Hobby Japan and from Anaheim\'s\nMonsters in Motion for &quot;Fewture&quot; model kits from Japan.  The kits were\nstrange, yet oddly intriguing.  They were also very expensive.  Last winter,\nI finally decided to take the plunge and splurge on my first (of several as\nit\'s turned out) kit by Fewture, Nirasawa\'s Devilman.\n\n<p>\n  The bulk of Fewture\'s kits are sculpted by Takayuki Takeya, one of Japan\'s\nfinest figure sculptures, and Yasushi Nirasawa, creator of the disturbing\n&quot;Phantom Core&quot; manga.  Most of the kits in the Fewture line are taken from\nPhantom Core or are completely original pieces, although they now have an\nextensive line of Zeiram kits and props.  The 1:6 Devilman kit was one of the\nfirst in a line of characters created by other artists, in this case Go\nNagai, an influential anime and manga artist.\n\n<p>\n   Not content to simply sculpt a Devilman similar to those produced by\nVolks, Nirasawa has worked the character over in his own style.  Nirasawa\'s\nDevilman, is NOT the happy-go-lucky high school student (who just happens to\nbe possessed by a devil) that you might be familiar with from the anime.\n Here, Devilman is an emaciated, demonic, figure who\'s limbs have been warped\nand stretched.  It\'s clear that this humanoid figure is something far more\nthan human.  To give you some idea of how distended the anatomy is, consider\nthat this 1:6 kit is 2 and 1/2 feet tall with his arms raised over his head.\n (A typical 1:6 figure stands 12&quot; tall.)\n\n<p>\n   Two color reference photos are included, along with a large instruction\nsheet, some chain for detailing, and the kit itself.  The figure is cast in a\nclean white resin, while the base is cast in clear resin.  The base consists\nof a nameplate, some soil, a skull and vertebrae, and several crystal\noutcroppings, thus the base BEGS to be lit so that the crystals will glow.\n\n<p>\n   The castings themselves are quite clean, and devoid of any significant\npinholes.  Seam lines, though present, cleaned up easily.  There were no mold\noffsets.  Several of the fingers and one of the antennae did contain\nsubsurface voids which were not immediately obvious.  In fact, I was unaware\nthat they existed until one of the parts broken while I was sanding it.  They\nshowed up easily once I held the parts up to a bright light.  Popping them\nopen and filling them with putty made short work of this minor problem.\n\n<p>\n   The parts themselves fit together cleanly.  The head/torso fit neatly into\nthe waist cavity on the lower body piece.  Arm breaklines were amidst\nbandages, and thus required little/no puttying.  Some putty was needed at the\nfingers, antennae, and at the breaklines where Devilman\'s &quot;wings&quot; attach.\n\n<p>\n   The kit was fun to paint.  Devilman\'s leather pants were well textured,\nand took drybrushing well, as did his leathery wings.  The musculature on his\ntorso was well defined, and provides a great exercise for shading washes.  I\nalso used washes on his arms to provide a transition from humanoid skin to\nthe demonic claw-like hands at the ends of his arms.\n\n<p>\n   Detailing the kit was also enjoyable.  Devilman comes with a skull and\nsword to hang on his belt.  It was fun to drape the chain that came with the\nkit around his waist, as well as some additional chain I used.  My wife\nreally thought he needed some boot chains, but alas, I\'d already attached him\nto the base by the time she pointed it out.  (I could probably still add\nthem, but it would be a little tricky.)  I also drilled out the base, and\ninserted three LED\'s up in the crystals.  The glow effect is really cool in a\ndimly lit room.  It\'s not at all hard to imagine Devilman standing on the\nplains of Hell.\n\n<p>\n   I was very impressed with the quality and attention to detail in this kit.\n It was fun to build and paint, and its large size (this is a tall kit, yet\nit only takes up around 16 square inches of shelf space) makes a distinctive\naddition to any collection.  Like many Japanese kits, this one is expensive,\nand clocks in around $225.  Still, I felt that I really got my money\'s worth,\nand have since purchased and built several other Fewture kits.  Devilman is\navailable in the United States from Monsters in Motion.'),(20,270,'1998-01-24 13:47:27',3428,'sitting_hell','<img src=\"../garage/letter/i.gif\" alt=\"I\" WIDTH=\"11\" HEIGHT=\"29\">\nsuppose everyone has a \"grail kit,\" a kit they\'ve lusted after and\npursued for a long time.  For some, it might be one of the beautiful Volks\nSilene or Soryama kits.  Others might have a craving for an Argonaut Cain\nor Comet 1:3 Predator.  It\'s little secret that I\'m a Fewture kit junkie,\nso it shouldn\'t come as a surprise that my \"grail\" was Fewture\'s Sitting\nHell, sculpted by Takeyuki Takeya.\n<p>\nI first came across Sitting Hell, along with the rest of Fewture\'s\nunique line of kits 2 or 3 years ago.  I\'ve always been intrigued by things\nEgyptian, so it\'s probably natural that the image of a cowled pharoah\nseated on his throne immediately drew me in.  As I examined the grainy\nblack and white photo of the kit, I began to realize that the pharoah was\nin the process of injecting himself with a drug and transforming into some\nunspeakable creature.  Like most Nirasawa inspired kits, I found the\nbizarre sculpture repulsive, yet strangely intriguing.\n<p>\nAt the time, the 248,000 Yen price tag (~ $248) put me off immediately.\nI was still laboring under the misconception that all worthwhile kits\ncould/should be had for under $125.  Over the years, I gradually learned\nthat (for the most part) you get what you pay for, and Fewture\'s kits are\nusually worth every penny in terms of casting and sculpting quality.\nGradually I began to consider the creepy little pharoah more and more\nseriously.  Sadly, when I was finally prepared to \"make my move,\" the kit\nhad become nearly impossible to find in the US!  Fortunately, earlier in\nthe year I discovered Hobbylink Japan.  Scott Hards was able to find the\nkit in Japan without difficulty, and shipped it to me at the original\nretail price.\n<p>\nOut of the box, Sitting Hell consists of the figure, throne, animal\nskull, syringe tray, axe, sword, and canoptic jar.  The stone base depicted\nin the reference photo is NOT included.  I\'m unaware of any \"prefab\" bases\nthat are appropriate for this kit.  The best bet is probably to pour a slab\nof plaster for the throne to rest on.  Detail freaks will be interested to\nknow that Cellar Cast produces a \"Metamorphosis Addict\" nameplate that is\nentirely suitable for labeling this kit.  Fewture also sells a pair of\nkneeling figures called the \"Ambassador Servants\" which can be used to\nenhance the scene.\n<p>\nThe kit contains 20 white resin  and 2 brass parts that make up the\npharoah and sundry accessories that can be used to adorn a base.  The tall\nthrone is cast in 4 charcoal resin parts.  All of the white resin parts\nwere cleanly cast, and required only minor flash removal.  The charcoal\nresin was somewhat coarsely cast, and required a bit more cleanup effort.\nMinor putty work was required at the \"shoulder\" where the mutating arm and\ntentacle join the torso.  A bit of putty was also needed around the face\nwhere the \"beard\" and headdress snake attach.  Breaklines at the waist and\narm bracelet are completely natural and require no putty work.\n<p>\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/larry_brackney/sitting_face.jpg\" width=\"96\" height=\"144\" align=\"right\" hspace=\"8\" vspace=\"8\">\nSculpting quality is well above par for the hobby, but I must say I\ndon\'t think Sitting Hell is one of Takeya\'s best efforts.  That is NOT to\nsay that I find the sculpture deficient.  Takeya is perhaps the best\nsculptor in the hobby, and it stands to reason that his work will be held\nto very high standards.  The reptilian skin textures are really\ninteresting, and the pharoah\'s face does a wonderful job of depicting the\ncharacter\'s wicked glee at what he is doing.  Some of the flesh texturing\nis just a bit coarse, and contains \"cracks\" in various places around the\nbody.  I suspect these were intentionally sculpted to simulate rupturing\nflesh, but they just didn\'t look right to me.  Upon retrospect, I wish I\'d\nputtied these areas in.  I\'d also have liked to have seen some more\n\"radical regions\" of transformation.  Case in point, I think the figure\'s\nskin should have split down the back revealing a spinal column.  With the\nexception of the arm and left leg, you just don\'t get the sense of fragile\nhuman flesh barely containing the volcanic transformations occuring within.\n<p>\nI chose to paint the pharoah in two subassemblies.  The main body\nassembly included the torso and legs as well as mutant arm and tentacle\nalong w/ the headdress attachments.  I painted the human arm seperately so\nas to not hamper my ability to paint the right leg and chest area.  I have\nyet to attach the figure to the throne, and may never do so.\n<p>\nSince the pharoah is, more-or-less, still human, I opted for a\nflesh-tone color scheme for much of the figure - including the mutating\nsections.  I basecoated the entire figure w/ an orangey fleshtone (similar\nto Tamiya flesh).  I followed up by airbrushing lighter shades of flesh\nover the raised areas, ending up with what looked to be a fair caucasian\nskin tone.  I then washed the entire figure using a ruddy Horizon skin tone\n(#7 as I recall), working the darker shade into crevices and areas where I\nwanted shadows.  Once I achieved a \"well tanned look,\" I sealed my work w/\na matte lacquer and proceeded to the FUN part of the kit - the mutating\nflesh!\n<p>\nThe pharoah\'s reptilian/insect hide is a perfect opportunity for a\ndiscovery that Art Antiporda turned me on to, Polytranspar Taxidermy\npaints!  These paints were designed for painting fish and are OUTSTANDING\nfor painting garage kits w/ unusual skin coloration.  I began the\nPolytranspar treatment by airbrushing lavender flesh on the reptilian\nregions of the arm and left leg, applying the color more heavily in the\nhighly mutated areas.  I deepened the tones in recessed areas w/ violet.\nTranspar green was sprayed around the reptilian side of his face, as well\nas anywhere on the body where transformation was just beginning.  (Transpar\ngreen lightly applied over flesh creates a wonderfully subtle bruised flesh\neffect.)  I applied a striped pattern over the tentacle as well as some\nspot coloring on the arm w/ intense red and Transpar orange.  The finishing\ntouch was to mist the tentacle, arm, and transformed face sections w/\nshimmering blue/green and violet.\n<p>\nOf course that\'s a terribly abridged recount of how I painted the\npharoah.  There\'s certainly a lot of room for spot detailing on the kit\ntoo.  The throne was fairly straightforward, base coated in black then\ndrybrushed in greys and browns.  All metallics were base coated in black,\nthen drybrushed w/ silver or gold.\n<p>\nIn summary, I have to say that the search for Sitting Hell was slightly\nmore satisfying than realizing my goal.  It IS a great kit, perhaps a bit\noverpriced, but terrific none-the-less.  I would have felt a little better\nabout the kit\'s cost, had it come w/ at least a simple stonework base.\nIt\'s worth pointing out that Sitting Hell is not for everyone.  Most people\nto whom I\'ve shown my finished kit thought it was really cool, until they\nrealized what the pharoah was doing.  The image of drug (mis)use is\ncertainly taboo, and something many people are uncomfortable dealing with.\nFor Nirasawa and Takeya, the notion of drug-induced transendence is\nsomething they felt compelled to tackle headlong, at least symbolically.\nWhile I don\'t subscribe to that tenet of counterculture, I can certainly\nappreciate the sculpture for what it is, and had a great time working on\nit.'),(21,270,'1998-01-22 17:03:53',3425,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/s.gif\" alt=\"S\" WIDTH=\"21\" HEIGHT=\"33\">ome\ncharacters have proven popular enough to inspire multiple figure\nkits.  Many of Ray Harryhausen\'s critters have made the transition to vinyl\nor resin on multiple occasions, as have characters such as Giger\'s Alien,\nthe Predator, Frankenstein, and many others.  The japanese market is\ncertainly not immune to this phenomenon either - case in point the almost\nridiculous redundancy in Kaiju (giant monster) and anime kits that are\navailable overseas.  What\'s perhaps more interesting is the fact that many\ncollectors are quite willing to acquire as many variations of a character\nas possible.\n\n<p>\nI\'m certainly not immune to this \"affliction,\" as evidenced by a growing\nnumber of Nina Dolono kits in my collection.  Nina is the \"heroine\" in\nYasushi Nirasawa\'s Manga \"Phantom Core,\" a leather clad goth-chick, ready\nto dish out carnage on a grand scale.  One of Nina\'s more interesting\ntraits is her wardrobe of interchangeable arms that allow her to deal with\ndifferent situations.  Typically, there is a special outfit that\ncomplements each arm, providing Nirasawa with a nearly endless supply of\nfresh costume design opportunities.  No fewer than three garage kits have\nsought to capitalize on this variety. Fewture\'s Gillman and Spider suit\nNinas as well as Begul\'s Black Muscle suit version are all available as\nresin kits.  Fewture has also released a tiny (1:30?) resin kit of Nina and\nSteeve Oil.\n\n<h2>Nina Gillman Skin</h2>\nWhile the character design is still\nNirasawa\'s, this time, the sculpting chores are handled by Yuji Oniki.\nWhile I am unfamiliar with Oniki\'s work, I\'ll certainly be keeping an eye\nout for other offerings from this talented sculptor!\n\n<p>\nThis Nina features her in a skintight outfit stylized from the monster\nin &quot;The Creature from the Black Lagoon.&quot;  Nina stands casually on an\nelaborate ocean floor base that features the carcass of a weird prehistoric\nfish.  It\'s hard to believe that a sexy female and the scaly Gillman could\nbe melded so seamlessly, but it WORKS!  The Gillman Skin kit is cleanly\ncast from ivory and clear resin in 14 parts, some of which are optional.  A\nswatch of dyed rabbit fur is included for detailing Nina\'s hair.\n\n<p>\nCompared to the crude sculpting of the Spider Suit, the sculpting in\nthis version of Nina is simply stunning.  The scales and webbing in the\nlegs, bikini, and arm coverings are all incredibly detailed.  Likewise, the\nface (although hairless) is a thing of beauty.  The sculpting in the base\nis equally noteworthy.  The fish creature on the base is REALLY\ninteresting, and should be great fun to paint.  Oniki has also included\nreal sea shells in the base.\n\n<p>\nThe kit may be built with your choice of clear or ivory resin back fins\nand left hand.  The idea being that you can achieve a neat transluscent\neffect with the webbing between the bones in those pieces.  A third set of\nback fins features an entirely skeletal look without webbing.  An\nintricately detailed knife is meant to be mounted on her hip, but could\njust as easily be omitted.\n\n<p>\nAfter a bit of dremel work to remove pour spouts from the legs, arms,\nbody, and head, the parts fit together nearly perfectly.  All of the parts\nare clearly keyed, and will require minimal puttying.  The fingers, toes,\nback spines, etc. are delicately sculpted, yet all pulled cleanly from the\nmolds - no easy feat IMO.  Most of the parts appear seamless, or have very\nlight seam lines.  However, like the Spider Suit, the Gillman Suit body has\na substantial mold line across the thighs which will require careful\ncleanup.\n\n<p>\nThe sky\'s the limit when it comes to painting this piece.  Any number of\ncolor schemes for the Gillman suit would look terrific.  There\'s plenty of\ndefinition in the scales to make for great drybrushing, and the webs are\nwell recessed for washing as well.  There\'s plenty of exposed flesh for\nairbrush fans to go crazy with too.'),(22,270,'1998-01-22 17:04:14',4315,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/s.gif\" alt=\"S\" WIDTH=\"21\" HEIGHT=\"33\">ome\ncharacters have proven popular enough to inspire multiple figure\nkits.  Many of Ray Harryhausen\'s critters have made the transition to vinyl\nor resin on multiple occasions, as have characters such as Giger\'s Alien,\nthe Predator, Frankenstein, and many others.  The japanese market is\ncertainly not immune to this phenomenon either - case in point the almost\nridiculous redundancy in Kaiju (giant monster) and anime kits that are\navailable overseas.  What\'s perhaps more interesting is the fact that many\ncollectors are quite willing to acquire as many variations of a character\nas possible.\n\n<p>\nI\'m certainly not immune to this &quot;affliction,&quot; as evidenced by a growing\nnumber of Nina Dolono kits in my collection.  Nina is the &quot;heroine&quot; in\nYasushi Nirasawa\'s Manga &quot;Phantom Core,&quot; a leather clad goth-chick, ready\nto dish out carnage on a grand scale.  One of Nina\'s more interesting\ntraits is her wardrobe of interchangeable arms that allow her to deal with\ndifferent situations.  Typically, there is a special outfit that\ncomplements each arm, providing Nirasawa with a nearly endless supply of\nfresh costume design opportunities.  No fewer than three garage kits have\nsought to capitalize on this variety. Fewture\'s Gillman and Spider suit\nNinas as well as Begul\'s Black Muscle suit version are all available as\nresin kits.  Fewture has also released a tiny (1:30?) resin kit of Nina and\nSteeve Oil.\n\n<h2>Spider Suit Nina</h2>\nThis 1:6 white resin kit is\nsculpted by Nirasawa himself, and comes in 5 pieces.  The kit features Nina\nin a bikini that looks to be made of multiple insect-like plates.  A\nstylized spider forms her &quot;bikini strap&quot; and a second spider graces the\nbase as well.  Her modular left arm features long boney fingers and lays\nflat against her body, allowing the body and arm to be cast in one piece.\nHer right arm, cast separately, is adorned with a simple bracer and\nclutches a chain-laden sword.\n\n<p>\nCutting right to the chase, I\'ll say that I was disappointed with this\nkit for several reasons.  The castings were far &quot;flashier&quot; than I am\naccustomed to from Fewture.  A sizeable seam runs all the way around the\nmain body piece, across smooth armor plating and bare skin alike.  Care\nmust be taken in removing flashing like this to avoid leaving flat spots,\nespecially on the skin.  More seriously, though, is the apparent haste with\nwhich the kit was sculpted.\n\n<p>\nNina\'s body armor is minimally detailed, consisting of multiple smooth\nplates butted together with some dimples pressed into them.  Nirasawa\'s\ndone body armor like this before, and done it far better with his &quot;Raped!\nWoman Bee kit.&quot;  Nirasawa cut another corner by simply canibalizing one of\nhis Devilman\'s fingers for use as Nina\'s sword.  The decision to sculpt the\narm firmly against the body and legs together may have made for simple\ncasting, but it also makes for a boring pose.  On the positive side, the\nhead and right arm are the only parts to be attached, and should require no\nputty work whatsoever.\n\n<p>\nThe killing blow for this kit, IMO, is the poorly detailed face.  I\'ve\nseen $9 children\'s dolls with more subtlety!  In addition, her left eye is\nimproperly placed giving her a freakish, wall-eyed, appearance.  The eye\nsocket will have to be dremeled out and resculpted entirely to correct this\nhorrible defect.\n\n<p>\nThis version of Nina features her with a &quot;short haircut,&quot; which hangs\nabout her face with a modicum of detail.  While Nina does appear briefly in\nPhantom Core with a short haircut, Nina fans may wish to dremel this off\nentirely, replacing it with a swatch of fur to give her a more familiar\nappearance.   The base is simply adequate, and consists of a plain round\ndisc with some dirt, a crude spider, and a logo.'),(23,170,'1998-01-22 17:15:26',3769,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/i.gif\" alt=\"I\" WIDTH=\"11\" HEIGHT=\"29\">\'m fortunate to live close enough to\na really good hobby shop, Kit Kraft.\nIt is located in Studio City, Ca., a suburb of Los Angeles right next to\nUniversal Studios. They carry all kinds of hobby supplies but have a\nspecial area in the front of the store dedicated to &quot;garage kits&quot;.  Kit\nKraft also happens to be where you can find Tom Gilliland (Explorations In\nPainting video,  featured artist, he also handles the painting chores for\nthe prototype kits featured on the boxes of the Horizon kits).  Kit Kraft\ntends to get its share of true garage kits.  I think part of the reason is\nits proximity to the movie studios.  I believe some of these FX guys create\nthese garage kits in their spare time and sell them through Kit Kraft.  I\ncan vouch for this on the kit I purchased titled &quot;A boy and his Werewolf&quot;.\nIt was created by a member of Rick Baker\'s FX studio. Enough about Kit\nKraft.\n\n<p>\n        During my last visit to Kit Kraft I came across a kit that caught my eye.\nIt was a massive Alien 3 kit. This kit stands 25 inches tall!  I asked Tom\nwho made it. He said jokingly, &quot;What, the &quot;Doorstop&quot; Alien?&quot;.  Since that\ncomment, I\'ve referred to it as such. Tom said just some local guy made it.\nI took a closer look at it.  I first noticed how well it was finished.\nPainted in the tans and brown/reds of the Alien 3. The figure itself had a\ncertain &quot;raw&quot; sculpted look to it. Its no where as finished looking as the\nDark Horse Alien 3 kit. But this kit does have this &quot;garage kit&quot; appeal to\nit. I visualized the guy sculpting it, creating the molds, then pouring it.\nI also figured the other Alien kits were/are more accessible. I had never\nbuilt an Alien kit before and thought this would be an excellent first\nAlien kit. Price is right, size is good for getting good airbrush effects\nand detailed enough to experiment with washes.\n\n<p>\n        The kit comes in a box with a carrying handle which is nice due to its\nsize and weight. Weight is probably on the order of 6 pounds. A large color\nphotograph of the finished kit is pasted on the front of the box. The kit\nconsists of 9 pieces. The head, torso, left and right arms, right and left\nlegs below the knee, two part tail, and the base.  The only detail I don\'t\nparticularly care for is the tail.  It comes up straight behind him. I\nwould rather have seen a more curving/arching tail.\n\n<p>\n        The overall sculpting detail for a kit of this size is good.  There are\nsome areas where details are a bit crude, where it looks like he started to\nadd some detail then either decided against it or figured it was enough.\nAlso, certain areas lack symmetry, giving it a look as if it was just\nsculpted from clay instead of coming from a mold.  Gives it a very\n&quot;one-off&quot;/prototype look. Not bad just different. The head is well sculpted\nwith good shape and detail although probably a little short.  The mouth\narea could have used just a bit more fine detail as the teeth are not as\nsharp or defined as I would have liked, no problem though, just a bit of\nmilliput.\n\n<p>\n        My kits overall casting was very good. Only one mold offset line, some seam\nlines, and a few areas with pin holes.  There are a couple of areas where\nlarge hole/air bubbles may have been when the kit was taken out of the\nmolds and then patched over.  About average casting for a true garage kit.\nOne of the nice touches were the molded-in pins.  These were cast as part\nof the resin on the arms and head.  The feet of the Alien are cast into a\nlittle piece that fits into the base making the overall figure more sturdy\nonce mounted.\n\n<p>\n        Assembly is going well, (This kit is under construction as I write this).\nI must admit that I have made some modifications at this point.  I have\ncreated/added the inner mouth extended and repositioned the right hand and\narm slightly.  All the parts seem to being going together nicely.  The only\narea that requires a bit of grinding is where the lower legs join the\ntorso.  Nothing major, just a grind here and there with the dremel and a\nbit of putty. Due too its large size I am not going to completely assemble\nit first. It will be much easier to paint it in portions then assemble.\n\n<p>\n        Admittedly, this review is a bit on the premature side since I have not\nyet completed the kit. But I feel the major areas of concern when buying\nand building a kit have been addressed.  Since this is a true garage kit\nwhose availability can be very limited, I thought I should share this NOW\nfor those Alien kit collectors who may be interested in buying this kit.        \n\n<p>\n        If you are looking for the definitive  Alien kit, go with the Takeya\nAlien, Dark Horse Alien 3, or any of the Halcyon kits. But I believe this\nkit is worth owning. The size and &quot;raw&quot; sculpting look appeals to me. Yes,\nits a bit on the crude side, certain aspects could have been made more\nsymmetrical and finished, but this is what makes this kit unique.'),(24,4933,'1998-01-24 13:48:18',4037,'am_cop','<b><img src=\"../garage/letter/t.gif\" alt=\"t\" WIDTH=\"21\" HEIGHT=\"29\"></b>he\n  kit I would like to bring to your attention is: Martin Herricks, AM Cop\nthis kit is produced by \"Forbidden Planet\" New Oxford Street, London, England.\nIt retails for #29.99 which I think is about $40.00!!!\n<p>\nIt has to be one of the finest cast pieces that I\'ve ever had the chance to\nown. It has clean fine lines, nice 1/6th scale and a resin that hasn\'t heard\nof the air bubble syndrome, let alone, considered even needing preperation work.\nApart from the mild wash in warm soapy water, then primer this kit is fantastic.\nThe base isnt very imaginative, but hey isnt that where we come in, us pesky\ngarage kit builders?\n<p>\nThis piece is screaming for attention, its dying to be placed in a diorama.\nIt could possibly kick the granny out of Judge Dredd.  As some of the  more\nobservant of you may have noticed that this is the comic book version of\nMean Machine Angel.  Possibly through some sort of copyright law though the kit\ngot its name AM Cop....anyway, i love it.  Here in England usually good things\ncost a great deal more, our only source of value for money is Drone magazine.\nThis is where people like us can relate to our kit building needs on a friendly\nfamily like way.  Incidently if you would like to join Drone, either see the latest\nModellers resource or e-mail me and I\'ll send it to you!!'),(25,224,'1998-01-22 17:20:55',3434,'stasis','<img src=\"../garage/letter/a.gif\" alt=\"A\" WIDTH=\"23\" HEIGHT=\"31\">s Ripley slept, dreaming that the whole days events were just a bad\nnightmare, Trying to convince herself that there were no real monsters,\nshe was aroused by a sound. Was that the door? Who was here? As she\nturned she saw Newt sleeping at her side, she realized, it wasn\'t a dream,\nmonsters are real. Another noise.. Oh god the stasis chambers are open.\nKeep cool, there\'s a pulse rifle right above my head. Quietly.. Where\nis it!.. <b>WHERE IS IT!!!</b> Burke! You slime-ball bastard! You set them loose\nto impregnate us. Burke! God why couldn\'t it all be a dream?....\n<p>\nThe kit is well done and of a reasonable size. I imagined the 1:1\nscale hugger and figured this kit would be the size of a 5 gallon\npail. It\'s actually about the size of 3 coffee cups stacked up.\n<p>\nThe Chamber: Assembly is quite simple and consists of two, 2 part\nend caps connected by 3 rods. The rods are held in place with 6\nsmall resin screws. These don\'t actually &quot;tighten&quot;, rather they\nare glued in place and the threads extend into the ends of the\ntubes to hold them in place. The top-most part of the chamber has\n3 openings which have plugs cast in place that require careful\ntrimming to open up. The prototype had a slight flaw in the alignment\nof one of the tabs that support a tube, which needs modification\nto get the tube straight. I added black construction paper disks to\nthe inside top and bottom end caps and painted the inside part that\nshows thru the tube black. I painted the outside parts light gloss\ngrey and the screw heads chrome silver.\n<p>\nThe Specimen: The sculpting is amazing and the casting is clean.\nThe detail on the bottom looks quite obscene like the original movie\nprop which was patterned after the female genetalia.\nAgain assembly is simple. It took a while to figure out the correct\nplacment of the tail on the body. The joint requires a bit of putty.\n<p>\nThe legs seem to have been sculpted for a standing face-hugger and\ntherefore are too long if used as is. The prototype kit had pencil\nmarks describing where to cut the legs to correct the problem and\nget the assembly inside the tube. I opted to cut the legs at each\nknuckle and pose them to create the image of an escape struggle.\nAfter cutting each knuckle I drilled small holes in the ends of\neach part and dowel pinned the joints with soft wire super glued\nin place. I dowel pinned each leg to the body also.\n<p>\nAfter inserting the hugger in the tube I used long tweezers to pose the\nlegs where I wanted them. then I carefully removed the assembly and\nspot glued the joints. I used milliput to fill in the joints so I could sculpt\nthe knuckle detail back in place. Any good quality non-shrinking\nputty would do the job.\n<p>\nOverall this kit was easy and fun to assemble. The parts are well\ncast with very few if any bubbles. The legs come cast on one peice\nof flash and require the most cleanup. The fact that the legs are\nlonger then they should be allows you to build the face hugger as\nit\'s own kit for display without the chamber. All you need to do\nis figure out some way to make the tail straight.\n<p>\nI didn\'t glue the top on my kit so beware when you sleep. Burke\nmay be around..'),(26,270,'1998-01-22 17:37:31',3455,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/t.gif\" alt=\"T\" WIDTH=\"21\" HEIGHT=\"29\">his\nis an out of the box review of GEOmetric\'s Pumpkinhead: The Metamorphosis.\nA year or two ago, GEOmetric stopped production\nof their immensely popular Pumpkinhead resin kit.  The kit featured the\nPumpkin-headed demon of retribution in a 1:6 scale Aurora-styled pose.  When\nthe original writers of the P\'Head movie plotted a sequel which featured\nan amazing Metamorphosis of the creature at the end of the film, they\napproached GEOmetric w/ the idea for possible production as a kit.  A\nyear or two later, the film\'s still not done, but the kit is.  And what\na kit...\n\n<p>\nThe new P\'Head is a 1:6 scale vinyl model designed and produced by\nJapanese modeling house, Max Factory, for GEOmetric.  The figure is sculpted\nby Takayuki Takeya who was also responsible for Max Factory\'s Future\nNinja and Devilman kits.  The metamorphosed P\'head features the same basic\nbody structure of the original figure, but has sprouted a huge pair of\nleathery wings, and stands astride a spiral staircase section.  The pose\nis quite dramatic, and the figure stands an impressive 18&quot;+ tall.  The figure\nis well detailed.  Surface textures are quite believable, and the facial\nexpression seems cold and calculating.  Another nice touch, is the little\nboy\'s necklace laying on the staircase.\n\n<p>\nOpening the box may be a bit of a shock for those familiar w/ vinyl\nkits.  P\'Head is CLEARLY the most complex vinyl kit this modeler has ever\nseen, and is IMHO NOT suitable for beginning figure modelers.  The kit\ncontains on the order of 40 parts which must be cut and fit together.\nThis would be of great concern if it weren\'t for the obvious care with\nwhich Max Factory sculpted and separated the kit.  Test fitting some of\nthe parts together indicates that break lines were very well planned, and\nsome may require no puttying whatsoever.  The parts are also cleanly\nmolded w/ a minimum of flash or warpage.  The vinyl itself has been\npoured thinly, so some type of stuffing WILL be needed to reinforce the\nfigure.\n\n<p>\nPinning will most likely be required where the wings join the body.  I\nplan to fill the upper wing sections w/ an epoxy for drilling.  The lower\nlegs and base may also require weighting w/ plaster to keep this tall model\nfrom tipping.  Painting chores on this kit are fairly straightforward.  (As\nopposed to say, Horizon\'s Creature From the Black Lagoon.)  Primarily\nbrown/tan base coats w/ light and dark brown washes and dry brushes should\nproduce acceptable results.\n\n<p>\nThe packaging and instructions are quite nice as well.  A crisp color\nphoto of the finished kit serves as the box art.  The instruction booklet\nis printed on glossy heavy-weight paper and serves two purposes.  First,\nit contains synopses of the canceled Dark Horse Pumpkinhead comic series,\nand completes the story as it was intended.  The prose is accompanied by\nsome beautifully painted artwork.  Assembly instructions are brief but\nadequate, with appropriate diagrams.  Painting scheme suggestions are also\nincluded.\n\n<p>\nAt a hair under $100, this is the most expensive domestic vinyl kit I\'ve\ncome across.  Is it worth it?  For figure kit builders who appreciate\nStan Winston\'s Pumpkinhead character, I\'d say yes.  As I\'ve mentioned,\nthe character is captured in a dynamic pose, and is quite imposing.  The\naddition of wings to the character makes for a striking figure which\nwill definitely attract the attention of those viewing a collection\ncontaining him.  Having seen photos of the original kit, and then a\ncompleted kit at this year\'s Wonderfest, I felt compelled to purchase it.\nI\'m not at all disappointed with the package, and have since picked up\nanother Max Factory kit based on the quality of Pumpkinhead.'),(27,330,'1998-01-22 17:40:25',4286,'bane','<p>\nGreetings fellow modelers! Some of you called for me to hop into the web with my kit\nreviews so here I am. I hope the years of experience that I\'ve devoted to our paragon of a\nhobby (lifestyle?!) will serve you well.\n</p>\n<p>\nUp for review this time we have Horizon\'s 1/8 scale Bane.\nNo question about it, Horizon\'s recent choice to produce Batman characters according to\nup to date comic book renditions is a stroke of genius. No one has actually ever done this\nbefore to this extent.\n</p>\n<p>A relative newcomer, Bane is Batman\'s singular most lethal rival in that he\'s the guy who\nbroke ol\' pointy ears\' back nearly killing him! </p>\n<p>The figure of Bane comes in nine pieces. A street level base, cracked roadway with manhole\ncover and a nameplate complete the piece. </p>\n<p>Pieces are all injection molded quality solid resin; to wit, not a single air hole is to be found\nherein! Outside of the two-piece right boot; careful sanding will assure no seams to fill.\nCareful cutting on Horizon\'s part assured all seam lines fall in joints where one is to drill for\npinning... a Horizon original idea saving the builder hours of measuring time. Actually,\nHorizon\'s solid resin kits have lock in joints and I find them strong enough to the point of not\nhaving to peg them at all. </p>\n<p>Sculpturally, the piece\'s nearly perfect. But when Bane pumps up on his steroid-like drug, he\nalso goes into a hormone turbo charged state of affairs. He gets super strong with muscles to\nrival The Hulk and grows body hair to nearly outdo Wolverine (of that &quot;other&quot; comic book\ncompany,) D.C. artists never represent this latter with more than an off the cuff scribble of\nink. But when one is dealing with 3-D art, a sculptor must address characteristics in full\ndetail. </p>\n<p>The patches of chest and shoulder hair sculpted on Bane are a sheer let down. They look\nlike a contrived after thought of sculpey sponged on. But the kit can be made to work just\nfine. Just dry-brush the hair in your choice of brown. Then water down the same color and\nwith a fine 5/0 brush, fan out tiny singular hairs around the edges. It won\'t cover Bane\nproperly with body hair, but will give the existing hair a more natural look. In a sense, I suppose all is for the best here as the arm\nhair, for example, would tend to obscure the sheer power of the muscles in this piece. A\npurest can do as they wish over the hairy hormone problem. </p>\n<p>Horizone has done so many things right of late, I almost hate to mention the following. Still,\nhonesty in reviews has always been my hallmark so... A few months ago the artist who did\nall of Horizon\'s box cover art and written painting instructions, Tom Gilliland, had to depart\nfrom Horizon\'s employ. To date, Horizon has not replaced him. I never truly realized how\nvaluable his work was until we lost it! For example, what color are all the mechanical\ngizmo\'s on Bane\'s mask? This is a tiny bit of a hole concerning all Horizon kits lately.\nResearch, of course, is called for in all the new Horizon\'s. Does anyone besides me miss\nTom\'s painting instructions? For me, a quick fix in instructions is a lot better than many hours\nand dollars worth of research. You can\'t pay enough to anyone with Tom\'s talents and I\nsincerely hope Horizon will hire another artist to replace him soon. </p>\n'),(28,330,'1998-01-22 17:43:25',3494,'batman','<p>\nKiya San\'s sculpting is not just smokin\' it\'s BLAZING like the fires of hell!\nHe has captured the spirit of the newest D.C. comics rendering of Batman and\ncrew to a veritable miracle.  No matter what version one likes best over all\nthe years, this kit is sculpturally the best on the market.  This coming from\na fan of ole\' pointy ears since the word go!\n<p>\nFirst of all, since Bane broke Batman\'s back, D.C. comics has presented B.M.\nin a slightly newer looking costume while not forsaking the original state\nof affairs.  Mainly, Batman no longer wears his briefs on the outside.  The\nresulting effect is that the entire outfit is slicker and gives comic and\nsculpting artists a more artistic stab at rendering smoother lines to anatomy,\nand Taishiro is, without question, one of the very best sculptors of anatomy.\n<p>\nThe singular point that sets this kit in a class unto itself is not actually\nthe flawless anatomy, but rather the cape.  This cape is a pure orchestration\nof material movement and flow!  I\'ve seen great draping sculpted into vinyl\nand resin pieces, but what gives this piece the stellar edge is that the cape\nwas cut into five pieces for the casting process thus assuring as much detail\non the inside of the fabric as on the outside.\n<p>\nThere\'s the problem in this kit; to make a beautiful work of art you must\nallow yourself added time.  While there\'s no case here of a mystic voice\nfrom above booming at you that you need special modeling skills, there is\nstill hard work involved.\n<p>\nForget air holes in resin.  Horizon has eliminated those nasty little things\nfrom their kits!\n<p>\nConcentrate on seam lines!  Being that you must glue pieces to the cape and\nleft leg into the groin area where you especially don\'t want seams to show,\nthe majority of your work will be on these spots.\n<p>\nOnce I constructed the head/cape assembly and body assembly (left apart for\neasier painting,) I sanded all puttied in areas at the same time I did the\nmold lines.  Leaving them all for the same step seemed like a good idea.\n<p>\nAn interesting sideline here for all new modelers.  I always rough in my\nsanding work with coarse sandpaper.  I then use 380 grit wet/dry sandpaper\n(wet) to refine the pre-painting work.  On a puttied in seam line, NEVER\ntrust your eyes!  Instead, run a toothpick tip or finger nail across the\nseam line, if you detect  a slight &quot;click&quot; feeling as you cross the seam,\nthen your area needs more work.  Even if you run your fingertips over the\nseam line it may register as clean as your eyes detect it to be.  For the\nhighly polished look (and the paints will eventually detect the spots\nmissed) try this technique.\n<p>\nBack to Batman.  As stated, leaving the head/cape assembly off for painting\naffords a monumentally superior paint job, do not overlook the fit before\nyou start to paint.  Chances are you\'ll have to Dremel, file or hot water\nwarp the right hand area to fit the cape.  All the extra care taken at this\nstage pays off later.  I spent about a half hour extra to assure a good fit\nof head and cape assembly over body assembly and didn\'t have a single spot\nto re-address with putty or paint!\n<p>\nI\'m still not convinced that a 1/8 scale kit need cost $90.00 when produced\nin the U.S..  This especially as vinyl kit molds are a galaxy above resins\nrubber molds.  Then again, perhaps all the extra expense is put into\nsculptor\'s fees.  I only know that forewarned of the size of this piece,\nthe price should seem a matter of relativity considering the quality!\n<p>\nHorizon\'s best to date!!!'),(29,4957,'1998-01-22 17:44:59',3494,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/w.gif\" alt=\"W\" WIDTH=\"37\" HEIGHT=\"30\">hen\nI saw that Horizon was producing a series of comic book characters\nin resin, I was very enthused. The initial pictures of the kits did\nnothing to lessen the enthusiasm. Robin throwing a Batarang, the\nCatwoman with her whip, Joker and Bane, and Batman standing on a\nwindow ledge all looked to be winners. The initial release of\n<a href=\"../mark_warthling/robin_review.html\">Robin</a>\n(reviewed elsewhere) promised great things for this line of kits.\nUnfortunately as with most things, reality provided both high and\nlow points.\n\n<p>\nThe Batman depicted does not resemble either of the cinematic versions,\nnor is he the TV version. Instead we have the character as depicted in\nthe comics. On first impression, the kit is nicely sculpted with crisp\ndetail. Mr Kiya has done a nice job representing the cape. It is\nintricate with many deep folds and creases. The figure stands with\nleft foot forward and head turned to the left. The cape is draped\naround the body,billowing out off the back  and is held in the right\nhand. While nicely sculpted, when compared to the dynamic pose of\nHorizon\'s  Robin and Catwoman, the pose is rather static. Because\nthe face does not resemble any of the actors to play the caped crusader,\nit appears to be somewhat generic. Unfortunately, the window ledge with\ngargoyle base the kit is often pictured with (see Amazing Figure Modeler\n5) is a seperate item costing as much as the figure. I was very\ndisappointed to find that the base was considered a seperate item.    \n\n<p>\nHorizon\'s Batman comes well packaged in a sturdy box with foam inserts.\nMost major components are individually wrapped to protect them. The kit\nconsists of  13 parts: torso and leg, leg, 2 forearms, 2 sets of &quot;barbs&quot;\nfor the forearms, six pieces which comprise the head and cape, and a one\npiece street base.   The kit is molded in a brittle grey-green resin,\nvery similar to the material Verlinden productions uses for their military\nfigures. The moldings had only a very fine mold line and a few pinholes.\nOf secondary interest is a fine layer of release agent coating all the parts. \n\n<p>\nAssembly started with a brisk scrub using Murphy\'s Oil Soap and a firm\nbrush in hot water to get rid of the release agent. The mold lines\nwere lightly sanded and the pin holes filled with Cyanoacrylate cement.\nMajor components were test fit . I assembled the torso and leg first.\nBecause the position of the forearm/hand is dependant on the position\nof the cape, I decided to assemble the cape next. This is where my\nproblems started. As noted before the cape and head comprise six seperate\nparts. Seam lines are relatively well hidden in folds of the cape but\nwill need some camouflage to hide completely. I found it difficult to\nadequately eliminate the seams, but they aren\'t really visible unless\nyou go looking for them. Because of the complexity of the cape and the\nway the pieces join together, it is virtually impossible to dry fit the\npieces to make sure all the components fit in harmony. I found after\nassembling the pieces the way mine lined up that the cape seems to ride\nup the figures back leg. This creates an unnatural appearance and prevents\nthe cape and head from seating correctly into the torso. Secondarily, I\nhad to force the right hand which is gripping the cape into place. Between\nthis and having to force the cape/head assembly down into the torso, there\nis a lot of internal stress in this part of the figure. I wouldn\'t be\nsurprised if the stress causes the thing to fly apart one of these days.\nI\'m not sure if this fit problem is due to some warpage in the fairly thin\npieces that comprise the cape, or just an error during construction.  In\nany event, with exception of the cape problems, the kit goes together well\nwith minimal need for seam work. I opted to keep the head/cape seperate\nfrom the torso until after painting to make access a little easier. A light\ncoat of Floquil laquer primer was sprayed to give a good base for the paint\nto grab onto.\n\n<p>\nI use lots of different paints but for this kit I stuck with the new\nPolly S paints. I find they airbrush well and come in a wide variety\nof shades. I started by painting the torso a light blue grey, similar\nto a WWII US Navy aircraft medium blue. I shaded with a darker grey\nto pick out the musculature and then highlighted with a lighter shade\nof the first color. I sealed this with Floquil figure flat. I then\nmasked the area around the utility belt with liquid latex. I painted\nthe belt white to mask out the grey primer and then used a Japanese\nyellow paint for the belt. Unfortunately, when removing the mask, it\nbecame apparent that for all my vigorous scrubbing with the oil soap,\nthe release agent was not gone. My paint pulled up in some sections\nleaving me to curse loudly and question the lineage of the nice folks\nat Horizon. I stripped all the paint and primer and rewashed the kit.\nI reprimed and tried it again. Same problem. Whatever Horizon uses as\na mold release agent, it is very tenacious. I never adequately solved\nthis problem and was forced to do a lot of touching up on the kit. The\ncape was painted USAAC blue and shadowed with Luftwaffe dark blue.\nFlesh tones were, well, flesh tones. I used the Horizon paints for this.\n\n<p>\nIn summary, there are some high points and low points. First the high\npoints. The sculpting is very good as is the quality of the castings.\nThe character is accurately modeled. Assembly (except for the cape)\nis easy. Now the low points. First, the pose is too static. There are\na lot of dynamic poses this character could have been placed in. Take\na look at Horizon\'s Robin or Flash and you\'ll see what I mean. Secondly,\nthe cape while beautifully sculpted is a real pain in the neck to\nassemble. Third, for the price of this kit,I would really like to have\nseen the base pictured in AFM included. There are many other kits out\nthere that give more bang for the buck. Last, whatever it is that\nHorizon is using as a mold release agent has got to go. I follow \nthe same cleansing steps for all my resin kits and have never had a\nproblem. Why this stuff wouldn\'t come off is beyond me. In closing,\nI\'ll tell you that I\'m relatively happy with the way the kit came out.\nIf I don\'t look too closely to see all the touch ups, I\'m not displeased.\nI just think that perhaps the end results were not necessarily worth\nthe headaches.'),(30,330,'1998-01-22 20:32:10',4318,'batplane','<p>Because I so seldom have a single negative comment to relate on a Horizon model, let\'s start with the truly\namazing aspect of this kit... the instructions sheet to a vehicle model should be much better. Herein we only\nhave blow apart drawings with no numbered parts and no step by step write up. For an advanced model\nbuilder used to cryptic or no instruction sheets, this model will be o.k. But I\'m thinking about the novice\nbuilder here. Assembly and paintings write up, amounts to three short paragraphs of a &quot;one size fits all&quot;\ntype nature. &quot;Refer to box art&quot; for paint up directions does not reflect Horizon\'s glory years with a\ncompetent instructor addressing the buyer. </p>\n<p>One tip I\'m compelled to pass on to the novice. Never, but never use P.V.C. (super or Krazy) glues you\nuse to construct the resin and metal parts of this kit on clear plastic parts like the canopy. To do so will\nresult in a &quot;bleeding&quot; foggy area from 1 m.m. to 6 m.m. from every spot you touch the glue to. Instead, use\na toothpick to spread a <b>very</b> fine line of rubber cement to the edge of clear parts. This will glue pieces in\nplace, and while not actually dissolving them into place, will hold them without doing everlasting damage to\nthem. </p>\n<p>There were only two production problems in this piece; both in the main fuselage (largest) resin chunk in\nthe kit) piece. There was an air bubble in the fin art joint area and one male joint pins was broken off and\nmissing from kit. Neither point matters much to resin-o-holics like most of us, but considering Horizon\'s\nspotless reputation in the last year and a half, this is truly huge news! </p>\n<p>Now, while I\'m not a very big fan at all of vehicle models, I\'m more than pleased to work with standard or\nelectronic publications which are desiring to bring modeling factions together as a whole. Long time\nbuilders will still realize the rift between us all. So, toward this cause... </p>\n<p>Resin or vinyl fans must not overlook the fact that, while glued and primed and finally painted as we do\nwith plastics, metal pieces must have seam lines and sprues removed as do resin parts. The same\ntechniques used in resin, dremel/sawing, sanding, final wet/dry sanding and replacement of detail are used.\nThis is due to the metal parts being cast in &quot;soft metal&quot;. </p>\n<p>The major joint of the three fuselage parts will be the main resin work. As the seams must be puttied up\nand sanded, you\'ll want to use extra time and extra care as one would in an awkwardly placed joint on a\nnude figural study. A couple of extra days spent on careful hand sanding with medium grit sandpaper and\n380 grit wet sandpaper are well worth the time... every little scratch will show up on your finished kit, so\n&quot;haste makes waste&quot; here, and also creates unwanted headaches. </p>\n<p>So while one should plan to finish this kit in standards of weeks rather than days, it should come as no\nsurprise. Patience is a virtue many of us figural modelers do not have much of compared to vehicular\nmodelers. They seem to thrive on it when constructing a kit!! We can learn lessons from them in this\nrespect! </p>\n<p>The practical upshot of this review is that this is a beautiful piece if one has the time and willpower to spend\non it. There can be no mistake about this in any way! I personally feel that even the first time kit builder, if\nthey apply themselves, can accomplish this one if they are truly in love with the subject matter. But it is a\njudgment call all the way. I\'ve told the whole truth on this kit (as always) and you, dear reader, must take it\nfrom there. </p>\n<p>Excelsior! Steve </p>'),(31,330,'1998-01-24 13:52:01',3498,'catwoman','<p>First off, three of these pieces in the kit are non-figural parts: the base, the nameplate (not shown), and the\nwhip. The latter is a plain piece of copper wire, and my only gripe with the kit. A nine or ten foot bullwhip\njust does not come off very well in such a thin rendering. For those wishing a nicer look to the whip, I\nfound that pieces of plastic twist tie (plastic coated wire) can be twisted or braided to more resemble a real\nwhip. </p>\n<p>Catwoman is a very easy piece kit to accomplish in both building and painting. The joints are of \"plug in\"\ntype that, once the sprues are removed can be glued in place with no pinning required. </p>\n<p>The highlight to talk about here, is the sculpting. As with this entire series of Batman kits, Horizon has\nopted for the present comic book version of Miss Kitty. And boy howdy, is this one slick costume she\nnow wears! As with all of Taishiro Sans\'s sculpting, this is one reviewer who stands in awe of all he does.\nHis knowledge of human anatomy is so perfecting, he can jump from the super muscular bane to the full\nsmooth lines of Catwoman and make the characters come to life. Guys who only collect female kits should\nnot judge this one first on the merit of seeing a photo. I know, I know, she isn\'t nude, but she makes a very\nsexy statement fully clothed that a good number of nude studies fail to make! That skin tight costume, those\nblack leather boots, those claws, the whip...oops! I digress! Catwoman seems to just bring out my \"kinky\nside\"! </p>\n<p>The base of this piece is the \"vent\" or register base as opposed to the one with the manhole cover. One of\neither of these bases comes with each of the characters in this Batman series. One can also purchase these\nseparately, or use a building ledge with wall backdrop for any of them. While the smaller standard bases\nrepresent street level by hanging the wall bases on the wall over a stand representing street level. As well,\nHorizon encourages us to take a closer look at this series of bases for other kits besides their own. So this\nis a nice point to segue into talking about them and, hopefully with the magic of computer scans, we can\ntake a look at these bases. </p>\n<p>First, while Catwoman is shown on the base long side facing front, all fine Batman characters may be\nmounted with narrow edge of base as the front. You can display them side by side in this way saving a\nconsiderable amount of shelf space. </p>\n\n<p>\n<center>\n<table bgcolor=\"#000000\">\n<tr>\n<td><img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/steve_goodrich/catwoman_a.jpg\" width=\"97\" height=\"108\"></td>\n<td><img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/steve_goodrich/catwoman_b.jpg\" width=\"145\" height=\"108\"></td>\n<td><img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/steve_goodrich/catwoman_c.jpg\" width=\"72\" height=\"108\"></td>\n<td><img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/steve_goodrich/catwoman_d.jpg\" width=\"113\" height=\"108\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td bgcolor=\"#ffffdd\"><center>Figure A</center></td>\n<td bgcolor=\"#ffffdd\"><center>Figure B</center></td>\n<td bgcolor=\"#ffffdd\"><center>Figure C</center></td>\n<td bgcolor=\"#ffffdd\"><center>Figure D</center></td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n</center>\n</p>\n\n<p>In photo A we see the two standard street bases. To the bottom is the \"vent\" base in which the vent has\nbeen dremeled off and the spot textured to match the sidewalk. (one assumes the manhole base is part of a\nroad) </p>\n<p>Figure B shows two of three backdrop walls, the third being a single window wall. </p>\n<p>Figure C shows, that while unintended by Horizon, one may secure a street base (\"vent\" or \"bashed\" plain\none) with screws to one of the walls... <b>perfect fit!</b> Plus you can also hang this affair on the wall and it\'s\nstrong enough to hold a figure! </p>\n<p>Figure D shows the single window wall piece, ledge piece and stone (optional) gargoyle piece. I chose to\nput Batman himself on this most elaborate set up... only seems logical somehow. </p>\n<p>So, being able to mix and match here, all you diorama fans will be in heaven when you hear Horizon has\njust begun in this series of bases! Get the feeling that before too long one will be able to create an entire\ncity scape? Well you should! </p>\n<p>For the record, here\'s Horizon\'s full line up, to date, on these parts and their prices: </p>\n<p>Street base w/manhole - $9.95<br>\nStreet base w/ panel (\"vent\") - $9.95<br>\nBrick wall w/out windows - $19.95<br>\nBrick wall w/one window - $25.00<br>\nBrick wall w/two windows - $25.00<br>\nLedge #1 - $30.00<br>\nGargoyle #1 - $35.00<br>\n</p>\n<p>Till next we meet, keep those paintbrushes working at warp speed!</p>'),(32,275,'1998-01-22 20:41:06',3498,'catwoman','<img src=\"../garage/letter/u.gif\" alt=\"U\" WIDTH=\"26\" HEIGHT=\"29\">pon\nopening the Catwoman kit, it is obviously the same high quality\nas the rest of the series, [see Robin review also on Gremlins].  She\nis cast in 4 main parts, arms and left leg being separate from the\nbody.  The kit also comes with a wire whip and the standard city\nstreet base that accompanies all in this series.  The castings are\nvery clean completely free of any air bubbles in the resin.  Horizon\nhas done a nice job of capturing the &quot;look&quot; of the comic book version\nof the cat heroine.  She has been sculpted sporting a very sexy\nfigure, but I felt there was something missing........more skin!\nI decided to dress my Catwoman kit in an outfit she was wearing\non a recent cover of the comic book.  The outfit is a two piece\nrevealing Catwoman\'s abdomen and lower back.\n<p>\nWith my new outfit idea in mind, I began assembly.  The parts went\ntogether easily being pinned with brass rod and glued with 5 minute\nepoxy.  Very little work was required on the subtle seam lines\nsurrounding the entire kit.  Once assembled I applied A+B epoxy\nputty in the almost non-existent seams between the parts, and\nsmoothed it out with a Q-tip and water to minimize the sanding\neffort.  After the filler had completely cured I applied several\nthin coats of Plasti-Coat  sandable grey primer.  After the primer\ncured over night I &quot;cut&quot; it down with &quot;0-0-0&quot; steel wool.  Gently\nrubbing the figure under running water with the steel wool smoothes\nthe primer down to an ultra smooth finish.\n<p>\nFor the two piece outfit you could simply paint the clothing\ndemarcation lines on but I decided to actually add a ridge of\nputty around the whole figure to really show the clothing lines.\nI accomplished this by rolling a very small amount of A+B putty\non a table with my finger making a very thin &quot;snake&quot; of putty.\nI then wrapped the &quot;snake&quot; of putty around the figure where the\nclothing line would appear.  Then, using a toothpick dipped in\nwater, I spread the putty Down or up away from her tummy trying\nto smooth it out as much as possible.  After the putty dried\ncompletely over night, I sanded the putty, blending it up or\ndown into the shirt or pants, leaving the ridge at the top of\nthe pants and at the bottom of the shirt.  Tah-dah, new outfit.\nI then painted her using an acrylic undercoat and oil paints for\nthe finish coat.  (See Robin article for oil painting technique).\nNow I needed to add the bow in the front of her shirt.  This was\nmade from A+B putty and attached with 5 minute epoxy.  For the\nfinal touches, I utilized some tips from various Gremlins on this\nsite and added a cool zipper with a pull-ring and glossed her\nlips with Liquitex Gloss Medium.  When using the Gloss Medium\nfor lips or eyes, you want to &quot;puddle&quot; it on and then let it\nself level.  Don\'t mess with it or you will end up leaving\nwhips or brush strokes in it.  Catwoman\'s whip is a wire which\ncan be bent into any position before painting.  I wasn\'t too\nsure how well that was going to work but once glued into her\nhand with 5 minute epoxy, it held any shape I put it into.\n<p>\nI did not use the standard street base with the kit as I was\nprovided with one of Horizon\'s Gothic Bases for this project.\nThe Gargoyle was in about nine parts - the body and head, two\nwings, wing claws, feet, and horns.  The base was in two parts,\nthe vertical and horizontal pieces.  I assembled the base and\nthe large parts of the Gargoyle with 5 minute epoxy and pinned\nbrass rod.  Smaller parts were assembled with just 5 minute epoxy.\nAgain, very little putty was required and the subjects were\nprimered.  I wanted the whole building and Gargoyle to have a\nstone look to it, so I chose a few shades of grey from the\nTestors Model Master Acrylic line and mixed three colors.  I\nmixed a medium shade of grey, a dark color grey and then a\nlight shade.  I shot the whole piece with the medium shade first.\nNext I shot a thin line of the dark color along all of the brick\nlines and any shadow areas on the Gargoyle.  Now it looks a\nlittle strange and needs the highlight color to tone down the\nharsh shadow lines that bled too far into the center of the\nbricks.  Spray the highlight color into the center of each brick\nand on any high areas on the Gargoyle.  Now spray a light mist\nof the highlight color over the whole piece to blend everything\ntogether a little more smoothly.  Next I shot the whole thing\nwith a lacquer gloss coat to seal it and prepare for an oil wash.\nAfter the gloss coat is completely dry, mix up a black or dark\number color oil paint and some thinner.  It should be a lot of\nthinner and very little oil paint in the mixture, it\'s more\nlike dirty thinner.  Apply the wash with a narrow brush into\nthe cracks and crevices.  The gloss coat will help it run just\ninto the cracks.  If the wash bleeds out onto the bricks, you\ncan blend it smooth with a tee shirt or other cloth and your\nfinger.  Once your happy with the effect, shoot it with a flat\ncoat lacquer to dull it back down.  I wasn\'t too sure what to\ndo with the window and just painted the blinds white and the\nframing brown.  Once Catwoman is mounted onto the base it all\nreally comes together and looks great!\n<p>\nI highly recommend any of the kits in the Horizon Solid Model\nseries and I would like to thank Denis and Horizon for providing\nme with these kits, it was very generous of them and their\npatience was endless.  Please e-mail me if you have any questions\nregarding the assembly or painting techniques used for this kit!\n<p>\nMark Warthling'),(33,330,'1998-01-22 20:50:08',4319,'gigantor','Greetings again fellow modeling maniacs. This time around I\'ll be reviewing horizon\'s\nGigantor solid resin kit. </p>\n<p>In an age where garage kits range from utter foolishness to the outright pornographic, it\'s\nnice to think we can also capture a sense of lost innocence and celebrate the gentle\npleasures of our lost childhood. Kits which celebrate cartoons we grew up with are happily\nfinding their own niche in collecting. These include the wondrous Jap-animation classics and\nGigantor pretty much leads the pack hands down in this area. </p>\n<p>At about 5 1/2&quot; tall this piece is quite bulky, but the $79.95 price of the kit, I feel, would\nscare many potential buyers if they couldn\'t look the pieces over. Horizon\'s solid resin kits\nare compared to other companies, stiffly priced. But believe me, I don\'t secretly work for\nHorizon yet can fully attest that for collectors of their given releases, you truly get your\nmoney\'s worth. </p>\n<p>The mega robot Gigantor comes in 19 resin pieces free of air bubbles. Eight joint springs are\nalso included in the kit to be put in at elbow, shoulder, knee and ankle joints. Sculptor Sen\nMaruyama has sculpted the joints in a ball and socket style so you can pose Gigantor in\nvirtually any pose the original robot assumed. </p>\n<p>While not a problem for advanced modeler\'s, I must point out that the entire surface of\nGigantor needs an ultra smooth finish so special care should be taken over your standard\nsanding jobs. </p>\n<p>Let\'s save some time here and include a mini &quot;how to&quot; technique with the review. First, yes,\nit does matter using fine wet/dry sandpaper. Standard sanding is fine where you only have to\ntexture something like hair over mold seam lines, but for skin, tight superhero costumes or\nmetallic surfaces like Gigantor or a car, after you fill any cracks, you owe it to yourself to\nhave a really slick finish as opposed to one that shows sanding scratches. </p>\n<p>A big apology to long time modeler\'s here, as we hit upon a major point for all the\nnewcomers. </p>\n<p>Wet/Dry sandpaper is expensive, but a piece can last for up to a year so it\'s actually\ncheaper than standard sandpaper in the long run. Why wet sanding? Because, simply put,\nthe sandpaper will never clog up on a microscopic level when you keep the surface and\nsandpaper wet. </p>\n<p>Some modeler\'s who I feel, personally, are anal retentives, demand you use a grade of\nnothing less than 4,000. I truly believe they hold stock in companies that manufacture this\nultrafine stock! Really, I only ever use 3200 or 3400 grit and it works just fine. </p>\n<p>To use it, cut a small piece about 2&quot; by 2&quot; and let it soak in a cup of water for about 5\nminutes. Water absorbing qualities of this stuff makes it more supple after this. </p>\n<p>All you really need to do is, once you\'ve removed all seam marks, is go over the surface wet\nand you\'ll amaze yourself at the mirror-like surface you create! </p>\n<p>Back to Gigantor: you\'ll find this kit a surprisingly quick build up and paint job due to\nhorizon\'s present laser pin point aim to perfection and sculpting standards. </p>\n<p>But I do have a gripe, as with many buyers, I don\'t always know how to color the back of a\npiece without extensive research. Horizon, at very least should feature back view photos on\ntheir boxes of the kits. The fun and informative painting instructions by Tom Gilliland once\nprovided are no more. More than anything Horizon should have picked up on how much an\ninstructor of this merit is worth in gold. I, for one, couldn\'t wait for new Horizon kits to come\nout in the past! If one had any reservations about spending on the kit, Tom had us so hyped\nup by the end of his contribution we were inclined to start a religion revolving around the\nsubject matter! Still, Gigantor does have a cult following and I\'d be massively errant to lead\nyou to think that this wasn\'t a true gem of a piece!</p>'),(34,275,'1998-01-24 13:54:48',3513,'robin','<h2>First Impressions</h2>\n<img src=\"../garage/letter/u.gif\" alt=\"U\" WIDTH=\"26\" HEIGHT=\"29\">pon opening the box you find\nthe model is finely cast in an olive green color and in 9 pieces:\nTorso/Right Leg, Left Leg, Right Arm, Left Arm, Head, Cape, Batarang,\nReversible Base, and Logo\nNameplate. Examination of the parts shows very few air bubbles, and an\nextraordinarily smooth finish to\nthe resin except that the casting process has left a raised mold\nregistration line completely surrounding\neach part. Included with the kit are a promotional flyer for other\nHorizon kits and a very general\ninstruction sheet. The instructions provide a picture of the parts\npositioned in their general location for\nassembly, and some basic assembly and painting tips. The modeler\nis referred to the box art for color\nscheme; however, the box art makes the mask appear to be black,\nwhen it should actually be green. Since\nit only shows Robin\'s front view, referencing the comic book was\nnecessary.\n\n<h2>Assembly</h2>\nThe instructions recommend using cyanoacrylate (\"super glue\") for\nassembly, but I chose to use 5 minute\nepoxy. Epoxy provides for more working time to position the parts\nand has a much greater sheer strength\nthan \"super\" glues. I \"pegged\" all of the parts with 1/8\" brass\nrods at all of the assembly joints to ensure a\nstrong bond. I installed the arms, left leg, and head first;\nleaving the Batarang and Cape for last. The\nparts fit was fair, requiring some filler at all of the joints.\nFor this task I use a 2 part epoxy putty, similar\nto Milliput. Since \"A+B\" putty is water soluble, a slightly wet\nfinger or other applicator can be used to\neasily smooth the putty around joints, requiring less sanding\nthan traditional hobby putty. Once the putty\nhad dried overnight, I sanded all of the joints smooth. Next\nI had to tackle the raised mold registration\nlines surrounding the entire kit. These can be easily sanded\nsmooth with a sanding stick, however, I had\nto use some small shaped files for some of the tight areas.\nOnce the surface of the entire model has been\nsanded smooth, it should be washed thoroughly with soapy water.\nThe figure is now ready to receive\npaint.\n\n<h2>Painting</h2>\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/mark_warthling/robin_colors.gif\" width=\"167\" height=\"216\" align=\"right\">\nBefore beginning painting, the figure should be firmly\npegged, by the feet, into a block of wood for ease of handling.\nBegin painting the figure by spraying the entire kit with\nFloquil grey primer from the spray can, and allow it to\nthoroughly dry. Next the figure is \"undercoated\" with <i>Jo\nSonya Artist\'s Gouache</i> acrylics in the appropriate colors.\nThe leggings, sleeves, gloves and mask are green, the shirt\nand pants are red, the cape is black on the back and yellow on\nthe inside, the boots are black and the belt is yellow. The\nacrylic paint should be thinned, with water, to produce a\nsmooth finish, and may require several coats. Once the\nfigure is completely undercoated, the next step is to paint the\nfigure\'s eyes.\n<br clear=\"all\">\n\n<h2>Painting the Eyes</h2>\nA figure\'s eyes are the most important part, because that will\nbe the first place your audience will be looking. A good pair\nof eyes, brings a figure to life! For painting the eyes, a small\nquality brush, like a 10/0, is necessary and a pair of\nOptivisors proves very helpful.\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/mark_warthling/robin_eyes.gif\" width=\"236\" height=\"144\" align=\"left\">\nOne method of painting eyes is\nto first paint the entire eye hole with a\ndark color, I use Polly-S Ogre Brown. The next step is to paint\nthe \"whites\" of the eyes, Polly-S Elf Flesh\nworks well for this, leaving a very thin outline of Ogre Brown\nsurrounding the entire eye. This outline\nrepresents the eyelashes. Next, paint the colored portion of the\neye the desired color, making sure that\nboth eyes are looking in the same direction. Now, place a black\npupil in the center of each colored\nportion. For a final touch, a back-light can be added. The\nback-light is the natural tiny light reflection\nthat is present in everyone\'s eyes. Simply wet your 10/0\nbrush with some white and make a very tiny\nwhite dot in the same spot on each eye. (refer to Diagram-l)\nThe figure is now ready to receive oil paint.\n<br clear=\"all\">\n\n<h2>Painting Flesh with Oils</h2>\nThe first step in painting flesh with oil paints is to mix\nthe color. I have provided a ratio chart with the\ncolors I used, but the mixture can be modified to your liking.\nRealizing that precision in not required\nhere, I use toothpicks to remove the oil paint from the tubes\nin approximate measurements, and mix the\ncolors well. Apply the flesh sparingly as oil paint will go a\nlong way. Once you have covered all of the\nflesh areas, smooth it out with a soft flat shader brush to\nproduce a smooth surface.\n\n<h2>Highlights and Shadows</h2>\nWhen you apply a shadow or highlight, you want to apply a\nvery small amount in the appropriate area and\nthen \"blend\" it with the \"basic\" flesh already on the figure.\nWhen blending, it is very important to use a\nquality #0 or #1 soft brush, like red sable. Blending requires\na very light touch and patience which will\nresult in a soft feathered edge between the original color and\nthe shadow or highlight color. Again, use\nyour blending colors very sparingly, as you can always add later\nif necessary.\n<p>\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/mark_warthling/robin_face.jpg\" width=\"189\" height=\"216\" align=\"right\">\nApply dark shadows, an\neven mixture of Burnt Umber and Venetian Red, at the edge of\nthe hairline, collar, mask, at the crease\nbetween the nose and face and in the nostrils. Medium shadows,\nusing Mars Orange, can be applied\nunder the bags of the eyes, on the sides of the nose, and\nanywhere else you feel there is medium depth.\nFor highlights, I use Naples Yellow and Naples Yellow Lt.\nExtra with White for the \"High\" Highlights.\nApply your shadow colors first, blending each area before\nmoving to the next. To blend, \"dab\" at the\nshadow/highlight color lightly, basically mixing it with the\nbase color on the figure. As you blend, you\nshould wipe your brush often on a paper towel to keep from\njust smearing the colors everywhere. Next\napply the highlight color, Naples Yellow, to the high points\non the face and arms such as the forehead,\ncheekbones, bridge of the nose, tips of the nostrils, and chin.\nAt the very highest points, use the Naples\nYellow Lt. Extra and/or White. Blend the highlights in the same\nmanner as the shadows. After\ncompleting the flesh areas, you should allow it to dry overnight\nbefore working on Robin\'s clothes, this\nallows the flesh to dry to a natural \"sheen\" depicting the oils\nin skin.\n\n<p>\nFor Robin\'s Leggings, Sleeves,\nMask and Gloves, I used Permanent Green. Application is basically\nthe same as with the flesh. I\nshadowed the green color with Greenish Umber, and highlighted\nwith the Naples Yellow Lt. Extra,\nblending them in the same manner as with the flesh colors.\nAfter completing the green areas, I placed the\nfigure in the oven for about an hour at approximately 150 degrees.\nThis does several things, it accelerates the\ndrying of the oils, dries the color to a matte finish, and\nallows you to begin painting the next clothing\ncolor almost immediately. Repeat this step after each clothing\ncolor. The alternate to this method is to\ncomplete the figure, and then spray the clothing with a matte\nclear coat, and remember you can only use\nthe \"cooking\" method with resin figures as vinyl and plastic\nfigures will melt. For his Shirt and Pants, I\nused Cadmium Red mixed with a dab of Mars Red. I shadowed the\nred color with Acra Violet and\nhighlighted with Cadmium Orange mixed with Naples Yellow Lt. Extra.\nThe boots are supposed to be\nBlack, but I used Indigo mixed with a dab of Ivory Black to\nresult in a very dark Blue/Black color. I\nshadowed with Ivory Black and highlighted with Titanium White.\nI used the same colors to paint\nRobin\'s black hair. Now that the figure is basically complete,\nthe Cape, Belt and Batarang can be painted.\n<p>\nThe inside of the cape should be painted first, as it will be\nsnug against Robin\'s back when attached. For\nthe yellow portions of the Cape, and for Robin\'s belt, Cadmium\nLemon can be used for the basic color,\nRaw Sienna for shadows, and Titanium White for the highlights.\nHold off painting the yellow folds that\ncome over Robin\'s shoulders until the Cape has been installed\nbecause some filling in that area will be\nrequired. Once the yellow has been completed, install the Cape\nto the back of the figure. Once the epoxy\nhas cured, use the A+B putty to fill the gaps at the Cape\'s\njoints. Sand the area smooth and re-apply the\nblade and yellow undercoat colors where filling and sanding\nwas required. Next paint the black portions\nof the Cape and the Batarang using the same colors used for\nthe boots and hair. The Batarang can now be\nglued in Robin\'s Left Hand. Finally, Robin is ready to be\nfixed to his base, but first the base must be\npainted.\n\n<h2>Painting the Base and Completing the Display</h2>\nThe base provided in the kit is reversible, and can be\npositioned to have a large manhole cover or a\ngeneric meter cover in the street. I chose the manhole\ncover for my project. I painted the street with a\ngrey color and then laid down a dirty wash. I painted\nthe manhole cover with Humbrol Scenic Track\ncolor adding some dry-brushed white highlights. I used\nVerlinden Static grass in two of the corners of the\nbase to add some additional character to my display.\nI then painted the frame of the base with black\nenamel. Now Robin can be added to the base to complete the scene!\nI drilled holes in the base to\naccommodate the 1/8\" brass pegs already in Robin\'s feet\nto insure a strong bond, and once again used the\n5 minute epoxy.\n<p>\n<center><img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/mark_warthling/robin.jpg\" width=\"316\" height=\"432\"></center>\n<p>\nRobin is now complete and can occupy a proud place in your\ndisplay case. I enjoyed building this kit and\nspent about 25 hours completing it. I would like to thank\nIPMS for providing the kit, and look forward to\nbuilding and painting other kits in this series.\n<p>\nMark Warthling\n<p>\n<font size=\"-1\"><i>Originally published in the IPMS Journal Magazine.\nReprinted here with permission. Thanks, IPMS!</i></font><br>\n<p>\n<b>\nFor another perspective take a peek at\n<a href=\"http://www.smithy.net/Cybersmith/SFM/review_resin.html\">Garret\nParsons Robin review</a> in\n<a href=\"http://www.smithy.net/Cybersmith/SFM/SFM.html\">Quantum\'s\nScience Fiction Modeler</a>.\n</b>'),(35,330,'1998-01-22 20:58:15',3520,'the_joker','<p>Cesar Romero gave us a splendid &quot;clown price of crime&quot; in the 60\'s version of Batman. A high camp fun\nlover. </p>\n<p>Jack Nicholson was a more sinister paradox in Tim Burton\'s <b>Batman</b>. Rather a demented Mafia style\nprankster. </p>\n<p>D.C. comics have only recently started to show us what the Joker is all about. Despite his larger than life\ncomedic side, he is also a full fledged sadist (D.C.\'s &quot;The Killing Joke), the paragon of a &quot;flaming queen&quot;\n(D.C.\'s &quot;Arkham Asylum), and a cold blooded murderer (D.C.\'s &quot;A Death in the Family&quot;). His darker side\nhas finally been exposed in comic/graphic novel form. </p>\n<p>In rendering a solid resin model kit of the Joker in the likeness of the comic book version, one tends to\nwonder if it can be done. Horizon, thanks to the nearly supernatural talents of Taishiro San, has done so!\nThis artwork denotes every single fun loving urge of the Joker and all the psychotic under statements of his\npersonality while he\'s captured in the act of robbing a bank vault; rather mundane compared to his true\ngoals! </p>\n<p>As the Joker is rather a &quot;touchy subject&quot; presently (to say the least!) Horizon has done him full justice. </p>\n<p>First up, astute builders will notice that the box cover photo and painting instructions refer to the exposed\nright wrist. This is not in the resin kit: the right glove fits snugly onto the right arm. A call to Horizon got an\nanswer to this mystery. When the Joker was first sculpted by Taishiro San, there was no wrist. It was\nsomething that had to be added for the sake of vinyl molding. (This kit was originally released as a vinyl\nfree standing piece.) In this resin release, the piece has been restored to it\'s original anatomical integrity.\nThe subsequent base and backdrop sculpting was done by Bob Dullam and makes a dioramatic chunk of\nresin for those so inclined. It follows that despite my earlier review rantings, this particular Horizon resin is\nwell within accepted price ranges. </p>\n<p>The base&quot; is an affair of four pieces with have pinning marks pre-marked for sturdy assembly. The Joker is\nin six major pieces. A Plexiglas dowel serves as his walking stick. </p>\n<p>Four tiny resin pieces make up the lapel flower. (heaven know what it\'s squirting into the face of victims on\ntoday\'s agenda), and three strands of hair have fallen out of place. Seemingly puerile details such as the last\nreally do add a cavalier aspect to models! </p>\n<p>A sheet of printed &quot;money&quot; is included on the instruction sheet to cut out, fold, and glue to add to the piece.\nActually, one will need to have several color laser copies made of this sheet to make all the money\ndepicted on the box cover, if so desired. </p>\n<p>While seam lines are present to sand off on all parts of this piece and major pour gate spots must be\ndremeled or sawed off, the pieces of this review kit lacked one single air bubble. Careful trimming of\npieces plus that last bit assure no putty work. Again, Horizon is going turbo charged on the genius level of\nhow and where they make their figural cut lines. But prepare yourself for a nightmare of epic scale when\nyou come to paint this one! </p>\n<p>As you sand off the seam lines of the pants inseams, you will also sand out a bit of inscribed detail. On\nouter seams this doesn\'t matter as you can pretty well &quot;eyeball&quot; the black stripes of his pants. Like\nBilliken\'s Nicholson Joker, painting between the legs may well result in a permanent vacation in Arkham\nAsylum! Proceed with caution and work with a game plan is the order of the day. </p>\n<p>All in all, I can safely recommend this kit to not only fans of the Batman mythos, but to anyone as a nice\ncoffee table piece, for those truly into the ultra demented. </p>\n<p>A last note is that we have a hangover from the vinyl version of this kit: Tom Gilliland painting instructions!\nFor all Tom\'s instruction sheets, I\'ve finally broken a cryptic code! In painting eyes!!!!! Tom has always\nreferred to the iris... the colored part of the eye, as the pupil! Bingo! He\'s a brilliant artist, but missed the\nanatomy of the eyeball in high school. The pupil is ALWAYS the final black dot we paint in the central\nspot of the iris. </p>\n<p>For the purist, the Joker, according to D.C. Comics (and not Tom) has blazing red brown eyes rather than\ngreen. To accomplish this; paint iris in a &quot;rust&quot; or red- brown color. When dry, paint inner iris in medium\nbrown leaving an outer ring of the red around iris. Dot pupil in with black using a very fine toothpick tip\ndipped in watered down black paint. When dry, use a 10/0 brush to strike in super fine dots of white on\neach eyeball at same positioning over the brown, and/or, black... the &quot;lifelight&quot;, as it\'s called, will make a\nmonumental change in eyes that are even glossed over. </p>\n<p>A footnote to this review is that modeling master himself, Tom Gilliland, considers this Joker as his all time\n&quot;best kit&quot;. I know Tom well enough to know this isn\'t a lot of b.s. hype Horizon paid him to say while he\nwas working for them. He\'s on the most respected names in this hobby and, while I can\'t echo his feelings,\nI sure see where he\'s coming from! The Joker is one major league cut above most of what\'s out there! </p>\n<p>Enter this psychopath\'s world at your own risk - then go crazy! </p>'),(36,190,'1998-01-23 08:08:47',4320,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/h.gif\" alt=\"H\" WIDTH=\"25\" HEIGHT=\"30\">orizon\'s\nJurassic Park T-Rex was a kit I was glad to see produced. \nWhen I recieved mine I was instantly impressed with the packaging.  Like the\n<a href=\"raptor_review.html\">Velociraptor</a> kit, the box art on the T-Rex\nshowed a nicely detailed finished\nmodel as well as a good close-up shot of the head.  I really must say that I\nappreciate Horizon\'s efforts to include photos of finished kits on the box as\nthis becomes a nice reference piece for future paint schemes.  They also\nincluded nice, easy to understand assembly instructions as well as complete\ninstructions on how to duplicate the box art paint scheme.  I chose not to\npaint mine like theirs, I instead flipped through pages and pages of dinosaur\nbooks until I found a pattern I liked.  By the way, I recommend &quot;The Ultimate\nDinosaur Book&quot; by David Lambert for both good reading and as color reference\nmaterial for dinosaurs.\n<p>\nThe kit itself was molded in vinyl and consisted of 12 parts all of\nwhich required the usual careful trimming.  In addition to this trimming there\nwas a small &quot;flash line&quot; along the left leg that required some trimming up. \nAlso included in the kit was a wire stand with which to mount your rex model\non.  I went ahead and used this even though it probably could have been\noptional as the beast has a natural balance point when assembled and could\nstand without it.\n<p>\nAssembly was fairly easy and did not require too much putty work. \nHeating the vinyl and pre-fitting the parts together allows for a nice fit\nbetween most of the parts.  My kit did require some putty work along the\nneck/body, head/lower jaw, and body/main tail pieces to make the kit look\nright.  Perhaps the most difficult part of the assembly process was the head. \nIt took me a long time to get the head pieces to fit together to my liking.  I\nfound it helped to assemble the head/upper teeth and lower jaw/lower teeth\nseparately and then paint the mouth pieces along with the tongue before\ncontinuing to put the rest of the head together.  I also took the extra time to\ninstall 5mm taxidermy eyes into the model before putting the head/upper teeth\nsections together.  It is worth it to spend extra time on the head as this is\nthe most prominent part of the kit and is the first thing the viewer usually\nsees when looking at the model.\n<p>\nThe assembled model itself stood about 11&quot; high and 24.5&quot; long.  The\nRex is posed standing on both feet with its head turned slightly to the left,\nmouth agape.  The pose is nice but I felt it lacked a bit of energy and\ncould\'ve been made a bit more dynamic.  I chose not to alter mine but perhaps a\nslight change in posture would have made the model seem a bit more lifelike. \nThis is not to say the overall likeness isn\'t effective.  It does have the look\nof a T-Rex and the detailing was very nice.  I really liked the folds of skin\nalong the belly and neck along with the skin texture that runs the length of\nthe underside of the tail.  The real eye catcher with this kit is the head. \nThe detail work in the mouth and teeth along with the tongue and eyes really\nhelp draw the attention right to the business end of the T-Rex.  By installing\n<a href=\"taxidermy_eyes.html\">taxidermy eyes</a> in the kit I was able to add an additional level of realism to\nthe Rex.  They really helped it come to life.  The eyes I chose were 5mm frogs\neyes (Hey, in the movie they used frog DNA right?) that I got from Van Dykes.\n<p>\nAs a side note I will mention that the kit is not quite scientifically\naccurate as the arms are longer than fossil evidence suggests.  Other than that\nit is a good approximation of a T-Rex.  The tail could be a bit straighter\nthough so it looks more like it was balancing the animal.\n<p>\nIn conclusion I would like to say that Horizon\'s T-Rex was a great kit\neven for the original retail of $65.  Unfortunately, Horizon has since\ndiscontinued their Jurassic Park line making this kit hard to find.  If you can\nfind one and are a dinosaur nut I recommend getting it before they become\nextinct.  However, if the rumors of the second movie &quot;Lost World&quot; are true then\nwe may just see a rerelease of this bargain dino kit.\n'),(37,190,'1998-01-23 08:11:30',3526,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/d.gif\" alt=\"D\" WIDTH=\"22\" HEIGHT=\"29\">uring\na search for decent dinosaur kits I decided to give Horizon\'s\nJurassic Park Velociraptor a try.  Horizon has done an excellent job with this\nrendition of the deadly velociraptors from the movie.  The packaging, as with\nany horizon kit, was super, providing a great pictures of the completed model. \nThese pictures include close-ups of the head area which aids in the later\ndetailing of the eyes.  The instructions were easy to follow and provided a few\nkey pictures of the required assembly.\n<p>\nThis vinyl kit contains 31 pieces and is a great kit for those new to\nthe hobby or the experienced modeler.  The vinyl pieces in my kit had very few\nholes in the casting and most of the pieces fit together snugly.  Some parts\nrequired heating and pressing together in order to get them to fit correctly. \nThe kit also included a wire loop base designed to be inserted in the left leg\nas a support.  I had to bend this a bit to conform with the curvature of the\nleg but it inserted into the foot easily and was also easy to conceal on my\nscratch-built base.\n<p>\nThe head was perhaps the most time consuming to assemble.  It consisted\nof five pieces and the teeth sections did not fit as snugly into the jaws as I\nwould have liked.  This was the area that required the most putty.  Speaking of\nputty, the kit itself did not require much filling.  The areas on mine which\nrequired the most were the tail/body junction and the teeth/jaw pieces.\n<p>\nThe pose of the finished model is very active.  It portrays the raptor\nin mid run with its left leg on the ground, right foot curled up as if taking a\nstep, and the body is twisting to the left as if the critter is darting in a\nnew direction.  The detail of the kit is great.  Most notable is the realistic\ndinosaur flesh (as realistic as anyone could guess anyway) and very noticeable\nbelly folds.  I also liked the rendition of the &quot;terrible claw&quot; for which these\ndinosaurs are famous.  These rear claws are posed in a &quot;cocked&quot; position and\nlook rather menacing.  I will mention that those looking for a scientifically\naccurate velociraptor will find that the Horizon model is not quite correct. \nThe main discrepancy is that the tail is curved instead of being held stiff and\nstraight for balance.  Also the head is a bit shorter and not longer and\nnarrower as fossil evidence shows.  The likeness to the movie critter is dead\non however and those that like the movie will find the dimensions to their\nliking.\n<p>\nAlterations to this kit were also simple to make.  The soft polyvinyl\nallows for ease in repositioning the figure into a desired pose.  Please\nremember that vinyl does have a &quot;memory&quot; so radical changes will need support\nand some cutting and resculpting work may be necessary.  I also found that the \ninsertion of 11mm taxidermy eyes was relatively simple (an idea given me by \nLarry Brackney - thanks Larry!!).  I cut out the old eyes and seated the glass \nones from the inside of the model.  If you plan on doing this I suggest\ninstalling them BEFORE assembling the head.\n<p>\nIn closing I feel that Horizon\'s JP Velociraptor provides you with a\ntop-quality dinosaur model (I had a friend of mine call it museum quality!) for\na great price - $65 is listed retail.  As mentioned above, those who like the\nmovie critters will find this one to their liking.  Those looking for\nscientifically correct models may not enjoy this model as much but I feel that\nit is a great kit and would recommend it to anyone who is interested in one.  \nAlso I forgot to mention the size of this model.  It stands 13&quot; high and is \n17.5&quot; long from nose to tail.  So get \'em while they last.\n'),(38,305,'1998-01-23 08:14:27',3529,'wind_slasher','<img src=\"../garage/letter/h.gif\" alt=\"H\" WIDTH=\"25\" HEIGHT=\"30\">ello fellow Gremlins!!\n<p>\nI am going to attempt a review of the latest Horizon release, \nWind Slasher, sculpted by Steve Wang.  For those of you familiar with Steve\'s work, no\nintroduction is needed.  For those unfamiliar, Steve sculpted the legendary 1/1 scale Gremlin, and\nis responsible for the Guyver kit.\n<p>\nThe Wind Slasher is a 1:8 scale sculpture of an original design by Steve.  The creature is cast\nin solid resin, and resembles a spider/crab, with a torso, head and two arms, one of which is\nholding a white metal sword.  The kit includes a base, which is a four-pedestal tree stump affair. \nEach of the creatures legs rests on one of the four stumps, creating a mid-air look.  Once based,\nthe kit comes in at about 10 inches tall.\n<p>\nSculpting quality left a little bit to be desired in my opinion.  Overall detail was plentiful, but &quot;soft&quot;. \nLines of detailing were not the crisp, fine look we have come to expect.  Match this kit up with\na comparably priced Fewture model, and it falls short.  Although I\'m glad to see another Steve\nWang kit, I don\'t believe this is his best work.\n<p>\nThe casting quality was very good, with only four bubbles to contend with.  The resin itself is\nrather soft, making cleanup a breeze.  Be careful if using the backside of an X-acto knife to\nremove mold lines, as the resin is easily gouged.  Better to use files or fine sandpaper.  The\nsword blade and a nameplate were cast in white metal\n<p>\nAssembly is also straight forward.  The kit includes pre-cast pilot holes to aid in drilling and\nplacing pins.  Pins are recommended for the torso to main body, main body to tail connections. \nThe only &quot;fiddly&quot; part to deal with is the placement of the legs.  Since the legs must rest on the\nstumps of the base, it is recommended they be done last.  Horizon does not provide numbers for \nthe legs or tell you which is which, so leg placement is a guessing game.  I had to modify the\nposition of one of the stumps of the base to get my kit to sit correctly.  I would also recommend\npinning the legs to the body, once their correct positions are determined.  After gluing the legs\ninto position,  I drilled upward through the legs into the main body and inserted pins.\n<p>\nAs for painting the kit, I would recommend you paint the underside first, before mounting the\nkit to the base.  This will become obvious once you try to get a brush between the bases stumps!!\nSince this is an original design, you are free to paint the Wind Slasher anyway you choose. \nHorizon provides instructions on painting the kit to look like the box photo.\n<p>\nOverall, the Wind Slasher is a very nice kit.  Is it worth $195.00?  I don\'t think so.\nIn my opinion it\'s a bit overpriced, considering Horizon\'s &quot;Sil&quot; kit is $89.95.\nHowever, it does build up nicely and looks good when put together.\nSo if you\'re a fan of original design model kits, I would recommend it,\nbut definitely look around and try to find it at a cheaper price.\n<p>\nHappy modeling!!'),(39,226,'1998-01-24 13:56:30',3543,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/s.gif\" alt=\"S\" WIDTH=\"21\" HEIGHT=\"33\">pawn.\nThis semi-demonic character created for Todd MacFarlane\'s Image comic\nwas destined to be a hot license. I\'m sure that there were many people\nout there just waiting for the official vinyl kit to be produced. There\nwere probably many new people brought into the hobby just for this one\nkit. Wouldn\'t it have been nice for their experience to be a positive\none? Well, anybody hoping to cut their teeth on this kit is likely to\nrun screaming into stamp collecting or some other less frustrating\nhobby.\n<p>\nFirst, I want to point out that this kit has potential. John Dennett\nis an incredible sculptor who never fails to provide great detail with\na dynamic pose. Everything good about the kit can be leveled on his\nshoulders. The sculptor did a wonderful job in capturing the right\nfeel for this character. I hope that John keeps up the good work.\n<p>\nThe problems obviously started in the production end of this kit.\nFrom looking at the built-up kit you get the impression that this\nshould have been a cake-walk for Inteleg to do. You have a basic\nhuman form, a cape, some chain, and a base. Okay, making the\ntransition from cape to base might have been a little tricky but\notherwise this is all stuff that other companies have been doing\nsucessfully for years. How could they go wrong?\n<p>\nThey did.\n<p>\nThe body pieces (arms, legs, torso) fit together well enough. The\nupper and lower torso could have been better but that\'s not hard\nto fix and the seam will also be hidden a bit by the chain. The\nhigh collar... needed more definite lines to be able to tell\nwhere to trim it - but again, it goes together okay... until you\ntry to mount it on the body. Then you have the cape - which just\nplain sucks. It just doesn\'t fit properly and all of the heating\nin the world doesn\'t change this. There is a little piece that\nneed to be fitted on the inside of the cape but it is unclear\nwhere, exactly. Should the collar go on before or after the cape?\nWhere does the cape on the body connect to the cape piece on the base?\nThe pieces just aren\'t intuitively seperated to make construction easy. \n<p>\nOf course, had the instructions been clear none of this would have\nbeen a real problem. The instructions are not clear. What you get\nis a basic front view line drawing of the kit with the pieces\nunassembled with lines pointing to where they go. Well, this \nis very little help since the drawing is rudimentary and there\nis no printed help beyond the most generic type (don\'t heat your\nx-acto blade, glue it with Zap, fill seams with Milliput). The\nclosest it comes to actually talking about specifics of assembling\nthe kit is it tells you to refer to the comics to figure out how\nto position the chain!! Nothing that tells you how to make this\nloose plastic chain nice and rigid like on the cover.\n<p>\nOuside of these problems I also noticed that the vinyl seemed to\nhave its own problems. There was a strange texture on my cape that\nmade me think that perhaps it had been a bad batch or something.\nOne area of the cape had a large void in an area that should have\nbeen solid where I was trimming away the excess. This meant even\nmore filling, rescupting and sanding than normal.\n<p>\nThis is not a kit for beginners, nor is it a kit for anybody who\nis easily frustrated. Personally, I would tell everybody to stay\naway from anything from Inteleg until they learn how vinyl kits\nare made but since they are the only ones doing Spawn I\'m sure\nthat won\'t stop everybody. At least you can be forwarned. Garage\nkits should be fun to build and paint. Inteleg has managed to\ntake all the fun out of it.\n\n<p><center><img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/jay_adan/spawn_base.jpg\" width=\"544\" height=\"266\"></center><p>'),(40,270,'1998-01-23 08:24:36',3545,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/j.gif\" alt=\"J\" WIDTH=\"20\" HEIGHT=\"29\">anus Company has been\nproducing resin kits of Lugosi and Karloff &quot;classic characters&quot; for a\ncouple years now, and have been\ncomplimented for their quality sculpting and casting.  A recent tribute kit,\nLon Chaney - Man of a Thousand Faces, lives up to their high standards, and\nshould make the Chaney family proud.\n\n<p>\nIf you have a copy of Amazing Figure Modeler #1 laying around, you\'ve seen\nsome excellent photos of David Fisher\'s build-up.  The kit features Chaney\nsitting on a stool in front of a crate.  His make-up kit sits on top of the\ncrate along with some other paraphernalia, and he\'s in the process of applying\nyet another make-up.  A trio of busts representing some of his more famous\ncreations: Quasimodo, Eric, and the Vampire sit on pedestals on the back of\nthe large oval base.  The kit is in 1:6 scale, and the base measures 15&quot;x10&quot;\nalong its major and minor axes.  A nameplate is also included, along with a\nseveral scale &quot;photos&quot; to throw around the base, a sheet of mylar for\ndetailing the mirror Chaney\'s holding, a stencil for labeling the crate, and\na signed certificate of authenticity which may be affixed to the bottom of\nthe base.\n\n<p>\nYou may well think it\'s Christmas morning when opening the large box that\nis brimming with goodies.  The kit is cast in 20+ creamy resin pieces.  All\nof the castings are clean with no significant pinholes and only a few\nbubbles.  No mold offsets were present, and the seam lines cleaned up easily.\n Since the kit was designed to be painted in sections and then assembled, the\nonly putty work you\'ll need to do is to repair any of the few casting flaws.\n\n<p>\nJeff Yahger\'s sculpting is outstanding.  The busts alone are worth the\nprice of admission IMO.  One of my favorite things about the kit is that the\npieces DO lend themselves to individual painting.  I\'ve used the busts as\nmini-projects in between other kits, so they\'re ready to go on the pedestals\nas I complete the rest of the kit.  A side-by-side comparison of Yahger\'s\nPhantom and the Horizon Phantom reveals that there IS no comparison.\nYahger\'s faces must be seen to be believed.  Skin folds, texture, and\nexpression have all been given the treatment they deserve.  The Vampire bust\nin this kit simply blows the doors off of the Screamin\' equivalent IMHO.\n\n<p>\nLikewise, the make-up kit is intricately detailed.  Face paint, wigs,\nteeth, eye pieces, etc. are faithfully reproduced in 1:6 scale.  I suspect\nthat the make-up kit will take a good deal of time to do justice, even using\nthe labels which are provided.\n\n<p>\nThe Chaney figure itself is not outdone by the rest of the kit.  The pose\nis natural and relaxed, and fits together quite well.  The figure sits on the\nstool easily with no balancing problems whatsoever.  Chaney truly looks like\nhe is enjoying his work.\n\n<p>\nThe bottom line?  This is one CLASSY piece.  Probably one of the most\nrespectful pieces of sculpture/casting that the hobby has yet seen.  The\nfolks cranking out kits in homage of Ed Gein and Charles Manson should take a\ntip from Janus.  There are subjects truly worthy of producing kits of.  This\nis one.  Lon Chaney is a limited edition casting for $170 from Janus.  If\nyou\'re a fan of Chaney, or classic horror, I recommend this one highly.'),(41,201,'1998-01-24 13:57:31',3550,'hunchback','<b><img src=\"../garage/letter/t.gif\" alt=\"T\" WIDTH=\"21\" HEIGHT=\"29\">he Hunchback Of Notre Dame...</b>\nJust reading the title brings about vivid memories of ringing bells\nand gothic images of stone gargoyles.\nAurora captured the essence of this classic story back in the \'60s with\na single moment frozen in plastic.  If you built the original\nAurora kit back when it was first released I\'m sure you will\nremember it!\n<p>\nAurora\'s original copyright on The Hunchback Of Notre Dame kit\nwas registered in 1963 and never renewed.\nIf a copyright originally secured before January 1, 1964, was not\nrenewed at the proper time, copyright protection expired at the\nend of the 28th calendar year of the copyright, and could not be\nrestored.  Thus, this kit entered the public domain in 1991.\n<p>\nNow in 1996, JimZ Hobbies honors this great kit by bringing it back\nfor all of us to enjoy again.  The molds for this new version were\nmade from original Aurora parts.  The parts are cast in tan resin.\nI was very impressed with the quality of the parts! I couldn\'t spot\nany mold lines or flash on any of the parts.  Most importantly, the\nair bubbles that can plaque resin kits were almost totally absent!\nThis kit is on par with the best quality resin castings that I have\nseen made in the US.\n<p>\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/denis_bohm/hunch_art.jpg\" width=\"144\" height=\"360\" hspace=\"8\" vspace=\"8\" align=\"left\">\nThe parts come in a box with a copy of the original Aurora\nbox art.\nA copy of the original instructions are also included.\nYou need to make sure you don\'t follow these directions and\nuse plastic cement on these parts.  Since the parts are made\nout of resin superglue should be used.\n<p>\nThis kit is\neasier to build than the original plastic version.  There are no\nsprues to remove the parts from, so this reduces the amount of\ncleanup to be done.  Also, since the kit is resin, fast setting\nsuperglue can be used.\n<p>\nOne way to fill seams on this kit is to thin out some putty and\n\"paint\" it on. Dr. Microtools Supreme Putty or Squadron White Putty\ncan be mixed with nail polish remover to thin down until it\'s a\nlittle thicker than paint.  Then use a inexpensive brush and \"paint\"\nthe thinned putty into the seam.  After it dries just scrap away\nwith an X-Acto and the seam is filled. The same method can be used\nto fill any air bubbles.  After scraping you can use a paper towel\nor Q-Tip dampened with nail polish remover to smooth over the\nscrape marks. You can even replicate the texture on the clothes\nby dabbing with the towel. Just be careful not to use too\nmuch remover or you\'ll wipe the putty out of the seam and have\nto start over.\n<p>\nI decided to assemble the figure without his hands and chains\nand then paint all the parts.  After painting I carefully cut\nthrough the chain hoops and bent the hoops open so I could\nput them around the appropriate parts of the figure.\nThen a touch of superglue fastens the hoops back together.\nThis let me fill the seams and paint without having the\nchains constantly in the way.\n<p>\nThis is great kit!  I thoroughly enjoyed building and painting it!\n<p>\nYou can order one from:\n<dl><dd>\nJimZ Hobbies<br>\nP.O. Box #22<br>\nClawson, Michigan 48017<br>\nPhone: (810) 435-KITS<br>\nEmail: <a href=\"mailto:jimz@Fe3.rust.net\">jimz@Fe3.rust.net</a>\n</dl>\n<p>\n<font size=\"-1\"><i>Aurora is a registered trademark owned by\nTomy America and these kits are neither authorized or approved\nby Tomy America.</i></font>'),(42,170,'1998-01-23 08:36:58',3578,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/a.gif\" alt=\"A\" WIDTH=\"23\" HEIGHT=\"31\">s mentioned in an\n<a href=\"alien3_review.html\">earlier review</a>, I happen to live fairly close to Kit Kraft in Studio\nCity, California. During one of my many visits, I noticed this figure standing in the corner of\nthe display case. A figure dressed in &quot;pilgrim&quot; type attire holding the severed head of a vampire\nstanding atop a staircase. A very dark and cold looking piece. Upon closer inspection, attention to\ndetail was evident. From the expressive faces of both the slayer and the victim to the buckles and\nembossments on the straps and scabbard. But It wasn\'t the type of kit I was looking for on this\nvisit. This was the case with several visits afterwards. One day, one of the other contributing\nwriters to Gremlins in the Garage e-mailed me telling me he had picked up The Vampyre\nHunter kit.  Finally, 4 months after first seeing the kit, I bought it. Unfortunately I had waited\ntoo long. The kit no longer included the &quot;crypt staircase&quot;. This I was told, was to reduce the\nprice of the kit from $169.99 to $129.99.  I thought the staircase was nice, since I have a very\nhard time trying to create bases for most of my kits. The lack of the base worked to my advantage\nthough. It just so happened that\n<a href=\"http://www.dimensionaldesigns.com/index.html\">Dimensional Designs</a>\nhad just started their own web page through Gremlins in the Garage. Scrolling through their on-line\n<a href=\"http://www.dimensionaldesigns.com/gallery.html\">gallery</a>, I saw the\n<a href=\"http://www.dimensionaldesigns.com/gothic_door_gargoyles.html\">Gothic Door with Gargoyles</a>\nand immediately knew this was the base for my Vampyre Hunter.\n\n<p>\nThe Vampyre Hunter is an extremely detailed kit comprised of many different &quot;media&quot;.\nThe broad sword, ribbons, lock of hair, mallet, and hanging cross are white metal. The chain to\nhang the cross is also metal. The buttons on the jacket are glass beads. Details abound on this kit.\nIf you look on the scabbard there is a skull and a face embossed on it. The stakes are balsa wood\nwhich needs to be whittled down to points. The face detail on Roman Thorne &quot;The Vampyre\nHunter&quot; and the vampire itself are incredible. The story goes that the face of Roman Thorne is\nthat of Peter Murphy, member of BauHaus. Expressions on both faces are excellent. Roman has a\nsteely eyed stare. The vampire has his tongue half sticking out, eyes rolling back in his head.\nRoman is cast in solid white resin comprised of 8 pieces including his hat.\n\n<p>\nThe instructions are well written with plenty of good tips for assembly and painting. The\ninstructions were written by Tom Gilliland who is also credited with the character/model design\nand storyline (included on the first page of the instructions). My kits casting was average. No\nmold-offset lines or any real BIG problems. However, I think Kit Kraft will need to replace\ntheir molds soon, as my kit had plenty of mold material in-between some of the tighter places\nlike the breeches (ruffles on the lower part of the pants) and had excess resin where the\nprevious kit took some of the mold with it. There were also quite a bit of pockets where air\nbubbles had been on the hair. This kit has quite a bit of &quot;sprue&quot; where the parts were poured.\nA lot of little clean-up was needed, but nothing the average modeler couldn\'t handle and well\nworth the effort.\n\n<p>\nAssembly was straightforward. I like to assemble the major parts first then paint. This\nis not exactly as the instructions stated.  The only part of the assembly that was a bit confusing\nwas where the torso joined the lower legs. The lower legs have a circular hole to accept a\nmolded-in pin on the torso. However the pins on the torso are square or half round. Again, not a\nbig problem with a little grinding. One area not mentioned is the hand holding the head. I\nrecommend  drilling a hole in Roman\'s left hand top and bottom, and a small matching hole on the\ntop of the vampire\'s head. I used a straightened paper clip as a pin. I also drilled holes in the\nheels of his shoes and on the base for pins to insure stability once mounted.\n\n<p>\nPainting instructions were excellent. Very good tips for effects. I had the advantage of\nseeing Tom\'s completed kit which aided me in painting mine. I have to admit that on most of my\nkits 85 percent is done by airbrush. This kit was different. The faces, pants and only base coats\nwere airbrushed. Everything else was done using a regular brush utilizing washes and dry brushing.\nOnce completed I set Roman aside and started on the Gothic Door.\n\n<p>\nAs I mentioned, the Crypt base is not longer an option on the Vampyre Hunter kit.\nFortunately, Dimensional Designs has created a series of well sculpted bases with doors. After a\nlong period of indecision, I picked the Gothic Door with Gargoyles. When it arrived, I was amazed\nat the size. The height of the door alone is as tall as the Vampyre Hunter, add the wall and there\nis another 3+ inches. The door is the perfect size for the Vampyre Hunter or kits around 1/6\nscale. Casting was excellent. No bubbles, mold-offset or pin-holes. It is comprised of the door\nand wall, base, pillars, and gargoyles. Not much to speak of as far as assembly.  Be sure to pin\nthe door to the base for added stability. For painting, I used the photo on the box. It was\nextremely helpful. I base coated the door with Dark Brown, then followed it with light and dark\nwashes and dry brushing. The hinges were base coated with black, dry brushed with copper,\nthen washed with turquoise then caramel brown.  Once completed, I glued the pins to the\nVampyre Hunter and glued him to the base.\n\n<p>\nOverall I think the Vampyre Hunter is an excellent kit. As I have mentioned, attention to\ndetail abounds making this kit very desirable. Assembly is easy and straight forward. Just\nfollowing the instructions produces an excellent finished kit. The Gothic Door is an excellent\ncompanion to the Vampyre Hunter making for a very nice piece.'),(43,201,'1998-01-23 08:42:33',4321,'rez','<b><img src=\"../garage/letter/m.gif\" alt=\"M\" WIDTH=\"30\" HEIGHT=\"30\">alefactor Studio</b>\nblasts into the garage kit scene with their first release <b>Rez Bannon</b>!\n<p>\nIt\'s the classic garage kit tale:\nsculptor does some pieces for fun, friends encourage him to make kits,\na new kit company is born, and kit builders rejoice!\n<p>\nI think the sculpting on this piece is great!  The likeness is there\nand the pose and expression really come across.  The base, which is\na pile of bullet cases, is imaginative and adds a lot to the kit.\nEven the shirt style is right.\n<p>\nThe kit comes in a box with a photo of the finished kit on the cover.\nInside the box the kit is well protected with foam packing.  Also included are\n&quot;D-Structions&quot; that walk you through the basics of putting together\na resin kit.  Finally, there is a reader response card!  Very few\nkit companies include these and I applaud those that do.  Anything\nthat encourages people to contact kit companies directly with their\ncomments will help out both the kit builder and the kit company.\n<p>\nRez comes in five white resin parts: left hand and gun, right hand\nand gun, head, body, and base.  The parts were obviously planned\nout so that they could all be painted separately and then assembled\nafter painting.  Thank you Malefactor Studio!\n<p>\nThe mold seams were pretty minor and were cleaned up by scraping\nan X-Acto blade over the seam perpendicular to the surface.  Then\na quick pass with fine sandpaper finished them up.\nThere is a large round plug on the lower back of the head and\non the back of one hand.  I sawed them off with an X-Acto saw,\nthen used a medium file to remove the remaining excess resin\nand make the area even with the surrounding surface.  Sandpaper\nwas then used to finish smoothing out the area.  Since these\nareas don\'t have a texture to them this was a pretty simple process.\n<p>\nA couple of areas had small surface air bubbles.  There were also some\nmedium sized air bubbles on the back of the head where the plug\nwas removed.  To fix up these areas I mixed Squadron White Putty\nwith a bit of Acetone (nail polish remover is mostly Acetone and\nworks fine).  Then I used a Q-Tip to swab this thinned putty into\nthe small holes and to build up some putty over the areas.  Next\nwith a clean Q-Tip dipped in Acetone I smoothed out the putty.\nA little sanding later and no more air bubbles.\n<p>\nI always pin my kits and this one was no exception.  I snipped\nsome wire from a metal coat hanger to make the pins.  Then I put\na drip of paint in the center of two parts that will be joined\nand touch them together.  The paint then indicates where to drill.\nI drill a slightly larger hole than the size of the rod so I can\nadjust things a bit.  Rods are then glued into the hands, head,\nand feet.  Finally, I drilled holes into a block of wood and\ninserted the parts with the rods into the holes to support them\nwhile I prime and paint.\n<p>\nNow Rez is ready to paint!  I\'ll have my build up in the gallery\nsoon...\n<p>\nIn conclusion, this is a great first release and at an excellent price.\nLet\'s just hope that they turn this kit into a complete lineup!\n<p>\nYou can order Rez Bannon from:\n<dl><dd>\n<table width=\"544\"><tr>\n<td>\n<a href=\"../garage/companies.html#Malefactor Studio\"><img src=\"../malefactor/card.jpg\" width=\"265\" height=\"144\" border=\"0\"></a><br>\n</td>\n<td>\n<a href=\"../garage/companies.html#Malefactor Studio\"><b>Malefactor Studio</b></a><br>\n1036 Maple Blvd.<br>\nMonroe, MI 48162<br>\nPhone: (313) 243-7889<br>\nE-mail: <a href=\"mailto:race@tdi.net\">race@tdi.net</a><br>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n</dl>'),(44,330,'1998-01-23 08:46:49',3613,'devilman','<p>Wondering what to review next for my readers herein, I decided to take us on a little trip to Japan. </p>\n<p>First up, Devilman is a superhero of adult oriented Japanese animated movies. I\'ll leave this to you to\nresearch. Devilman, or Amon, has been clone countless times by many companies in various media. So\nwhy this particular kit herein? </p>\n<p>First, when you talk about quality vinyl kits from Japan, the leader of the pack, I.M.H.O., is Max Factory.\nThey produce vinyl kits that have an astonishing fit, no air bubbles and use a sturdier vinyl than most others.\nIn fact, when the esteemed U.S. company, Geometric, decided to switch from resin kits to vinyl (this was\ndone so they could produce many more kits from an unlimited mold life, run at better prices to us buyers)\nwho did Geometric choose to produce for them? Max Factory! </p>\n<p>Also while Akira Fudo is a demon hunter, he takes on powers (and possibly some physical traits of?)\ndemons he kills. We can therefore reckon Akira/Amon as indulging in slightly different looks from time to\ntime... the upshot being that this kit may not look like &quot;classic&quot; Devilman, but it\'s him all the way! Killer\nsculpting job by Takauki San! </p>\n<p>While there are many pieces to this kit, even with all Japanese instructions a novice vinyl builder can figure\nit all out. My only advice is to fill legs to thigh level with plaster. This is a free standing piece and this will\ngive the piece a much more secure weight. The other hint is to leave the antennae off during painting, and\nglue them on as your last step, prior to final spray. </p>\n<p>This is one of those rare kits that defines the word &quot;perfection&quot; from Alpha to Omega all the way. Even the\nmost critical of kit builders may wish to give this one a second or third take as that special &quot;coffee table&quot;\ndisplay piece. </p>'),(45,270,'1998-01-23 08:50:49',3661,'','<p>\n<b>Note:</b>\n<i>The following is a fairly descriptive review of a &quot;gore&quot; kit.  If\nyou are not interested in such kits, you may well wish to stop reading at\nthis point.</i>\n\n<p>\n<img src=\"../garage/letter/i.gif\" alt=\"I\" WIDTH=\"11\" HEIGHT=\"29\">\'m a big Hellraiser fan, and for a long time, I was hoping Screamin\'\nwould do a kit of my favorite Cenobite, Dr. Channard from Hellraiser 2.  Last\nSpring, Netherworld fulfilled that desire by releasing a 1:6 scale resin kit\nof the &quot;good&quot; doctor.  However, not content to simply sculpt the figure, Paul\nKomoda has gone above and beyond by integrating the figure with an elaborate\nbase.\n\n<p>\n   If you\'ve not seen the film, then a textual description of Channard will\nmost likely leave you confused, if not a little repulsed.  Nevertheless, the\nbasic  character design, is standard Cenobite fare: leather body suit, strips\nof loose flesh hanging off him, and piano wire pulled taunt into his face.\n Channard\'s hands have been gashed open, and a trio of tentacles protrudes\nfrom each palm, brandishing a nasty instrument of torture.  Since the\nhand/tentacle assemblies are cast in white metal, they may be reposed as\ndesired.\n\n<p>\n   The nicely textured leather suit is festooned with chain links, ties, and\nbuckles which accent it nicely.  Even the soles of Channard\'s shoes are\ndetailed with the trademark puzzle box pattern.  (Read on to discover why\ndetailing the BOTTOM of the figure\'s shoes was not a wasted effort.)\n Channard\'s spinal column is also exposed, with a few buckles holding the\nleather suit in place around the wound.  The buckles and vertebrae represent\na nice bit of casting, as the buckles are delicate and the undercutting is\nsevere.\n\n<p>\n   The likeness to actor Kenneth Cranham is well done, and the facial\nexpression is as good as it gets.  The howling sneer looks like it was\nplucked right off of the screen, and goes a long way towards making Channard\nlook like the malevolent nasty that he is.\n\n<p>\n   So far, I\'ve not mentioned the base, an INTEGRAL part of this kit.  In the\nfilm, shortly after Channard\'s &quot;recreation&quot; as a Cenobite, a giant purple\nworm grips Channard\'s head in it\'s maw.  Presumably, the worm drills into his\nbrain, directly linking Channard to Hell\'s lord, Leviathan.  The worm carries\nChannard around for the rest of the movie, and it makes for a disturbing\nimage on the screen.  Like the film, the Netherworld also features the worm,\nfrom which Channard is suspended over the base.  The entire piece measures\n18&quot; tall when assembled.\n\n<p>\n   The base itself measures 11&quot;x11&quot; and is a large relief of the &quot;famous&quot;\nHellraiser puzzle box.  The center region is occupied by a skinned man (or\nwoman) in a fetal position.  Four relief designs depicting the other\nCenobites, Leviathan, and people being mutilated surround the four edges of\nthe base.  The worm itself is far more detailed than his movie counterpart.\n Komoda\'s let his imagination run rampant, and the worm in the kit is a\nphallic monstrosity, festooned with vertebrae, boney protrusions, veins,\nscrotum-like sacs, other assorted protrusions.  (Quite honestly, the worm\ndidn\'t seem nearly as disgusting until I started painting it!)\n\n<p>\n   All of the kit parts are well keyed, and cleaned up with a minimal amount\nof fuss, with the exception of the two pieces which comprise the base.  The\nbase is cast in two large pieces due to it\'s size, and was separated into two\ntriangular regions.  One of my base halves was fairly badly warped, and while\nthe base parts mated well at the corners, there was easily a 1/8&quot; mismatch\nnear the center.  A good deal of grinding and putty work was required to\nblend the two halves of the base together.  This unpleasant task represented\nthe majority of the effort required to assemble the kit.  Some putty work was\nrequired around the figure\'s shoulders and to fill in small voids and\npinholes that didn\'t cast cleanly.  Pinning is a must in this kit, due to the\nstructural load that is supported by the worm.\n\n<p>\n   Channard was supposedly limited to sale at the Chiller \'95 Spring show for\n$170, however\n<a href=\"../garage/companies.html#Puff N\' Stuff\"><b>Puff N\' Stuff</b></a>\nand\n<a href=\"../garage/companies.html#Monsters In Motion\"><b>Monsters In Motion</b></a>\nboth have been stocking\nthe kit.  I recommend it to Hellraiser fans in particular, with my only\nreservation being the difficulty in assembling the base properly.  It should\nbe clear from the text of this review that this kit, like Netherworld\'s\n&quot;Julia&quot; companion piece, are NOT for the squeamish.  It is a graphic\nsculpture that should have great appeal to collectors of &quot;gore&quot; kits.\n'),(46,270,'1998-01-23 08:52:30',3665,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/n.gif\" alt=\"N\" WIDTH=\"31\" HEIGHT=\"32\">eil Andrythal\nand I first &quot;met&quot; in the &quot;Resinhead Ball&quot; section of Terry Webb\'s\n<a href=\"../garage/books.html#The Garage Kit That Ate My Wallet\">Revenge\nof the Garage Kit That Ate My Wallet</a>.  Readers of the book\nwill recall that chapter as a hodge podge of miscellaneous garage kit related\nphotos culled from shows, impromptu photo shoots, and just plain lunacy.  One\nset of photos always struck me as a standout in that section - Mark Newman\'s\nNeil Andrythal, a raging gorilla-like beast standing atop a rocky outcropping\nand brandishing a large club.  I don\'t recall ever seeing the word &quot;majestic&quot;\napplied to a garage kit sculpture, well, I did once, but it wasn\'t deserved\nIMO.  In the case of those three photos the word certainly seemed to suit.\n\n<p>\n   Neil is now available from Newmanoid models as a numbered limited edition\nresin kit, and I\'m happy to say that he\'s as imposing as I remember him in\nthe photos.  The kit is roughly 1:6 scale, and stands right at 10 inches once\nassembled.  All of the parts come <b>well</b> packed with a signed and numbered\ncertificate of authenticity.  No instructions or color reference photos are\nprovided.  While assembly is intuitive, some sort of base color scheme would\nbe appreciated.  Should he be done in Caucasian fleshtones?  Browns?  Greys?\n While there\'s no &quot;right&quot; answer here, a color photo might help individuals\nin making the &quot;tough choice.&quot;\n\n<p>\n   Each of the kit\'s 5 pieces is cast in a rather dense white resin that I\'d\nalmost mistake for cold cast porcelain impregnated resin.  The casting\nquality is certainly consistent with results that can be achieved with that\nmixture.  Detail in the skin and hair texture has all pulled cleanly from the\nmolds.  Attention to mold allignment and perhaps some additional finishing\nwork results in minimal seams and no offset.  The only visible air bubbles in\nthe pieces were in the head on the lower jaw and near the key.  Some\nsubsurface voids (honeycombs) revealed themselves during clean up of the hand\nand face.  Typical problem areas such as the teeth and ears are flawless.\n Overall, a first rate job on the castings.\n\n<p>\n   All of the parts are well keyed, and fit precisely.  Neil drops neatly\ndown onto the base and is keyed via plugs cast in the feet as well as with\nknuckle and toe depressions.  Breaklines on the figure occur at the biceps\nand around the neck in areas with little to no hair detailing.  This makes\nputtying and seaming the parts a snap, since the putty can be blended with\nsimple sanding.\n\n<p>\n   Sculpting detail and the overall presentation are where Neil really\n&quot;shines.&quot;  Sculptor Shawn Nagle is often praised for his dynamic poses.\n Nagle has <b>NOTHING</b> on Newman.  Neil is caught in mid stride, muscles bulging\nas he raises his spiked club.  Neil\'s skeletal and muscle structures are\nquite believable, although I feel that the neck seems a little exaggerated\nwhen viewed at certain angles.\n\n<p>\n   Tendons, veins, and muscles stand out in the arms, legs, and neck adding\nto the credibility of the pose.  Fur and loincloth details are delicately\nsculpted, not simply blocked in.  The spiked club was most likely whittled\nfrom a real stick, so it\'s detailing is perfect.  The rocky base is fairly\nplain, but completely adequate as the figure is meant to be the focus of\nattention.  Facial detailing adds a great deal to the piece, conveying a\ngenuine feeling of rage or possibly annoyance.  (I suspect that Neil\'s range\nof expression might be limited!)\n\n<p>\n   In a hobby glutted with Predators, Aliens, and the like, Neil Andrythal is\na refreshingly original sculpture.  Neil is being released in a limited\nedition of 300 numbered kits and retails for $130, easily on par with other\nkits in this scale.  I strongly suspect that those who opt for this &quot;change\nof pace&quot; will be delighted with a unique and well made sculpture.'),(47,262,'1998-01-23 08:56:09',3670,'bunny','<img src=\"../garage/letter/h.gif\" alt=\"H\" WIDTH=\"25\" HEIGHT=\"30\">ello Gremlins!\nToday\'s Anime Model Review is P-unit\'s Bunny Girl, a\nJapanese model kit based on the archetypical Playboy Bunny.  All the\nelements are there - leotard, shoes, collars and cuff, and, of course,\nthe bunny ears and tail.\n\n<p>\nTaking this kit out of the kanji-covered box, I see that fifteen white\nresin pieces make up this kit, with only a few small parts.  The\nsculpting of Bunny-chan is excellent, with fine detail and clear, easy\nassembly.  The proportions are far off from regular human anatomy,\nhowever - this is an anime kit, after all, and in the anime style the\nfigure, legs and eyes are emphasized.  Bunny-chan has quite the figure,\nindeed!\n\n<p>\nMost features of this kit show great detail.  However, the sculptor\ntook a vacation with Bunny-chan\'s hair.  A massive blob of hair sits\nbehind her head like a Hefty bag slung over her shoulder.  This is\ndisappointing, as most anime hair is quite well done with numerous\npieces and, in comparison, this hair looks lifeless.  Another poor\nchoice of the sculptor\'s was to have the large, expressive eyes covered\nfirst by the bangs of the hair and then by the kit\'s hand, though this\ncould be an attempt by the sculptor to further emphasize the eyes by\nhiding them.\n\n<p>\nThe casting is very well done, as I\'ve come to expect from the\nprofessional Japanese model companies and their expertise at resin\nmodeling.   All parts are joined at natural seams - the legs join at the\nedge of the leotard, for example, and the head and body are cast as one\npiece.  A mold line that runs around the edge of the main body and legs,\nbut this isn\'t difficult to remove.  The rest of the kit requires little\ncleaning.  Small defects are uncommon on this kit; the only place I\nfound pinholes was on the shoulder.\n\n<p>\nAssembling this kit wasn\'t a challenge.  The instructions are all\nwritten in Japanese, but the kit is a very simple assembly and goes\ntogether with little effort.  Numerous pictures in the instructions help\nthe modeler to be certain where each of the few small parts fit.\n\n<p>\nEven though there are tabs cast into the kit, to support the kit -\nespecially the chunk of hair - and facilitate painting later,  I decided\nto pin this kit during pre-assembly.  The arms and legs were pinned to\nthe main body, as was the hair.  All other pieces were small enough to\nsupport themselves with glue.  The kit was then disassembled for\npainting.\n\n<p>\nPainting on this kit is relatively straightforward, with one\nexception.   The only difficult part is the kit\'s legs, perhaps the\nhardest parts I\'ve ever had to paint.  The kit as shown on the box wears\na pair of pantyhose, with subtle shading to mimic the stretching\npattern.  Rather than try and duplicate this difficult technique, I\nchose to instead paint the legs as a heavier pair of tights.\n\n<p>\nTo paint the legs, I first painted them with flesh tone of the rest of\nthe body.  After that I used a transparent black paint over the legs,\nairbrushing this on very lightly until  the desired color is reached.\nCare must be taken - it is very easy to add just a bit too much, and it\nis far harder to lighten the legs than to darken them.  Patience!  If\nyou wish, the legs can be painted a straight flesh tone and the\nstockings ignored.\n\n<p>\nOverall, Bunny-chan is a cute model kit that assembles easily and is\neasy to paint - just remember not to get frustrated over the legs, and\ntake them at your own skill level.  The kit is a good introduction to\nanime models; a transition from the American female kits like Vampirella\nand Babylon Beauty to the more stylized Japanese anime figures.  The\nprice is high at $150, but is reasonable for an imported kit of this\nsize.\n\n<p>\nHave fun painting the eyes!'),(48,305,'1998-01-23 09:04:11',4322,'kothoga','<p>\n<b><img src=\"../garage/letter/h.gif\" alt=\"H\" WIDTH=\"25\" HEIGHT=\"30\"></b>ello\n   fellow Grems!  I\'m here to give a review on the latest Revell- Monogram\n  vinyl release of the Kothoga creature.  I can\'t believe\n  it was finally released!  After months of hype, the critter is\n  finally here.  The Kothoga is the bad guy from the movie &quot;The Relic&quot;,\n  and is a genetic mix of Horse, Reptile and Mammal.  This leads to a\n  very interesting look for the creature  The kit comes in 11 parts,\n  and is done in sturdy gray vinyl.  The pose is animated, with the\n  creature appearing to be looking over his left shoulder at some\n  tasty morsel ( Those who have seen the movie know what I mean).\n  My only complaint is the mouth is sculpted closed.  I don\'t\n  remember ever seeing the creature with it\'s mouth closed.  I\n  decided to open mine.  This model looks good standing flat-footed,\n  or possibly hanging off a wall.  Construction is straight forward\n  for vinyl, but will require careful putty work on a few areas,\n  notably the head /neck area, the foot/ankle join, and the tail.\n  The tail is a tricky fit, and doesn\'t mate to the body very well.\n  This is probably the worst part of the kit, IMO, and will require\n  extensive putty work to fair it into the rest of the body.  The\n  tail also has a strange seam line running down both sides of the\n  tail, and will require you to re-sculpt skin lines if you decide\n  to fill it in.   Revell/Monogram also chose to stamp their logo\n  on the underside of the tail in a conspicuous square block.  This\n  is not evident in the box art, but will stick out on the finished kit.\n  I shaved mine off and re-puttied.  Detail is good, but there are a few\n  discrepancies.  The hair of the beast starts short of the movie version.\n  The hair on the kit starts right about the shoulder, but should begin right\n  behind the head &quot;spider&quot; and slope up from there.\n  <p>\n    Overall, I think the kit is a winner.  I\'m also glad to see the &quot;Big\n  Boys&quot; wading into the figure part of the Hobby.  This should\n  definitely help the modeler on a budget, as the Kothoga is affordably\n  priced at around $30.'),(49,4875,'1998-01-24 12:18:12',3920,'cylena','<p>\nCylena Queen of the Night is a solid resin porcelain kit. This is\nthe first in a line of Screamin\' Queens. She comes with her own story written by Jeff Brower,\nwho is also the sculptor. Although Cylena requires no modeling and may be left unpainted for a\nnatural porcelain look, we chose to paint our review copy with a pale, dead-flesh look. The\ncolor reference photo that comes with the kit shows her painted with a warm medium fleshtone,\nand jet black hair and cape. You could also patina her in a faux bronze finish or paint her as a\nred-head, or whatever you choose. There were no seams to speak of and we didn\'t need to do\nany sanding on this review copy. It\'s nice to see a kit that requires no clean-up work strait out of\nthe box. We just washed it with warm water and dish soap to get rid of any mold release or oils\nfrom handing. After thoroughly drying, several light coats of Floquil brand base white (no.\n330021) were applied and left for twenty four hours to dry completely. Then a pale flesh color\nwas created using raw sienna, burnt sienna, and white. This was considered the master flesh tone\nand a portion was put in a separate jar and set aside for later use. Next we used an airbrush to\nbase coat the figure. A small amount of blue was added to the master flesh tone to give a deathly\nlook. This was applied to all exposed skin. Next the airbrush was cleaned and lighter versions of\nthe color were mixed adding white and flesh tone back to the bluish hue. The highlights were\nsprayed on the raised areas of the body to add more dimension to the flesh tone. When dry, the\nkit was protected with a clear coat. The hair and cape were then coated with some blue and\npurple, avoiding the edge that bordered the skin.</p>\n<p>\nNext, the flesh of the figure was coated with liquid latex, including the face and legs. When dry,\nthe latex masks the flesh tone and protects it from smudges that can occur during handling. The\nhair and cape were coated again with layers of purple and blue. Some magenta was added to\nbrighten up the tones. The boots were underpainted red and then washed with a thin layer of\nblack. We used a translucent black airbrush paint, applied with a soft sable brush, to glaze the\nhair. The black pooled in the deepest crevices and slid off the raised areas. At this point the cape\nwas predominantly purple and the hair was dark blue. The latex was peeled off and both 5/0 and\n10/0 brushes were used to apply dark color to clean up the edges of the robe and hair. Some\nbled onto the flesh, but that was temporary. The figure was coated again with a clear protective\nspray. The latex was then reapplied to the flesh to protect the figure while final highlights were\napplied to the hair and cape. (It is better to peel the latex masking off the kit and the end of the\nevening and reapply it the next day. Otherwise, the latex practically cements itself to the figure\nafter just a couple of days of neglect.) The highlights of the hair and robe were done in two steps:\na medium highlight using nearly pure blue pigment on the hair with just a touch of purple to match\nit to her robe. Then, a little white was added to the medium highlight and it was dry-brushed\nagain. This was done using a very soft blunt edged brush. We dipped it in the color then wiped\nnearly all the paint off before gently stroking the raised areas, depositing just the right amount of\ncolor. We erred on the side of not enough highlights rather than go too light and render the\nmodel garish. Finally, a touch of translucent pearlescent white was dabbed onto certain highlights\nto give a nice effect to the robe and make it sparkle a bit. Then, the latex was peeled off the the flesh tone again and\na protective coating was applied to the robe and hair.</p>\n<p>\nTo finish the flesh tone, we chose some Windsor Newton inks that contained shellac. The colors\nwere translucent and because of the shellac they did not mix with the previous layers and muddy\nthe result. The layers were applied first using a thin wash of yellow, then a thin wash of aqua-\nblue, then a thin wash of yellow-orange, then a thin wash of purple-blue, followed by a spare\napplication of magenta, then pure blue, then a basic purple (using 50% blue and 50% red).\nWhen painting these washes, first a translucent line of color is applied to the desired area. Then\nthe brush is rinsed in water and dabbed on a towel, and the edge of the wash is blended, using\nthe moisture remaining on the brush, outward until the line of the wash is &quot;erased.&quot; Then, there is\nno sudden jump from the color to the flesh tone. Once the last layer was completely dry, we\nmixed a little bit of flesh tone, some yellow Windsor Newton ink and water. This made a very\nslightly opaque layer which was brushed over the entire body. Now the layers of color did\nappear to be beneath the skin. The effect is subtle but highly effective. The layering of warm and\ncool colors in a skin tone is a method that Nina uses to do all her two dimensional portraits. It\nwas very gratifying to see how well the technique works on a 3-d model. The final look\nreminded us of actual color photos that we\'d seen of a morgue. Dead flesh is not at all grey\n(that\'s just a Hollywood look). In reality it is yellow, purple, blue, etc.</p>\n<p>\nFinally, we finished the eyes using the method outlined in David Fischer\'s Model Mania\nvideo, Volume 2. By the way, David\'s new video, Volume 3, has just been released. We\'re\nworking on a review of that now.</p>\n<p>\nThe last thing we did was complete the ornaments that encircle the figure\'s legs. These were first\npainted black. Then we dry-brushed metallic colors in the following order: bronze, copper, gold,\nand pewter. This gave the ornaments the look of an alloy instead of say pure gold or pure silver.\nThe overall effect was very warm and pleasing. Cylena finally coated with several doses of\nFloquil Figure Flat to protect her. Now she\'s ready for the display case! (We entered this kit in\nthe Mad Model Party model kit contest and were pleased to receive many compliments on it!).</p>\n<p>\nThis kit was a pure pleasure to paint. It is a great starter kit for those of you who want to start\npainting your own figures, but aren\'t ready to tackle the complexity of assembling a kit from\npieces. Jeff Brower has done a great job of sculpting a female who is both beautiful and realistic.\nIn closing, on a scale of 1-10, we rate this kit a 9.9. Screamin\'\'\'\' has a real winner with this kit.\nIt\'s a great value for the money. We highly recommend it.</p>\n\n-Ed Martinez &amp; Nina Kempf 1997'),(50,330,'1998-01-24 14:01:28',4323,'channard','<font size=\"+1\"><b>\"Surgery is Open!\"</b></font>\nYes, collectors, that amazing doctor in\nsearch of the unlimited knowledge of the maze of the mind is now yours to proudly\ndisplay with your other four (six if you got \'em all) Cenobites.\n<p>\nFirst of all,\n<a href=\"../ed_martinez/richard_hammecher_interview.html\"><b>Richard Hammecher</b></a>\nof Screamin\' told me prior to sending\nthis review kit, that it was harder to do than other Screamin\'s.  I didn\'t\nactually find this to be so.  He has more pieces (seven in vinyl and six in resin)\nthan most Screamin\' kits and it requires more plaster filling and painting time than\nthe others.  But the actual level of difficulty is in keeping with Screamin\'s\nreputation... EASY!  What I think Richard meant to relate is that it simply takes\nmore time to construct.  Fair enough, as we have a 23\" tall work which is well\nworth the few extra dollars.\n<p>\nFirst of all, the sculpting job is fantastic!  I\'m an absolute fan of the Hellraiser\nmythos and as much as I adore all of Screamin\'s Cenobite kits, Channard and Chatterer\nare now both in a tie for perfection.  Tiny errors are in both, but not even anything\nClive Barker would notice!\n<p>\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/steve_goodrich/channard_side.jpg\" width=\"205\" height=\"288\" hspace=\"8\" vspace=\"8\" align=\"left\">\nThe kit is so well designed, it even fooled a producer friend of mine when he saw\nit at the Oct. chiller show.  He said it would never survive in heat.  So convincing is the\nillusion of the \"tentacle\" holding the doctor aloft over the base, it even faked out an\nexpert!  The trick is that the \"tentacle\" is actually sculpted with a tree-like root\nsystem on the Laiment configuration \"lift\" base.  This, of course, is artistic licensing\nas the \"tentacle\" (so called in this kit)\nis hundreds of yards long and those being in the \"Order of the Gash\" will understand\nthe \"WURM\" to be just that.  The heel of Channard\'s left foot has a cube which fits\ninto the \"roots\"of the tentacle.  Filling Channard\'s left leg, the lower \"tentacle\"\nconstruction and base with plaster assures a solid vinyl piece that will not sag in any way.\n<p>\nYou will need to paint Channard and the base / WURM construction and then assemble as\na final step.\nThe only putty joints are over the smooth surface of the WURM.\n<p>\nNow, the six \"snakes,\" again as the instructions call them, are resin which are devoid\nof air bubbles.  A little sanding of minimal mold lines is required.\nBut you must approach them with a plan.  You are provided with a sheet of mylar in\nthe kit and template or pattern to trace six blades which vomit from the \"mouths\" of the\n\"snakes.\"   These are the amputation knives shown in the movie when Channard makes his\nfinal entry to his institute.  But notice these are on the right hand only.  Throughout\nChannard\'s scenes, before he temporarily looses his head, the six snakes spew forth a\nnumber of amazing devices.  These include an eyeball, a white flower, a beckoning finger,\na piece of dentures, a pair of scissors, an evisceration \"drill,\" scalpel blade, steel\n\"sea urchin\" like device etc..  Freeze frame any 4 head VCR to see these all!\nNaturally, production of this kit needs to go for an ideal goal to keep the cost down.\nBut any true Hellraisers or kitbashers will have a field day improvising on the snakes.\n<p>\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/steve_goodrich/channard_face.jpg\" width=\"144\" height=\"216\" hspace=\"8\" vspace=\"8\" align=\"right\">\nThe last piece of the kit is a thin wire to secure around the center of the face.\nWhile the other fine wires are sculpted into the head, I at first wondered, \"Why only one?\"\nWell, it\'s because the central wire covers the bridge of the nose.  To sculpt a solid fill\non either side of the nose would be sad news.  Brower opted to actually have the wire\ngap over nose to cheek area.  BRAVO!\n<p>\nThe plastic is all black in this kit.  This meaning you only \"fix\" the surface with\nspray and forget black base coating on it - just go to work on Channard\'s unique bluish\ncomplexion!\n<p>\nAny fan of the Hellraiser saga: I can\'t possibly tell you how much you need this kit...\npictures won\'t do justice to this gigantic artwork... you have to see, you have to know!\nMost highly recommended by a Cenobite in training!\n\n<br clear=\"all\">'),(51,330,'1998-01-24 14:02:51',4309,'myers','This review copy is one of the pre-release specials. Only 250 of the special edition kits are to be\nsold so if you are one who snaps up these Screamin\' collectors issues, it would be best to call\nbefore sending in the order to inquire about availability. (Screamin\'s phone number is (518)\n432-4582.)\n<p>\nWhat sets this kit apart from the subsequent standard issue is twofold. First is that it is cast in\nblazing Halloween orange. Second is that it comes with the Judith Myers grave stone as seen in\nthe cult movie classic. This latter is cast in Screamin\'s own unique plaster formula. This is an\nespecially tough plaster which, in coloring and surface texture, replicates a real grave stone.\nOstensibly, it needs no clean up or painting. Once the standard issue of this kit comes out, the\ngrave stone will have to be purchased by itself for $15.95. At $70.00 (plus $5.00 for s & h) for\nthis pre-release issue you save this $15.95.\n<p>\nSo why did it take Screamin\' so long to put out their all time most requested kit? According to\nScreamin\'s president,\n<a href=\"../ed_martinez/richard_hammecher_interview.html\">Richard Hammecher</a>,\nthe license was a bear to get hold of! Well, be that as it\nmay, he\'s now out and ready to thrill you this Halloween.\n<p>\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/steve_goodrich/myers_face.jpg\" width=\"93\" height=\"144\" hspace=\"8\" vspace=\"8\" align=\"left\">\nThe model itself comes in three vinyl pieces and a mylar knife blade. A fourth vinyl piece\nreplicates a large Jack-o\'-lantern seen on the movie poster. In 1/4 scale, the piece is quite well\nsculpted by Joe Laudati who had access to the original Don Post issue of the \"Shape\'s\" mask and\nthe J.C. Penney\'s coverall suit. The cuts are super easy to make and there are no air pockets or\nbubbles to fill. (These latter plagued many earlier vinyl kits.) In assembly, if one uses the \"warm\n& squish\" technique¹, the joints need no putty work as the left arm cut is exactly on the seam line\nof the coveralls and the waist cut is exactly above the elastic band. This is especially important a\nfeature where putty would kill the effect of the orange pre-release kit should one wish to leave it\nas is. All in all, I honestly have to say this is Screamin\'s easiest to build piece yet.\n<p>\nNow, I have a small problem with this one. I\'ll grant you - clothing worn under an overall suit\nand rubber mask worn over the head bulk out a guy considerably. This is compensated for in\nJoe\'s sculpting. Naturally, human sized hands would appear smaller than normal. But these hands\nare sculpted a tad too small on top of this. But... once painted, the flesh hands being lighter than\nthe dark coveralls, create the illusion of being larger! Initially, I\'d convinced myself that I\'d\neventually sculpt new hands for Mr. Myers. Well, once painted, the model works for me, and I\'m\npretty darn passionate about this character. So this point should not dissuade ones consideration\nto enjoy the finer points involved. We finally can have the King of Slashers on our shelf... who\'s\ncomplaining here?!\nScreamin Gets another robust thumbs up from this reviewer!\n<p>\n¹<i>This technique is to, for example, leave the upper body cool while heating the joint area of the\nleft arm with a hairdryer. Glue is applied to the rim of the body joint and the softened arm piece is\n\"squished\" into place. This causes an airtight solid seal. As well, the heated plastic will \"kick\"\nthe super glue to form a more solid joint. If you\'ve never used this technique before, it\'s best to\ndo a practice run without the glue to get used to how it feels.</i>\n<p><hr size=\"1\" width=\"50%\"><p>\n<center><b>Addendum</b></center>\n<p>\nOf special interest to those who may wish to embellish their Myers kit further than Screamin\' has\nsuggested, I present the following:\n<p>\n<a name=\"Coloring\"><b>Coloring</b></a><br>\nIn the heated momentum of getting a prototype painted for deadlines, a company may\nnot always have time to full research coloring. For the record, Michael Myer\'s undershirt should\nbe painted navy blue and not white. The hair is, on the original mask, very close to red.\nAccomplish this by basecoating hair dark or chocolate brown. Then drybrush with a medium\nbrown. Finally use \"rust\" or \"red-brown\" to lightly drybrush the high spots of hair. Also, its\nimportant that the masks eye brows be painted to match the hair! The mask should be painted\nivory, then drybrushed in white. Lastly, lightly chalk in creases in light blue and chalk deep recess\nin lips and ears in medium blue. Blend with white chalk.\n<p>\nEyes and ocular orbits should be painted the same flesh used on hands and neck gap area of skin.\nColor eyes brown as you would normally paint eyes. Last step is to finely chalk inner mask eye\nsocket ridges in black, and flesh area about the eyeball deep gray. This will make the eyes look\nlike black holes from a distance, yet staring at you viewed close up.\n<p>\n<a name=\"Knife\"><b>Knife</b></a><br>\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/steve_goodrich/myers_a.gif\" width=\"216\" height=\"96\" align=\"right\" hspace=\"8\" vspace=\"8\">\nThe Knife: While a kitchen knife is all well and good, no knife has the mirror like sheen of mylar.\nTo enhance the knife of your model, (seen in finished kit photo in this review), I\'ve scaled a\n\"Chicago Cutlery\" chefs knife down to 1/4 scale giving the blade correct curves. Take figure \"A\"\nand trace it onto thin, clear plastic. An old cassette box works well. Clip it out roughly with wire\ncutters. Then with a sanding drum on your Dremel tool, on slow speed, \"carve\" the plastic to your\ntracing marks. (A fine point magic marker should be used for this.)\nFinally, you can finesse the blade with a fine file. (You can sharpen the cutting edge as sharp as\nyou dare!) The tiny point side of this blade\'s handle edge is to be inserted into the knife hilt. Paint\nthe blade with spray, matte enamel paint to prime it and brush on silver before inserting it and\nbrush on silver before inserting.\n<p>\n<i>\nEditors Note: The knife image on this page is <b>not to scale</b>\ndue to variations in screen resolutions and browser printing methods.\nIf you would like a scale diagram please send a self addressed stamped\nenvelope to <a href=\"index.html\"><b>Steve Goodrich</b></a>. - Thanks!\n</i>\n<p>\n<a name=\"Stance\"><b>Stance</b></a><br>\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/steve_goodrich/myers_b.jpg\" width=\"199\" height=\"216\" align=\"right\" hspace=\"8\" vspace=\"8\">\nYour Michael Myers will look much more pensive and poised to strike if you widen the stance of\nhis legs. This will also make the model stand more securely.\nTo do this, cut out the hook portion of a coat hanger. Straighten out the wire. Then, fold it in\ntwo. Next, using pliers, \"twirl\" ends around each other to hold this \"V\" shape. Now, blast the\nlegs of your model (of course... before assembly) with a hairdryer to relax the vinyl and let it\nregain it\'s pre-warped \"memory\". Bend the \"V\" wire considerably wider than the models natural\nstance. Then, bend ends of the \"V\" inward to assure they do not puncture the vinyl when they\nforce the legs apart. (See fig. \"B\"). Next, insert the \"V\" into legs of model and pull legs apart\nwith wire inserted to desired stance. (see photo of finished kit.) Lastly, making sure the apex of\nthe wire \"V\" sets securely on the top of inside crotch? fill legs with plaster. The ideal level is up to\nthe crotch in each leg, but not covering the apex of the wire \"V\". This will allow you to pull the\nlegs wider, or push them closer together to get your final pose once the plaster is dry. As well,\nthe wire \"V\" will also anchor to the plaster and give you a much more secure model\n<p>\nA final step might be to sit back and pat yourself on the back and say, \"hey, I\'m an official kit\nbasher now and look at what I made!\".\n<p>\n<i>Enjoy the hobby... it\'s the best in the world!!!</i>'),(52,208,'1998-01-24 12:29:41',3731,'pinhead','<b><img src=../garage/letter/t.gif alt=\"T\"></b>his\n\n     kit is over 12\", tall and includes chain and cord, pins, and a resin skull, hook, and heart.\n    This is the third Pinhead kit released from Screamin\' and it is the first one in 1/6 scale\n    (the first two were both 1/4 scale.).  There are only five vinyl parts and a few resin\n    and metal accessories, so it is a very easy kit to build.  It would make a good first kit\n    for a novice modeler.  As with any vinyl kit it is always important to warm the vinyl\n    before cutting.  I find it also helpful to take a \"sharpie\" marking pen and\n    draw a line where I want to cut.  I generally start with a nice fresh sharp exacto\n    blade, cutting away from myself.  It\'s very easy to slip and cut yourself if your\n    not careful.  I cut away the majority of the plastic first then go back and slowly and\n    more carefully trim away the last bits of vinyl and test fit the parts.  It is a good\n    idea to fill vinyl kit parts with something;  newspaper, tinfoil, or plaster works.  I chose\n    rigid poly urethane two-part foam.  This will prevent the kit from changing shape\n    due to temperature and humidity changes.  Gluing the arms & body together with cyanoacrylic\n    glue will be stronger if you pin the arms to the body first.  I then primed the figure\n    with several light misted on coats of Floquil flat white primer.  I let this dry\n    overnight.  Be sure to wash all parts with a mild dish soap and dry thoroughly\n    before priming.\n<p>\n    I began by base coating the costume with flat black acrylic through my airbrush.  Next,\n    I based out the flesh with a light ivory mixed from white and tan.  Next, I dry-brushed a\n    pale blue-gray all over his costume to highlight the details.  Then I painted the torture\n    tools and hooks silver mixed with gun-metal.  I then sprayed clear flat to protect what I\n    had done so far.  After that had dried thoroughly I began to apply washes to Pinhead\'s\n    grid pattern of a bloody red color.  I also applied washes to the wounds on the belly and\n    chest, the hands, and the severed head at the base of his robe.\n<p>\n    Next, a mix of burgundy and road rash brown was used to shade in the major shadows of\n    face and hands.  A mixture of Horizon cbocolate brown and pink/burgundy\n    was applied, well-thinned, with a fine point detail brush to sharpen all of the\n    features on the face and hands.  Next Polytranspar Black Umber was used for additional\n    shading around the eyes.  Finally, a blue black was dry-brushed an the eyelids and\n    eye socket area.\n<p>\n    For the eyes themselves an off-white color was painted in first using a very small detail\n    brush.  When that had dried, large black pupils were painted in.  Pinhead\'s eyes are almost\n    completely black like huge dialeted pupils.  Base coat the inside of the mouth black and,\n    carefully paint in yellow/browilish teeth.  Apply several coat\'s of clear flat to the head\n    and face.  After that has dried thoroughly (overnight), carefully insert pins\n    after warming the head with a hairdryer.  The skeletal hand intestines on the back\n    of the robe and the severed head can now be finished.  I painted the hair on the head\n    dark brown with a black wash over that.  I then based-out the intestines with a pinkish red.\n    The skeletal hand and vertabrae were based out with a ruddy brown mixed by adding red\n    and brown.  I dry-brushed the skeletal hand with a dark bone color to pick up the highlights.\n     I put several washes of ruddy flesh color over the severed head and the intestines and\n    finished them with bloody washes.\n<p>\n    Finally, drill two holes into the temples of the resin skeull.  With a pair of wire cutters, I\n    snipped two pins in half.  After the skull has been based out in a bone-white color, I applied\n    washes of transparent black and brown. I glued one cut-off pin through one end of the\n    chain provided.  I put the pin through tbe chain and glued the pin into a whole in the skull.\n    One end of the hook has a hole.  Thread the chain through the hole and glue the second pin\n    the chain into the skull.  The hook and heart were painted bloody red and gloss coated\n    several times for a wet look.  The heart with hook and skull with chain were then\n    glued into either side of Pinhead\'s fist.  Finally some light veins were dry brushed onto the\n    heart with ivory white.  Last I cut the cord provided, drilled holes into the tool handles\n    and the belly wound, threaded the cord tbrqugh into the shape of a \"W\". I painted the handles\n    wood grain and gave the blades a black wash.  This part was a bit tricky with the cord,\n    but those of you who find this difficult can leave this part out and your kit will still\n    look great.\n <p>\n    The tricky part is you must cut the cord into four individual lengbhs and poke the ends\n    through the holes in the handles.  Put a drop of glue on it to keep it there, anchoring the\n    other end to the wounds on the stomach.  The center point of the W is the belly wound.\n    The artist sculpted in only two torture tools and Pinhead is usually seen with a total of\n    four tools hanging from his waist.  Also you must provide 2 small fishhooks to coplete a\n    detail on the back of his head.  These are the only items missing and can easily be provided\n    by the builder.\n<p>\n    In closing, I think this is the best most accurate Pinhead so far.  I like the scale and the\n    corpse/victim stuff on the bottom of the skirt for a mice change of pace.  You can work wonders\n    with this kit when you give it a really  good paint job.  I hope Screamin\' will continue to\n    produce the other Cenobites in this scale.  That would make some wonderful dioramas for\n    all of you brethren of the Order of the Gash.  It would also be nice to see some\n    diorama bases and accessoeries torture pillars, victims, etc.).  As usual, sculptor\n    Jeff Brower has surpassed himself again (I\'m looking forward to his Xena figure next).  Each\n    piece is better than the last.'),(53,270,'1998-01-24 14:05:13',3733,'conquest','<img src=\"../garage/letter/c.gif\" alt=\"C\" WIDTH=\"27\" HEIGHT=\"31\">hildren\'s\nbubblegum cards featuring Martian war attrocities - now\nTHERE\'S something you don\'t see everyday!  I have no idea what the folks at\nTopps were thinking when they released the Mars Attacks card set in 1962,\nbut I\'m glad they did.  Those leering faces and exposed brains made a real\nimpact on me as a child.  Images of soldiers shrieking as they were\nincinerated by Martian deathrays remain as vivid now as they did when I\nfirst saw the Mars Attacks cards.  You could almost smell the burning...\nWell, you get the idea.\n\n<p>\nOver thirty years later the cards are more popular than ever, having\nspawned off comics, novels, an upcoming movie, and (my favorite) garage\nkits.  At least two independent companies have produced resin versions of\nthe Martian marauders, but the most widely distributed and accessible kits\nhave been released by Screamin\'.  In this review, we\'ll be taking a look at\nthe most recent Martian in the Screamin\' series, \"Contemplating Conquest.\"\n\n<p>\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/larry_brackney/conquest_jar.jpg\" width=\"150\" height=\"216\" align=\"right\" hspace=\"16\" vspace=\"2\">\n\"Conquest\" marks a slight departure for the series, in that it depicts a\nMartian scientist, or Paeec, rather than the more familiar warrior-caste\nMartians.  Paeecs are distinguished by their slightly larger craniums and a\nsingular eyestalk that protrudes from between the brain\'s hemispheres.\nLike the \"Target Earth\" kit, featuring the Martian Leader, \"Conquest\"\nfeatures a solitary Martian figure without an elaborate base.  Instead, the\nfigure is accented with a column supporting a severed human head in a glass\n\"jar.\"  While I usually prefer bases with my kits, I think the figure and\ncolumn stand on their own quite nicely.  It also serves to keep costs lower\nand avoids some of the difficulties inherent in the vinyl bases Screamin\'\nhas adopted with their more elaborate dioramas.\n\n<p>\nAs with the other kits in the series, \"Contemplating Conquest\" consists\nof a mixture of resin and soft vinyl parts.  A clear plastic dome is\nprovided to contain the scientist\'s \"experiment.\"  Some straight pins and\nsolder are included to fabricate \"brain probes\" for the head in the jar.  A\nsmall resin nameplate doubles as a holder for the enclosed trading card.\n\n<p>\nAssembly of the martian is straightforward and requires no putty work at\nall.  Three assembly/prep tips are worth noting:  first remember to stuff\nthe vinyl parts liberally with newspaper, lest you suffer mightily at a\nlater date!  Secondly, if you choose to prime the kit (as I did) make sure\nto dust the kit VERY gently.\nThe head is cast in glow-in-the-dark vinyl which is particularly\nsensitive to lacquer-based primers. Screamin\' does not recommend using\nlacquer-based primers on their kits.  Finally, be sure\nto affix the clear dome to the column (after painting the head!) with a two\npart epoxy.  Affixing the dome with a cyano-acrylate (super) glue WILL\ncause the dome to fog up!\n\n<p>\n<img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/larry_brackney/conquest_face.jpg\" width=\"156\" height=\"216\" align=\"left\" hspace=\"16\" vspace=\"2\">\nJoe Laudati has turned out what I feel is the nicest figure in the\nseries.  The face is well detailed, and conveys a good deal of emotion in\nthe smile alone - no simple task given the Martian\'s minimal features!  The\nexpression of sadistic amusement, as the Martian strikes a Hamlet-inspired\npose, is entirely appropriate.  The flesh is also varied and nicely\ntextured, allowing for a great many possibilities in drybrushing.  Scales,\nboney protrusions, smooth folds of brain matter, and reptilian plates are\nall there, breaking up what might otherwise be a boring surface to work on.\n\n<p>\nThe scientist\'s robe is also interesting to look at.  Filligree work\naccents the robe, while not appearing busy or obtrusive.  A high collar and\nneck sash add additional variation.  I was quite impressed w/ Laudati\'s\nattention to what\'s going on UNDER the Paeec\'s robe as well.  The robe\nfollows the lines of the Martian\'s scrawny physique as evidenced by\nsculpting near the left leg.\n\n<p>\nThe sculpting of the human head for the \"head containment unit\" is a bit\ncoarse and could have stood some additional refinement, particularly in the\nmouth area.  The exposed brain is a nice touch, and looks great with the\n\"brain probes.\"  My only quibble with the kit concerns the resin \"hoses\"\nthat were included to detail the column.  As provided, the parts don\'t fit\nproperly.  Even after heating the parts in hot water and bending them, they\nstill didn\'t look quite right.  I finally opted to discard them and replace\nthem with real segments of wire.\n\n<p>\nAs a gimmick, the Martian\'s head is cast in glow-in-the-dark vinyl.  I\ndon\'t think any self-respecting garage kitter would leave that as-is.  For\nthose wondering how to paint a gruesome brain (you get TWO chances with\nthis kit!), I recommend basecoating with a medium fleshtone like Horizon #6\nor Tamiya \"flat flesh.\"  Follow that with washes of Horizon \"pink\nburgandy,\" or better still, Polytranspar \"mohr and lavendar flesh\" or\n\"violet.\"  Build the washes up slowly, allowing the color to pool in the\nundulations of the brain.  If the raised areas are getting too dark,\ndrybrush in your base color and lightly wash again.  A gloss lacquer is the\nfinishing touch!\n\n<p>\n\"Contemplating Conquest\" is a nicely sculpted kit which has definite\nappeal for Mars Attacks fans.  I think it\'s an interesting enough\ncomposition to draw in other modelers looking for inexpensive figure kits\nas well.  The castings are well executed, requiring minimal clean up.\nBuild up is effortless, making this a \"painter\'s kit\" or even a good\nbeginner\'s kit.  Although the Paeec Martian design is less familiar than\nthe others in Screamin\'s Mars Attacks series, this one is my favorite of\nthe lot.'),(54,270,'1998-01-24 12:36:19',3633,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/i.gif\" alt=\"I\" WIDTH=\"11\" HEIGHT=\"29\"> first set eyes on this version of the Bride of Frankenstein in Terry\nWebb\'s Revenge of the Garage Kit That Ate My Wallet.  You couldn\'t help but\nnotice her, a nubile figure, scantily clad in a tattered bandage bikini,\nbending over to do lord knows what.  This was DEFINITELY not the Bride as she\nappeared in the film!\n\n<p>\n   Webb\'s book lists her as being available from Kevin Stephens\' garage kit\noutfit, Shadow Castings.  Apparently, Stephens and Monsters in Motion have\nstruck up an agreement, because several of his kits (including the Horror\nHigh Cheerleader and possessed Ash) are now available exclusively through\nMiM.\n\n<p>\n   The New Bride is a 1:6 scale kit cast in white resin, and comes in 7\npieces: torso, right leg, left leg and arm, right arm, head, base, and\n&quot;voltulator.&quot;  I gather that a length of chain was to be included as well,\nbut was nowhere to be found in my box.  Like most indy kits, the packaging\nisn\'t that exciting - a plain box with a sheet of color Xerox artwork.  A\nreference photo and WONDERFUL instruction sheet are also included.\n (Stephens\' trademark instruction sheets are hilarious and not to be missed.)\n\n<p>\n   The casting I received was, for the most part, quite clean.  No\nsignificant air bubbles or pinholes were present, and flash was quick to\nclean up.  The head piece was a bit on the &quot;flashy&quot; side, and did require\nsome serious clean up and putty work for it to mate properly with the torso.\n The other parts are all keyed and socketed neatly.  Most of the breaklines\noccur where bandages meet bare flesh, so putty work can be avoided with\ncareful prep work.\n\n<p>\n   The pose?  In a word AMAZING, but not for the reasons that you might\nthink.  Yes, this is a titillating piece, but from a mechanical standpoint, I\nwas floored when I discovered that the figure was so well balanced that she\ncould stand on her own!  Quite a accomplishment when you consider the\noverextended pose and the fact that she\'s wearing high heels.  I\'ve seen\nplaster weighted 1:4 scale Screamin\' kits that couldn\'t stand up as well as\nthis kit!\n\n<p>\n   Detail is, on the whole, very nice.  The Bride\'s bandages are all well\ndetailed, and were great fun to drybrush.  Stephens\' attention to anatomical\ndetail is above average too.  Muscle detail is well defined and is quite\nrealistic.  The addition of bracelets, anklets, and charms is a nice touch\ntoo.  The face and hair were a little disappointing to me.  Once painted, the\nface looks quite nice, but is just a little plain.  Greater cheekbone\ndefinition would have gone a long way towards guiding &quot;make-up&quot; application\nto the face.  The hair could use more detailing as well.  More definition\nwould make drybrushed highlights much more dramatic.\n\n<p>\n   The stonework detail is adequate, and the addition of a few rats is a nice\ntouch.  The Voltulator is an interesting addition, but some may think its\nplacement as a phallic symbol pushes the kit over the top.  (Frankly, I don\'t\nsee how the kit can be any more &quot;over the top&quot; considering the pose she\'s\nin!)  I was amused to spot components from an X-Wing fighter kit buried in\nthe Voltulator.  See if you can spot them!  A final bit of detailing is the\nchain which connects the Bride\'s &quot;choker&quot; to the floor.  Although my kit was\nmissing this, every good monster kit builder should have some on hand!\n\n<p>\n   The New Bride is available for $99 from Monsters in Motion, and represents\nan excellent garage kit value in my opinion.  For those whose tastes lie in\nthis direction, the figure will have appeal as a unique combination of horror\nand playful sexiness.  The base is well designed to accent the figure, and\ntakes up no more shelf space than is required.  Forget baggy hospital gowns\nand bad attitudes.  THIS is a bride for Frankenstein\'s monster!'),(55,316,'1998-01-24 12:38:27',3933,'','<b><img src=\"../garage/letter/i.gif\" alt=\"I\" WIDTH=\"11\" HEIGHT=\"29\"></b>\nwas\'nt very taken by this kit at first, but my curiosity got the better\nof me and I broke down and got it.Not being a fan of vinyl kits, I was\'nt\neven sure if I would start it when it came in,but something about it\n(beside the fact that it\'s a Clive Barker design) drove me forward. The\ndesign was thought out well, and went together very good, although the\nsection where the upper and lower torso go together was a little tough to\nconceal. One thing that had to immediaely go was the huge schlong that this\nthing has. I display my kits openly around my house, and I will never\nunderstand why some kits are made with penises that would make John Holmes\njealous! Doing the plastic surgery (pun intended) on this creepy critter\nmade things a bit more difficult, but not impossible. The altar is hollow,\nwith an open bottom, so it took a ton of filler and resin.If it\'s not at\nleast partially filled, it wlll be top heavy and hard to pin the figure to.\nThe Demon is\'nt heavily detailed, but I believe it has plenty to comlpiment\nit, and a lot would have taken away from Mr. Barkers concept.For those who\ncollect box art and instructions, these are done nicely and better than\nmost I have seen lately.'),(56,270,'1998-06-01 10:08:11',3777,'sekenrah','<img src=\"../garage/letter/o.gif\" alt=\"O\" WIDTH=\"30\" HEIGHT=\"32\">ne\nthing I always appreciate in a garage kit, is when an artist\nattempts to take a tried and true subject and do something unique with it.\nThis is the case in Rick Wyatt\'s Curse of Sekenrah produced by the Shape of\nThings.  Sekenrah is a 1:8 mummy kit, but aside from the fact that the\npiece features an Egyptian undead, the kit has little in common with\n\"traditional\" mummies.\n<p>\nRather than opt for a bandage swaddled, lumbering, Karloff look-alike,\nWyatt\'s sculpted a decaying pharoah that would seem quite at home in a\nRomero movie.  Bandages are draped loosely about the shriveled flesh\nrevealing an abdomen that has rotted completely away.  The gaunt face is\nvery Wrightsonesque.  Strips of loose flesh hang from the bottom of his\nface, and the nose cavity and one eye socket are completely empty.  A regal\nskirt and cowl are all that remain of Sekenrah\'s burial finery.\n<p>\nSekenrah clutches a machette in one hand.  Optional parts give you some\nlatitude as to what\'s going on in his other hand.  Timid kit builders may\nopt to build Sekenrah with an empty, outstretched, hand.  Alternately,\nSekenrah may be made to clutch the dismembered torso of an unfortunate\narchaeologist.  A sandy base, featuring some partially excavated relics and\na shovel, rounds out the scene.\n<p>\nSculpting quality is on par to above average.  The pose is quite natural\nand the figures (including the victim) are well proportioned and detailed.\nI did feel that the bandages were perhaps a little too thick, however.  The\narchaeologist is one of my favorite things about the kit.  He looks like he\nis/was truly in pain, and the bowling ball grip that the mummy is employing\nto grasp his face looks agonizing.\n<p>\nSince Sekenrah is largely unbandaged, you have the opportunity to paint\nHIM rather than just his wrappings - although you get to do that too.  I\nopted for a grey/green/black color scheme, figuring that his decaying body\nhas been somewhat well preserved for centuries.  (If you\'ve seen pictures\nof real mummies, they tend to be black.)  Bandages were base coated with\nPolly-S Doped Linen, then aged with washes and drybrushing.  I opted for a\nblue and faint orange color scheme for his skirt and headdress - both of\nwhich I heavily weathered and faded.  The victim was a lot of fun to paint\nas well - lots of wounds to detail!\n<p>\nCasting quality was quite good.  The kit contains 6 creamy resin parts,\nincluding the empty hand and optional hand/victim.  A white metal machette\nis included along with a two part white metal shovel.  I chose to replace the\nwhite metal shovel handle with a dowel rod to cut down on the weight that\nhad to be supported at the shovel\'s head.  (It also meant that I didn\'t\nneed to paint it!)\nNearly all of the parts contained visible seam lines that took some time to\nclean up.  Some small air bubbles were present as well, but neither problem\nwas excessive or unexpected.\n<p>\nI purchased Sekenrah for $75, and felt that it was a great value for the\nmoney.  The finished piece does an excellent job of capturing the \"moment.\"\nThat moment being, a stupid tomb robber getting what he deserves at the\nhands of a vengeful corpse!  The option to build a horribly gorey or an\nonly slightly gorey kit is a thoughtful gesture that required little\nadditional effort from Rick or Matt.  Karloff\'s mummy not frightening\nenough for you?  Give Sekenrah a try!'),(57,5066,'1998-01-24 12:43:09',4030,'martian','<b><img src=\"../garage/letter/s.gif\" alt=\"S\" WIDTH=\"21\" HEIGHT=\"33\"></b>traight\n  off of 1960\'s bubble gum cards, detoured momentarily through a\nrecent film, and crash landing on your hobby bench is Skyhook Models new\nresin kit entitled, Attacking Martian Saucer.\n<p>\n   This latest spaceship from Skyhook owner, Dave Bengel\'s talented and\ncreative hands is sleek and slick! Designed precisely after the landing\nvehicles seen in the film last Christmas, the Attacking Martian Saucer is a\nvery professionally produced garage kit at a nice price.\n<p>\n   At approximately seven inches across, the flying saucer comes in 11\npieces; two saucer halves, top and bottom domes, six legs, and groundwork\nbase. The base has six indentations to allow the ship to stay in place\nwithout gluing. The six legs are ingenious little devices that look great\nextended from the ship body.\n<p>\n   The carefully engineered model was professionally turned by lathe and\nneeds a very minor amount of clean-up. Casting is excellent. The box includes\nfull color photo boxart on the lid. The ship is basically silver but the\nmodel on the box was given various different shades of metallic colors to\nsimulate each individual panel of its make-up. Really sharp!\n<p>\n   Get your own copy of this must-have kit by sending $65 + $6 shipping to\nSkyhook Models P.O. Box 327 Carmichael, Ca. 95609'),(58,5066,'1998-01-24 14:07:20',4324,'c57d','<br clear=all>\n<img src=\"../garage/letter/s.gif\" alt=\"S\" WIDTH=\"21\" HEIGHT=\"33\">kyhook Models\nhas been producing\nspaceships and other resin model kits for\na number of years now and just released\ntheir latest, the Space Cruiser C-57D.\nThis is a beautiful, scale model based on\nthe \"flying saucer\" space ship used by\nLeslie Nielsen and crew in the 1956 science\nfiction\nclassic, \"Forbidden Planet.\" The model\ncomes in ten parts finely cast in a off-white\ncolored polyurethane resin. The pieces\nthat make up the disc were professionally\nturned on a lathe based on blueprints for\nthe original spaceship. Consequently, the\nkit is extremely accurate and all parts fit\nlike a glove.\n<p>\nThe three landing gear pieces form a\ntripod for the space cruiser to sit on. They\ncan also be glued up into place if you\nwish to hang your model from the ceiling.\nOne landing \"leg\" forms a detailed\nstairway for entering and exiting the ship.\n<p>\nSkyhook owner, Dave Bengel has\nconvincingly sculpted a base made up of\nthe rocky, Altair Four landscape for the\ncompleted ship to rest upon. The ship\nspans about nine inches across with the\nbase being about 2 inches longer at its\nwidest. The\"Forbidden Planet\" logo is\ncast into the base\'s edge.\n<p>\nCasting is close to flawless with no air\nbubbles and minimal clean-up along the\nedge of the ship\'s disc. The model comes\nboxed with full color photo boxart. A\nclever instruction sheet shows Robbie the\nRobot explaining construction of the kit.\nOh, I almost forgot. A very tiny (in scale)\nRobbie the Robot is also included. This is\nsuch an \"easy build\" that it\'s perfect for a\nbeginning modeler and seasoned\nmodelers will have this baby up and flying\nin no time.\n<p>\nEven painting is pretty basic as the ship\nis entirely a silver, metallic color. The\ndetailed \"Robbie\" figure can be painted\nmonotone or given a more detailed paint\njob. You\'ll need a paint brush with only\none hair in it to color his almost\nmicroscopic buttons, though.\n<p>\nYou can\'t go wrong with this Space\nCruiser C-57D and it\'s priced right at $65\n+ $5 shipping from Skyhook Models P.O.\nBox 327 Dept. GG Carmichael, Ca.\n95609'),(59,316,'1998-01-24 12:51:36',4024,'dark','<img src=\"../garage/letter/t.gif\" alt=\"T\" WIDTH=\"21\" HEIGHT=\"29\">his is an AWESOME kit.\nIt\'s only 1/12th scale, but what it lacks in\nsize it makes up for in detail. It always blows my mind to see someone\ndo a kit from a painting and see their interpretation of what isn\'t\nshown on canvas. This kit is a prime example. The power of the horse is\nclearly shown in muscle tone and veins througout. The mane and tail\nare sculpted just as well. The rider has so much detail shown in\nhis muscletone and battle garb that I\'m still trying to fathom the time\nit must have taken to do it in this scale. All the accessories are\nincluded for the straps, chains, and rings. I won\'t mislead anyone, this\nis not a kit for someone who doesn\'t have patience. It consists of\n25+ peices (I can\'t count them all for fear of losing some of the\nsmall peices in the pack). The only thing I didn\'t care for is that\nthe base is cast in plaster of paris and was chipped just because\nit\'s a fragile material. I\'m planning on making my own base from\nepoxy putty.'),(60,190,'1998-01-24 12:54:05',4325,'','<img src=\"../garage/letter/t.gif\" alt=\"T\" WIDTH=\"21\" HEIGHT=\"29\">he\nDawn\'s Temptress kit is a 6-piece white resin model kit based upon\nthe comic character created by Joseph Michael Linsner.  The kit portrays the\nheroine standing clad in a neglige (I\'m told the outfit is known as a &quot;Merry\nWidow&quot; but I wouldn\'t know about such things...) adorned with a pattern of\nskulls.  Her attire also consists of a g-string bottom (with zipper), garters,\nelbow-length lace gloves, stockings, and knee-length boots.  She is posed in a\nsort of beckoning stance with her right hand outstretched toward the viewer,\nthe other held back at her hip.  Her left leg is raised up on the ball of her\nfoot with the left knee in front of her right knee.  The right leg bears most\nof her weight, giving her a difficult, but pleasing pose.\n<p>\nThe base of the kit is a good portrayal of the backgrounds common in\nsome of Linsner\'s artwork.  It consists of the wings, tails, heads, arms,\nclaws, and tentacles of monsters and demons.  Adorning the forefront of the\nbase are the two cinematic masks; comedy and tragedy.  Also along the base were\nthe initials J. Laudati.  I am uncertain whether or not he sculpted the kit as\nthere were no credits included in my box.\n<p>\nThe kit I purchased arrived in a plain brown box filled with packing\npeanuts.  The pieces appeared to have been placed in the box with not much\nregard to their safety during shipping.  There were also no instructions or\nphotographs of a finished kit.  I would have liked to have seen at least a\nphoto or a cheap set of photocopied instructions.\n<p>\nThe sculpting appeared to be a bit rough in areas as though the project\nwas rushed.  There were several areas where more detail would have been ideal\nand added more to the look of the kit.  For example, many of the folds in the\nboots and gloves could have been sharper and more defined as well as some of\nthe buckles and clasps found throughout the kit.  The base also had a degree of\n&quot;roughness&quot; to it.  A few of the appendages on the base melded into the\ngroundwork where I would have preferred to see clean, defining edges.\n<p>\nThe breaks for the pieces were well thought out.  The kit comes with\nthe base, two arms, two legs, and the main torso/head piece.  The legs were\nseparated where the thighs fit into the tops of the boots above the knee.  The\narms were similarly separated at the edges of the gloves just past the elbows.\nThese separations would allow the builder to paint the appendages independantly\nand then glue them to the finished body with little or no putty work.  When\nbuilding this kit, I would recommend the arms and legs be pinned with\ncorresponding holes drilled into the torso section.  The pieces should be test\nfit before painting and final assembly.\n<p>\nUnfortunately the casting quality of the kit was its major drawback.\nMy kit had gobs of excess resin at the joints and it was impossible to dry-fit\nthe pieces directly out of the box.  I had to use my dremel tool carefully and\nrepeatedly to create the necessary mating surfaces for each piece.  The boots\nwere also subjected to a dremel tool assault due to the amount of excess resin\non them.  I had to practically recreate one entire foot.  The fingers and hands\nalso did not cast well.  Some fingers were missing fingernails and appeared\nwrinkled and aged.  I spent a lot of time meticulously sanding them into a more\npleasing, youthful look.  There were also numerous air bubbles which needed to\nbe filled.  The hair was loaded with them and lots of time was spent filling\nand resculpting this region.  Worse yet, her body itself was not smooth in most\nplaces and was instead covered with pock-marks and depressions.  The major\nconcentrations of these were beneath the chin and on the front and backs of the\nupper thighs.  In all, I spent many hours sanding and reworking this model to\nmake it look more presentable.  Even after priming I found other areas I had\nmissed and had to go back and rework the kit some more.  Because of the casting\nquality I would not recommend this kit to beginners.\n<p>\nIn closing I find that Dawn is what I consider a below average kit.  I\nfeel for its price one could find other kits with greater potential.  Still, it\ncan be made into a decent piece for a collection with several hours of\nreworking.  Fans of Dawn and Girl kits in general should be prepared to spend a\nlot of time on the build up in order to make the kit an acceptable addition to\ntheir collections.  When primed and prepped the kit does offer some challenges\nto the painter.  Liquid mask is recommended and techniques such as see through\nclothing would be well utilized on Dawn.\n<p>\nUntil next review keep modeling and have fun.'),(61,208,'1998-01-24 13:17:52',4216,'victim','<b><img src=../garage/letter/t.gif alt=\"T\"></b>his\n    white resin head and torso is superbly realistic. The kit comes in one piece.\n    It depicts a male figure with an arched back and one arm torn off. There is\n    also a wound on the side of the neck. The organs are very accurately sculpted.\n    The lungs, heart, intestines, and spine are all easy to recognize. Because\n    the scale is so small, these details are very impressive.\n<p>\n    The face is nicely done,  although a little narrow. I was especially\n    impressed by the right arm and hand. The fingers look really good.  The only thing\n    that is limiting about this kit is also the same thing that makes it unique: the pose.\n    From the tip of the head to the tip of the severed spine is a perfect U-shape.\n    It will look great in the hand of a werewolf or some other monster.  But it\n    would look awkward if placed on the ground or elsewhere.\n<p>\n    The directions that come with this kit could seem quite daunting to a novice; it calls\n    for painting with both acrylics and oils.  If you\'re careful and patient the results\n    will be very rewarding.\n<p>\n    It\'s true that nothing creates beautiful skin tones like oil paint.  Just don\'t rush\n    to the next  step until oil color is completely dry.  If you live in a humid\n    climate, the paint may remain tacky a little longer, so wait. like the directions\n    say, until you cannot detect an, odor.  The painting directions are more specific\n    and detailed than one usually finds.  The diagram is very good and extremely helpful.\n<p>\n    Stephen F. Venters has created a beautiful diorama addition that will enhance whatever\n    it is placed with.  The sculpting is really top-notch.  His is a relatively new kit\n    company and I must admit that I am, impressed with the level of quality.  The\n    attention to detail includes things like castings, instruction sheets, and the painting\n    guide.  I am looking forward to seeing other products and reviewing them.\n\n  <p>\n\n    The literature that comes with the kit mentions that this was designed for use with Soldat\'s\n    Gore Hound (I have never personally seen this kit), Halcyon\'s or Geometric\'s Aliens or\n    their Predators or virtually any other 1/9 or 1/8th scale killer.  It may be placed in a\n    monster\'s hand (includes indentations for fingers on the back) or displayed alone. I hope\n    that Mr. Venters will continue with these diorama accessories.  I for one would love to see\n    victims such as this in many different scales including female victims and dismembered\n    bodies all the way up to 1/4 scale to go with my Screamin\' Cenobites.  These are the kind\n    of kits that cry out for gorey victims such as this.\n<p>\nContact:  Stephen F. Venters & Associates Productions,\n          2040 N. Elepson,\n          1 N. Chicago IL 60614'),(62,262,'1998-01-24 12:59:11',3825,'rei','<img src=\"../garage/letter/h.gif\" alt=\"H\" WIDTH=\"25\" HEIGHT=\"30\">ello again, anime Gremlins!\nThis time around I will be reviewing Rei\nAyanami from the anime series Neon Genesis Evangelion, now being\nreleased by A.D. Visions to video stores. This kit has  Rei, the pilot\nof Evangelion Unit 0, dressed in a typical Japanese school uniform.  Rei\nmaintains her distant, haunted expression she has in the series.\n<p>\nTaking this kit out of the box, I see that it comes in nine grey resin\npieces, with very few small parts: the main body, the limbs, the head, a\ntwo-piece skirt, and a school bag.  The sculpting of this kit is\nfantastic.  I was impressed by the casting; there were very few air\nbubbles and only a minor mold line where the two halves of the skirt\nmeet to deal with. The other pieces fit together at natural lines, such\nas the sleeves and neckline.  Assembly of this kit is quite simple. The\nonly necessary pins are between the legs and the waist and that assembly\nto the upper body.\n<p>\nPainting this kit is also very simple. The only problem I had was with\nthe dull, dark colors of the school uniform. As this kit is from the TV\nseries, I decided to paint the dress a bit lighter and with a very\nslight gloss, to spice it up a little.  Another thing to be aware of is\nthat Rei\'s skin tone is very pale.  No shadows or highlights are in her\nhair, as in the series, and I decided to paint her hair as she appears\non the screen.\n<p>\nThe eyes on this kit aren\'t the typical anime eyes, but are still fun to\npaint.  Rei rarely shows expression. Her lovely red eyes stare into the\ndistance, well framed by her short, blue hair.  A little time and\npatience will yield some very nice results here - just remember to have\nher eyes focusing far away, instead of at some nearby object.\n<p>\nTo sum up, this kit is another winner for Volks. Rei is  a very nice,\nvery simple anime kit from a good series, and lives up to Volks\nreputation for quality and style. Rei\'s another good kit to start with\nfor anyone interested in anime models as it is easy to find, assemble,\nand paint. Rei is also available in many other scales and poses, both in\nher school uniform and in the skin-tight combat suits the Evangelion\npilots wear, and even with her in bandages as she appears in the first\nepisode.  Enjoy this one.\n<p>\nHave fun painting the eyes!'),(63,201,'1998-01-24 14:10:48',3841,'orithya','<img src=\"../garage/letter/d.gif\" alt=\"D\" WIDTH=\"22\" HEIGHT=\"29\">elicate beauty.\nOrithya, the Sea Nymph, is a sculpture that can appeal to everyone.\nJust gazing at her gracefully swimming across the ocean floor makes\nme long for a trip to the ocean.  And to grab all my kit building\nsupplies and have some fun building and painting this intriguing kit!\n<p>\nI\'m a big fan of original designs and Orithya fits right in.\nEli Raphael designed this half fish and half woman character.\nIt\'s a great idea and one that I haven\'t seen before.  Her fish\nhalf seems to be based on the Loin Fish, which makes for a\ninteresting figure.\n<p>\nThe brown cardboard box that the kit arrives in includes a sketch of Orithya on the cover,\nbut it seems to be longer than the box and was partially cut off.\nThat\'s too bad as the sketch is very nice.  I would rather have\nthe sketch wrap around the side of the box, or have it included inside\nthe box (maybe rolled up or folded in half).  Each part of the kit is wrapped\nin bubble wrap and foam packing material is used to keep everything\nfrom bumping around.  The parts seemed to be very well protected.\n<p>\nThe sculpting was performed by Edgar Perez and is very nice.  The\npose has a lot of action and grace.  The flesh is very smooth and\nhas nice proportions.  The hair is quite detailed and\nlooks like it is flowing underwater.  The ears, gills, and webbed\nhands convey her underwater origins even in the human upper part\nof her body.  Her lower body is very fish like and has good detail\non the scales and fins.  The arched tail also adds the the effect\nthat she is ready to propel herself through the water.\n<p>\nThe base has some nice touches with coral, sponge, star fish,\nand sandy areas.  Also, it appears to be the site of a sunken\nship with some wood, cannon, bones, and a skull.  It is quite an\ninteresting base and sets off the figure nicely.  The figure is\nmounted to the base near the back of her body so that Orithya\nappears to be suspended in the water.  The base also includes a\nZotz nameplate on the back.  My one complaint with the sculpting\non the base was that there were a couple of places where finger\nprints could be detected.  But, since there are so many textures\non the base these are very hard to see once the base is painted.\n<p>\nThe body was cast in a slightly brittle yellow resin.  I didn\'t\nhave any problems working with this material.  It was easy to\ntrim the small amount of sprue left on the parts with an x-acto\nknife.  All the parts fit together well once the excess material\nwas removed.  There were <b>no</b> detectable seam lines anywhere.\nThere were a couple of medium size air holes that were easily\nrepaired by filling with putty.  There were also some small air\nbubbles in the hair.  I didn\'t attempt to do anything to these\nas I did not think that they would be noticeable once the hair\nwas painted.\n<p>\nThe base is hollow and appears to have been made using fiberglass.\nThe outside bottom edge of the base is covered with a thin piece\nof hard material and is very even.  The part of the rock that\nsupports Orithya is filled with resin for added strength.  I was\nvery pleasantly suprised by the casting of the base.  It is clear\nthat a lot of thought and energy went into developing a process\nthat would give great detail, light weight, high strength, and\na flat bottom surface.  More garage kit companies should pay this\nmuch attention to their bases!\n<p>\nThere isn\'t much included in the way of assembly instructions, so\nyou need to plan out how you want to assemble and paint the kit\nyourself. Here is the approach that I suggest.  You can attach the head to the upper body first.  Then\nthe arms to the upper body.  Next the large fin on the back can be\nattached followed by the rest of the fins on the lower body,\n<b>except</b> the large fins that attach at the hips.\nThen attach the upper and lower body.  The seams can be cleaned\nup and puttied as you go.  This assembly order should allow you to reach all of the\nseams easily.  This produces the following subassemblies: the\nbody, the two large fins, the coral, and the base.  The subassemblies\ncan then be painted individually and then attached after painting.\n<p>\nI\'m a big fan of pinning.  There is nothing worse than seeing\na part pop off of a finished kit!  I pinned each spine on the\nlarge fin on the back.  Then attached the fin to the back with\nsuperglue and hit it with kicker.  I also pinned the head, arms,\nand waist.  Super glue and kicker were used there also.  The smaller\nfins were attached with a two part 1 minute epoxy.\n<p>\nThe seams were fairly easy to deal with.  Where the fins join\nthe body I applied some fairly coarse putty and used a sculpting\ntool to blend the putty into the lines on the fins.  On the neck,\narm, and waist seams I used a very fine putty, smoothed with water,\nthen lightly sanded after it was dry.\n<p>\nTo get ready for painting I decided to mount some rods on the\nsubassemblies.  This provides a way to prime and paint the figure\nwithout having to hold onto parts of it and possibly damage\nalready painted areas. I drilled a hole completely through the figure\nat her hips where the large fins will be mounted and glued a long\nmetal rod into place.  Then I stuck the rod into a piece of wood.\nI also drilled into the large fins and mounted temporary\nrods.  One more rod was mounted into the bottom of the coral piece\nthat comes separate from the base.  At this point all of the\nsubassemblies can be dusted with a light coat of figure primer.\nAfter waiting 24 hours for the primer to dry completely you will\nbe ready to paint.\n<p>\n<center><img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/denis_bohm/orithya_reference.jpg\" width=\"381\" height=\"163\"></center>\n<p>\nFrom here the painting is up to you.  Three good reference photos\nare provided with the kit.  You can also take a trip to the library\nand checkout some books with pictures of Lion Fish to give you some\nadditional guidance if you desire.  If you want to spice up the kit\neven more you might want to take a trip to a pet store and get some\nfish tank goodies, like small colored rocks, to add to the base.\nThese could be attached after the base has been painted using some\nwhite glue that will turn clear after curing.\n<p>\nIn summary, I think Orithya is a great kit!  I was pleased with\nthe sculpting and pose and pleasantly suprised at the quality of\nthe base.  If you have a little experience with resin kits and\nyou like the pictures of this kit I recommend it.\n<p>\n<a href=\"../zotz/index.html\"><b>Zotz</b></a> is off to a great\nstart with\n<a href=\"../zotz/orithya.html\"><b>Orithya</b></a>!'),(64,280,'1998-01-24 13:10:59',4208,'smallalien','<b><img src=../garage/letter/o.gif alt=\"O\"></b>f\nall the creatures ever created by the Hollywood monster mills, the \"Aliens\"\nfrom all 3 films have got to be my favorite villains. These guys are serious\nbad ass creatures.  The original designs by Gieger has been redone, redefined,\nand reworked in each film.  Plus.the same thing has happened in the model\nindustry. You would almost think this creature has been done to death, then a\nnew one shows up.  The GEOmetric issue of this creature is a case in point.\nThe style of the sculpting is what makes this a must have item for\n\"Aliens\" fans.  The basic \"Allen\" is there but somehow it looks leaner and\nmeaner, more aggressive. I wasn\'t sure about the hands at first but once I got it\ntogether I was sure that they were right.  The black & white box art show\nthe creature in a semi crouched stance,\n<p>\nI wanted more of a ready to spring fully crouched look.  I\'d heard stories about\nthe model collapsing on builders so the first thing I did after removing all the\nexcess and finely trimming all the joints was to fill all of the joints that would\nhold the kit up.  I would also pin all these areas.  The feet, ankle, knee, and hip\nJoints were filled with plumbers putty.   A 2 inch piece of aluminum rod was\nplaced in the putty before it hardened and each Joint was glued together as soon as possible.  This\nhad the effect of giving the lower portion of the figure weight and the pins added strength.  Care\nmust be taken when positioning before this process, feet flat on the ground, line up the knees, hips\nand ankles first.  Once cured it will take a lot to change the position.  The rest of the assembly is\npretty straight forward, as with all vinyl kits there are a lot of parts and care should be taken when\naligning them.\n<p>\n    The head is tilted at a bit of an angle and the fit is not all that good so some extra time will\nbe needed here.  I liked the idea of opening up the mouth of the creature.  I cut along the outline of\nthe teeth from one end to the other also severing the jaw. I heated the mouth area in hot\nwater, when soft I pried open the mouth.  I inserted a ball of plumbers putty into the mouth first\nbehind the law and smoothed it out into the teeth areas.  When hard this would hold the mouth open\nand hide the empty head.  The jaw muscles were then resculpted in with plumbers putty. This is a\nlittle extra work but it does have a nice effect.\n<p>\n    The tail presented me with some more problems, this might have been because of my going\nfor a more crouched position.  The tail is both solid and hollow pieces. I used a piece of solid\ncopper wire (household wire with the plastic covering removed) to reinforce and allow me to\nposition the tail.  The solid pieces were drilled out and putty was used to fill the hollow pieces.\nThe wire was run through each piece, the correct position was twisted into shape and then all the joints\nwere glued.  The new position is more up and over, rather than dragging on the ground.\n<p>\n    The base is the small GEOmetric \"Aliens\" issue made to go with this model.  It\'s rather\nplain but has some very nice details so I decided to use it but to also add something of my own to\ngive it a bit more drama.  The Dark Horse \"Aliens\" kit by Randy Bowen has always been a favorite\nof mine.  So as a tribute to Randy and a inspiration to me, I scratch built a copy of the \"air\ncompressor?\" to add to my base.  I also found a U.S.C.M. boot in my spare parts box, this was also\nadded to the base.  The base is under coated in flat black, then highlighted with dry brushing of gun-\nmetal, copper, and silver.  The acid areas are yellow with a gel-medium added, then a \"glow-green\"\nwas added to highlight areas of it.  The compressor is undercoated flat black, then brown and green\nwashes were applied.  A few silver scratches and a bit of dripping blood finished this off.\n<p>\n    Painting the \"Alien\". This is where I try to be creative.  I\'m of the opinion that these beings\ncome in al I sorts of color schemes, so I try to vary it with each different kit.  The basic flat black\nwas used as the undercoat.  A dark brown and a lighter shade were then washed onto the hard\nsurfaces of the creature.  Brass and Gold were dry brushed lightly onto the more metallic areas. A\ncombination of silver, brass, and gold were used to bring out the mechanical parts of the \"Alien\".  A\nbit of fish line and two-part epoxy was used to create the drool hanging from it\'s chin.\n<p>\n    When mounted to the base and placed on a shelve, this model impresses me.  It\'s not my\nfavorite \"Allen\" kit, but it does have a certain something about it.  I\'d recommend that any fans of the\nTrilogy give this kit some room on a shelve of their own.\n'),(65,280,'1998-01-24 13:35:00',4063,'kong','<b><img src=\"../garage/letter/k.gif\" alt=\"K\" WIDTH=\"26\" HEIGHT=\"29\"></b>ing\nKong is a favorite of mine the film still makes me\ngasp in awe.  This was the film that got me interested in\nScience Fiction so many years ago. There are quite a few\nkits that were taken from this film.  If I like a particular\none or it just is too good of a deal to pass up I\'ll get it\nand add it to the collection.  This kit was just that.  A\nfriend who runs a comic book shop got it in trade it was\nbuilt up and badly painted but the price was right.  The\noriginal artist must have painted it in enamels it took\nalmost a week in pine Sol to get it cleaned up.  The wings\nof the Pteradacyl have been bent so the kit could fit into a\nglass display case.  Kong himself was tipped way far back\nfor my taste and some sculpting was needed.\n<p>\n\nOnce the repairs were made it was time to paint.  This was the fun\npart with this kit.  I\'ve painted several King Kongs before,\nso this one is merely a variation on the color scheme Black, Browns\nGrey, and a bit of silver to denote his longivity. The Pteradacyl was\nthe challenge I painted the inside a biege, and the outside a sandstone\nI airbrushed in a Olive Green in a streak pattern on the inside\nand a Depot Green on the outside.  Then a Dark Brown and Black was\nused to add a spotted effect to the wings.  The viens were put in\nwith a Dark Blue.  The base is shades of grey with some Railroad\ngrass added to simulate moss.  The whole kit was mounted to a stained\nwooden oval and the brass nameplate from the original kit was mounted\nled to this.\n<p>\nAll in all a worthwhile experience.  So keep in mind the next time\nyou see a kit that has been poorly assembled and painted but has\na nice price on it that it can be saved and turned into a welcome\naddition to your collection.  I don\'t know if this is an original\nKaiyodo kit or not it makes no difference.  It took many more hours\nto complete than a pristine kit would have and I\'m sure it didn\'t\ncome out perfect.  The challenge was to see if I could turn it into\na kit that I would be happy to add to my collection.\nThe next time I see a rather vile looking but hard to find kit on\nsale I plan to take it home.  It may be a lot more work but sometimes\nit\'s worth it\n <p>\nUntil next time\n<p>\n\"keep your imagination in gear\" \"Keep the Faith\"'),(66,280,'1998-01-24 13:38:12',4326,'rider','<b><img src=\"../garage/letter/d.gif\" alt=\"D\" WIDTH=\"22\" HEIGHT=\"29\"></b>ue\n\n to the high cost of licensing only\nthe name has been changed to save some money.\nThis is the \"Death Rider\" in kit form. The\npresentation in 3-D is great, for a small kit it\ncertainly has \"Presence\" once completed and put\non the shelf. The kit which was sculpted\nmolded, and cast in Sweeden, took some time to\narrive at my door. But the wait was worth it\nSwede Creations kept in contact with me during\nthe long wait seems there was a problem with the\ncasting process, new equipment was needed. The\ntime must have been well spent the castings on\nthis kit are excellent. Very little preperation was\nneeded to build the kit.\n<p>\nTHE KIT:  It comes in a box with a full\ncolor photo copy of the completed kit on the\ntop. A complete instruction sheet is enclosed. The\nBase as for the artwork, seems to be made of a\nplaster, you are given several small pieces that\nneed to be added to it. The Horse, comes in 3\nsections (head, forequarters, and hindquarters)\nseams are hidden by the horses hardware with little\ncare, no putty is required. The Rider comes in 7 parts\n(head, chest 2 arms, legs, and 2 boots. The moldmaker did\nanother excellent job here, as all the parts fit snuggly and\nseamlessly.  The Hardware, a bag of very small highly\ndetailed parts contains the hardware (helmet, horns,\nax, shield, sword, cape. and bits of chain and copper\nfoil for detailing)\n<p>\nTHE BUILD:   Not much to say here, the instructions are a\nlittle complex but if you follow them everything should\ngo nicely. The horse has a pouring lug on the neck that\nneeds to he removed with care.  The lug on the midsection\nis easier because it will be covered by the saddle.  Sand\nand fit until it looks right then glue it together. The rider\nis molded very nicely, the only problem that I had was with\nthe shield arm lineing up correctly to get the correct angle\nthat the shield sits at.  The parts fit together so nicely\nthat very little putty was needed on either the horse or rider.\nThe boots have very small eyelets in which to mount the\ncopper stirups, so great care must he taken to drill out\nthese. All the other small parts will also need much care in\nhandeling in order to get the best fit and look.  Patience\nis it\'s own reward here.'),(67,208,'1998-04-26 11:52:32',4327,'hellraiser','<p>\nThe <b>Hellraiser Wall Plaque</b> was sculpted by Paul Fay of <b>Invaders Models</b> of the United Kingdom.  He can be contacted at:\n</p>\n<p><b>\nPaul Fay<br>\nInvaders Models<br>\n48 Boston Avenue<br>\nRuncorn Cheshire<br>\nWA7 5XF United Kingdom<br>\nPhone: 01928 591344<br>\n</b></p>\n<p>\nPrice of this kit is 30 pounds sterling plus 12 pounds sterling overseas postage – U.S. only.  Please phone them if you are in another country.  The size of the plaque is approximately 7 inches tall by 11 inches wide.  The solid resin wall plaque is beautifully done – a must have for all Hellraiser fans.  It depicts Pinhead, Chatterer, Butterball, and the Female Cenobite in three-dimensional bas-relief.  The Cenobites are the head and shoulders only, but the Engineer is seen in his entirety grasping what looks like a bloody pancreas.  There is also a skeleton’s torso just below Chatterer’s right shoulder and an assortment of miscellaneous gory body parts along the bottom of the word Hellraiser.  Besides having to paint this baby yourself, you must also glue on the Engineer’s tail spike which is a separate piece as well as the metal torture device on the Female.  Last, but not least, you must cut down some pins to use on Pinhead.  It comes nicely boxed with color Xerox photo of the finished piece for painting reference.  I highly recommend it.\n</p>\n<p>\nThe likenesses as a group are very impressive.  The Chatterer looks particularly accurate on close examination.  The female is very expressive, especially if you paint her eyes right.  I don’t think any of the kits out there are perfect in their likeness of Doug Bradley as Pinhead.  This version is one of the better ones I’ve seen, though.  Being that the Engineer is one of my all time favorite movie monsters, I am particularly pleased with this plaque because he is featured full-figured.  Paul seems to be quite a decent chap; he sent along a selection of nice photos of some of his other work, as well as a few extra bits of resin for my personal collection.  If this is any indication of the kind of service you can expect, this must be a first rate copany!  I rate this kit a hearty 9.9.\n</p>\n'),(68,316,'1998-05-25 22:44:09',4222,'wolf_spirit',' Wolf Spirit is the newest kit release from the Fred DeRuvo at The Modeler\'s \nResource. This scantly clad 1/6th (although it looks a little closer to 1/7th)\nNative American female is as fine a kit as they come. The combined efforts of \nFred\'s critical preferences and Joe Laudati\'s skilled hand yeilds a kit that is \nas sweet to build as it is to look at. \n<p>\nThe female is 5 piecs. Upper torso, lower \nbody, right arm, left arm, left hand (which is attached to her spear). All of \nthe pieces are designed to fit so the attaching points are hidden. The finely \ncast 3 piece vest gives the builder the option of displaying her with breasts\nexposed or covered. I must say that if the vest is not used, the breasts arent\noverly exagerrated,and compliments the figure, so either way, you cant go wrong.\nOther options include a loincloth for the front and rear, and a bone type of \nweapon to be attached to her side.\n<p>\n  The wolf is cast in 4 clean, virtually seamless pieces. Main body with\nhead, two front legs and tail. Overall fur pattern is sculpted very good\nand shows no signs of being rushed, with extra attention given to the wolf\'s\nface and paws. The base is cast in one piece and adorned with varations\nof rock, vegetation ,tree stump and tree roots. There is even alignment\nplaces for the wolf and female, so there\'s no guesswork involved. The\nWolf Spirit name plate cleanly finishes off the face of the base.This\nis one of the best Laudati sculpts that I\'ve ever seen.On a scale from\n1 to 10, this is about as close to 10 as they come!'),(69,5314,'1999-05-04 21:32:46',4601,'uranusmodel2','Wow!  What a wonderful kit!  It was simply a joy to build!  I started by sanding down a bit of excess resin (not much though!)  and then assembled it.  What\'s so nice about this kit is the fact that it has male/ female matchers on all the parts.  (I didn\'t have to pin anything- it all took very well with a bit of krazy glue.)  Next, I painted the basic colors of each area.  I did some dry brushing to help highlight parts of the skirt, bow, boots, gloves, and collar.  The hair needed shading to really bring out the individual strands too.  For some final touches I added gold rubbing on the tiara and earring plus a pearl glaze over the jewel on her bow.  I find that it is these extra little details that really make the kit stand out.  Anyway, I would highly recommend this kit to anyone regardless of skill! '),(72,5206,'1999-08-19 12:02:53',4780,'saucer','The kit featured in this review is based on the early 1960\'s black and white, horror classic Invasion Of The Saucer Men.  I have very fond memories of watching this movie, as a child.   When I first saw the advertisements for the model in the Modeler\'s Resource, I knew I had to build it.\n\nThe kit arrived in decent condition.  All 28 parts were present and correctly formed.  This kit had more than its fair share of air bubbles and pinholes, though.  I spent about 8 hours over 4 sessions performing the fill, sand, prime repetitive loop.\n\nThe model is designed so the aliens are actually supporting the captive female.  The instructions don\'t do a very good job of laying out an assembly sequence to accomplish this.  The uneven surface of the kit base compounds the problem of trying to hold the female while at the same time trying to position the arms and hands of the aliens. An easy solution is to drill a small, blind hole in the female\'s lower back and insert a piece of brass rod.  Then drill a corresponding hole in the figure base.   Now the aliens can be positioned around the female\'s free floating body.\n  \nI was displeased that the aliens didn\'t really seem to have a good hold on the victim.  Their arms come fairly straight and the hands are opened flat.  To reconcile this shortcoming, every figure underwent major reconstructive surgery.  The female\'s right arm was cut and repositioned to lay across her torso, and her left arm was allowed to dangle.  All alien arms were cut at the elbows , at varying degrees of flex.  The right hands of both lead aliens were rebuilt to some degree, with copper wire, Bondo, and epoxy putty.  The hand holding the females left calf was scratch built from the palm to the finger tips.  I feel it was worth the effort to create the impression of  heft and a firm grip.  If you go this route, take your time and be prepared for a lot of repetitious test fitting.  The crown jewel of the figure modification, was the addition of the hypodermic needles in one of the alien\'s finger tips.  It\'s a critical aspect of the film, which I felt needed to be portrayed.  \n\nThe kit supplied base is a bit unusual.  It is as if the sculptor was uncertain of what textures  he/she was trying to create. There are what appears to be moss/bark covered rocks, on what would logically be dirt or grass, but looks more like sandstone or slate.  I liked the Saucer Men graphic and decided to modify  the moss covered humps and turn them back into rocks.  A small diameter steel cutter in a motor tool made fairly short work of this task.  I added Sculpt-A-Mold, various sizes of railroad gravel and static grass for ground work.\n\nIn all, this is an excellent kit, which builds up into a rather dramatic piece, despite it\'s small scale.  I would highly recommend it to builders who have a few kits under their belts.  \n\nUntil next time, may the kits be plentiful and your air compressor never die.\n'),(73,5099,'1999-09-30 19:55:22',4808,'predator','Overall this is an extremely nice kit. It won a major prize 3 years ago at the Japan Wonderfest. Which is not surprising given the quality of the casting which is first rate, no blemishes with nice detail on the skin. No offset on the mold and minimal join lines mean that it is quick and easy to prepare (no inies or outies) and it needs to be given the area that you have to paint. Assembly looks really straight forward and as all the major parts are hollow cast pinning could not be much easier. Recasts do exist but they are not hollow cast and the casting quality is poor. Each model is now done only to customer order with a waiting time of 21 days so they are exclusive. The run is supposed to be limited to 200 world wide but knowing Tony that\'s about as many as he thinks he can sell. Options on the model are right hand open, holding throwing disk or skull. Left hand holding spear or sword.'),(74,280,'1999-10-21 20:10:20',4809,'leia','Star \"Star Wars\", one of my favorite film series of all time has spawned a number of model kits One of the better kits from the film series is the \"Princess the in Slave Outfit\", a outfit\" high priced and exclusive issue from Japan. Being neither rich nor Japanese, I settled for the re-cast version. This is a rather nice looking ht. there seems to be something wrong with the sculpting though. I\'m not sure but I think that the face is too thin or that the nose is too long Other than that the attire and figure are right on. The molding and castings are way above average, only a slight seam ran around the figure some hair needed to be repaired was the worst of it very little puny head, left arm and pigtail. The collar is attached to the base This is where I decided to do some modifications.. The slave collar was removed from the base I wanted it around her neck where I felt it belonged I also modified the left arm, it was banging at her side just like the right arm. I cut the arm at the elbow drilled a hole for a pin in each section, bent a aluminum pin to fit, glued in it place, then resculpted the elbow. A lenght of chain was found to go from the collar to her left hand. The left arm, head, pigtail, and collar with chain were painted seperately from the rest of the figure. A lime puny was needed to repair the base after the collar was removed. The base is painted a stone look, with brass fixtures The flesh tones were painted using a airbrush, a base color of Creamcoat Medium Flash was followed by adding a touch of brown to the basecoat for the shadows and a Creamcoat Santa Claurs flesh color was used for the highlights. Finding the colorscheme for the costume was a bit of a task, most reference material differs in some way. I took the color of her boots from the Stand-up poster. The jewelery and decorations were done according to \"The Art of Star Wars\", painted black, then all but filled in with gold. The skirt is purple, shaded with washes and highlighted with drybrushing. The hair is as close to Auburn as I could get it. I tried for a 1970\'s style of make-up for the facial features. The collar and chain are a combination of Black and Silver, to get it a steel look, some of the gold is allowed to show threw. I mounted the figure to the base, held the collar in place while I glued the head on. Next came the left arm, which was positioned to hold the length of chain. The pigtail was added last. If your a fan of \"Star Wars\" this kit is a very nice addition to your collection, or if female figure kits are to your liking, this kit will also do nicely.'),(77,5559,'2000-02-07 00:05:20',4853,'believe','  \n \nFairly new to the modeling scene comes Pinkerton FX. I have to honestly say that when I saw a pic of this kit, I was not very set back by it. \"Another alien grey bust\", I thought. Let me reitterate on that and say just how mistaken I was! If you haven\'t seen this piece up close and personal, then you too might make the same mistake. \n\nFirst off, when I pulled it out of the box (which is packaged very nicely), I was astounded by the sheer size of it. Then as I rotated it and gave it the face to face (literally) inspection, I was amazed how lifelike it really was. Mr Pinkerton has added every possible detail into this massive piece that is humanly possible. Not only did he go into detail in adding the ear canals, but the four nasal canals as well. The pores covering the head lead into every minute fold, crease and wrinkle that you might expect to see if you were actually meeting this cosmic commando in person. From the protruding veins in the forehead, to the wrinkled chin, to the fleshy folds in the back of the neck had me looking the very cool guy over and over. Mr Pinkerton has even detailed what seems to be the focal point for me, which is the mouth. The lips are sort of drawn in, and include all of the lip lines. On the inside, is what else, but gums and teeth. \n\nFrom seeing a straight on view of this bust, you could be fooled into thinking that he has a normal, oval shaped head, but when viewed from the side, you\'ll see the it\'s not so.The head is actually a sort of a \"pod\" shape, which adds even more to the realism of it. \n\nAs for clean up work, there is virtually none! There are no mold lines what so ever. The only thing I had to do was flick out a few of those little resin balls that get caught in the deep recesses, and viola! It was ready to prime! \n\nMy wife, who I admit, is a tougher kit critic than I am, had only one thing to say about it when she saw it....\"wow!\" Out of all of the hundreds of garage kits that I\'ve owned, this is her favorite. Trust me, she\'s seen them all, and has given her, \"that\'s just stupid\" or \"that\'s really neat\" opinion, weather it was asked for or not. \n\nLet me stop rambling, and let you see for yourself. The thumbnails are linked to much larger pics. Be warned, the larger images are 480 x 640 in size, but there is no other way to show the great detail of this bust. \n\n   \n\n   \n\n   \n\nYou can contact Pinkerton FX by snail mail at: \n\n\nJohn Pinkerton \nP.O. Box 93 \nNavarre, OH 44662 \nOr by good old email:pinkfx@raex.com \n\nReviewed by: Ron Landis of Go Figure'),(78,5533,'1999-12-03 20:31:44',4878,'WORMCOV','\"For all of you X-Files fans Cryptic Creations have released their first kit, Worm Boy. Worm Boy is a super deform of the X-Files Flukeman. the castings on this little sewer dweller were fairly clean, making assembly a breeze. First I smoothed off a minimal mold line, then I drilled a hole through his hand for the plunger handle. I also pinned his feet to the base and his right arm to his body. A little putty was requiredto smooth out the gap at the arm joint. Lastly I used my pin vice to deepen the holes in his lips, before I epoxied the fangs into place. This piece does not have a whole lot of detail to it, but the size and shape of it gives the modeler a nice surface to get wild with the paint. I would suggest some tight mottling of colors to bring this sewage loving character to life. X-Files fans looking for a quick build can have fun with this one.\" **** (Dan Colonna of Amazing Figure Modeler)'),(79,5533,'1999-12-03 20:31:23',4879,'MAXCOV','\"They say that the quickest way to a man\'s heart is through his stomach, well the quickest way to mine might well be handing me a new Nosferatu figure! Tall, small, super life-like or super deformed, I want them all! Cryptic Creations are the latest kit company to try and win my heart, did they succeed? Oh yeah! The cleverly titled \"Maxferatu\" plays off the name of Max Schreck, the actor who portrayed the silent vampire, and the name of the character itself. As this is a super-deform, it is a parody of the Count, featuring enlarged head and hands. The likeness, while stylized for humor\'s sake is there, and the stiffness of the character is mimicked as well. All in all, a clever play on Nosferatu, while it doesn\'t pack a punch-line like a lot of super deforms, it certainly packs a smile. The castings were pretty good, I had to fill a few air holes and scrape some seams, but no big deal! A must for fans of the character, this is one of my favorites this issue!\" **** (David Fisher of Amazing Figure Modeler) '),(80,5627,'1999-12-11 05:52:10',4277,'','This is probably the worst vinyl kit I have ever \ntried to build. The details on the individual\npieces were wonderful, but the fit of various pieces \nwere im-****ing-possible! There was no way in hell,\nwith no amount of cutting or puttying, cursing or praying, that\nthe dragon could be built. Did I get a lemon or was the\nentire run put together by Incompotents R Us? And, by the way, it\'s vinyl and white metal, not resin as Gremlins says it is.'),(81,5627,'1999-12-09 13:30:15',4520,'','This version of the Cyclops was very good.\nThe castings were great, barely any seams, \nthe pose is great. Buy the Mad labs Cyclops Base,\nand you have one helluva scene. GeoMetric\nnever let me down.'),(82,5627,'1999-12-11 05:49:46',3664,'','Moonsinger was a very easy to build\ncold cast porcelain kit. The paint went on exquisitely, and I highly recommend it. The pose is dynamic, the base will look great with a little moss and skulls. If you put a nice moon backround behind hi, you\'ve got a great kit going for you at a reasonable price.'),(86,5956,'2000-12-02 09:50:11',5184,'gwd','<p>Dear Fellow Modelers,</p>\n<p>\nEver since my shark attack, I\'ve not been feeling to well. This all started\nlike most other days in sunny Southern California. I stopped by the Mad Model\nParty and was walking by Creature Feature\'s table when The Great White Death\njumped off the table and bit me with the need to build this mammoth kit.</p>\n<p>The Great White\nDeath is sculptor and special effects artist John Cherevka\'s personal\nproject. After many requests John has made this piece available exclusively\nthrough Creature Features and <a href=\"http://www.squidzkitz.com\">www.squidzkitz.com</a>.\nJohn\'s talents as a sculptor can really be appreciated with the release of\nthis giant kit. John\'s work can also be seen in movies like Dogma and TV\nshow\'s like Buffy the Vampire Slayer and the X Files. The feel of this kit is\nepic featuring the giant shark measuring in at 32 inches in length. This kit\nalso comes with many cool accessories, a shark tooth base, half eaten\ncorpse and a female diver. Plus the shark\'s teeth are cast out of acrylics,\nwhich are used to make dentures. Great White Death features a tremendous amount\nof detail and is well cast.</p>\n<p align=\"center\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_image9.jpg\" width=\"593\" height=\"266\"></p>\n\n<h2>Step 1</h2>\n<p align=\"center\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_image3.jpg\" width=\"431\" height=\"295\"></p>\n<p>\nAfter seeing this kit I got home as safely as possible and watched Jaws.\nThere\'s many great prehistoric kits available, but this kit is and opportunity\nto own one of the last true prehistoric creatures that exist today. John has\nadded a prehistoric feel to his sculpture. This makes the shark that much more\nmenacing. The casting\'s of this kit are really clean for a piece this size,\nwith only minimal mold lines that can easily be removed with your fingernail and\na exacto knife. The fins need to have the pore spout\'s removed but that\'s no\nproblem either. They can be grinded off or cut with and exacto saw.</p>\n<p>\nOnce the clean up was finished which tuck about 45 minutes. I glued the figure\ntogether with super glue and accelerator. Then filled in the seams with Magic\nSculpt epoxy putty. The diver and the corpse also needed a little putty work but\nthat only tuck about 5 minutes to finish. A simple build when you consider the\nsize of this beast. When building this creature I was wondering how I was going\nto paint him. Well I thought I\'ll try to make him look as much like a great\nwhite as I could. But then I started thinking I really like the paint scheme of\nthe sharks in The Deep Blue Sea, which are based on Mako sharks. Well what I\ncame up with is a paint scheme that combines the colors of each animal.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 2</h2>\n<p align=\"center\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_image7.jpg\" width=\"506\" height=\"380\"></p>\n<p>Once assembled, I premiered this beast with my favorite primer Dupli-Color\'s\nscratch filler&amp; primer. This also gives the shark a base coat of light gray\nto start with. The belly of the Great White is where he gets his name. His belly\nwas airbrushed with Titanium White.</p>\n<p align=\"center\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_image1.jpg\" width=\"502\" height=\"376\"></p>\n<p>Then a\nlight coat of Dead Guy Gray was airbrushed at the top of the White. This\nGreenish Gray color will be used to tie the White belly into the upper bodies\ncolor scheme.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 3</h2>\n<p align=\"center\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_image8.jpg\" width=\"551\" height=\"418\"></p>\n<p>\nThe broken pattern that separates the white under belly from the upper half of\nthe shark was added by using a flat brush and Freak Flex Nocturna Blue mixed\nwith Payne\'s Gray. This was applied by lightly dipping the brush into the\npaint then stippling the shark down the length of his body. I used National\nGeographic for my reference. This helped a lot with the placement of the\npattern. This edge needs to be very jagged not a smooth line. Remember this is\nand animal and nothing is symmetrical on it. Remember, it doesn\'t really need\nto be exactly like the other side. Asymmetrical is more interesting due to the\nnature of things being different. After this break line is complete I went a\nhead and colored the rest of the upper body in the same fashion using the color\nto break up the Gray base color.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 4</h2>\n<p align=\"center\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_shark03.jpg\" width=\"514\" height=\"387\"></p>\n<p>\nI then took my airbrush and highlighted the high points of the shark with Mossy\nMoor Brown, which is a dark greenish brown color. This color was also applied to\nthe rest of the upper body. Then other highlights where added with Gangreen,\nFrankenflesh. Adding these colors brought the pattern more in line with the\nGreat White\'s color scheme. Once this was complete the figure was then lightly\nmisted with Transparent Black.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 5</h2>\n<p align=\"center\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_image02.jpg\" width=\"398\" height=\"526\"></p>\n<p>Not too bad, now on to what we all like about sharks, their giant\nmenacing mouths. The mouth was already painted white so I sprayed the inside of\nthe mouth with a mixture of Red, Burnt umber. Then the teeth where base coated\nwith Bleach Bone Tan which is kind of and off White. Great White Death\'s teeth\nand mouth where then washed with Burnt Umber. The last step was to highlight the\nteeth with a light drybrushing of White. When a shark attacks its prey there\neye\'s rollover and a white membrane covers the eye. I wanted to create the\nillusion of the eye\'s rolling back; this was accomplished by painting the\neye\'s White first then placing the Black iris where I wanted. Once this was\ncomplete the figure was then coated with clear lacquer, the eyes and mouth where\nhit with a gloss coat.</p>\n<p align=\"center\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_image04.jpg\" width=\"506\" height=\"380\"></p>\n\n<h2>Step 6</h2>\n<p align=\"center\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_image07.jpg\" width=\"557\" height=\"421\"></p>\n<p>I really dig that not only does this kit come with a corpse but also\ncomes with a 4inch female diver. Her outfit was base coated with Navy Blue, the\nWhite was added the drybrushed on. This brought out the detail nicely. The\nstraps and goggles where painted yellow with a wash of Burnt Umber. Her air tank\nwas painted Gunmetal, then the air hose was applied using superglue. Next the\ncorpse, this poor chap must have been surfing one to make waves when this beast\ngot him. He was base coated with a mixture of Burnt Umber, Raw Amber and white.\nOnce dry he was drybrushed with a lighter version of the base coat then washed\nwith Burnt Umber. The mutilated area was base coated with a Hammer Horror Red\nthen washed with a dark Purple to give it that deep wound effect. The corpse and\nthe diver where then sprayed with dull coat then a light gloss coat. This gave\nthem a nice wet look.</p>\n\n<h2>Step 7</h2>\n<p align=\"center\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_image01.jpg\" width=\"349\" height=\"187\"></p>\n<p>Now on to the base this has to be one of the coolest bases I\'ve seen. I\njust dig the giant tooth. I live in Bakersfield Ca. and thousands of years ago\nthis area was covered by ocean. Once the oceans reseeded a lot of sea life died\nin this area. The giant Meglodon shark was one such animal. There\'s been allot\nof his teeth found near Shark Tooth Hill hear in Bakersfield. I\'ve seen these\nteeth and I\'m telling you there awesome and this tooth is almost a perfect\nmatch. Now on to the painting, base coated with Freak Flex Bleached Bone Tan,\nand then washed with Freak Flex Mossy Moor Brown. The outside edge was then\npainted Black. The metal rod was painted Black as well.</p>\n<p>The corpse was base coated with a mixture of Burnt Umber, Raw Umber and\nWhite. The rest of the body was painted different color Reds. Red mixed Burnt\nUmber, Purple and Forest Green, these different reds gave the corpse a nice\nmixture of deep wound color reds. Then the entire kit was sprayed with Testors\nDull Coat. The corpse was placed in\nthe shark\'s mouth and the diver was super glued to the front of the sharks\nface. This completes the painting and building of this mammoth figure once\ncomplete this piece makes and excellent addition to anyone\'s collection.</p>\nThis figure can be purchased through <a href=\"http://www.squidzkitz.com\">www.squidzkitz.com</a>\nor Creature Features in Burbank Ca. If anyone is interested in contacting John\nfor a commission sculpture or pre-painted shark please contacted <a href=\"http://www.squidzkitz.com\">www.squidzkitz.com</a>.\nThey will be able to get you in touch with him. One last thing before I go is\nthis sneak peak at one of John\'s figure busts the Mummy based on his own\ndesign. This is and in progress picture, look for and upcoming article featuring\nsome of John\'s fantastic busts. Till next time don\'t glue your fingers\ntogether.\n<p align=\"center\"><img src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_mummy.jpg\" width=\"205\" height=\"275\"></p>\n<p align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.squidzkitz.com\"><img border=\"0\" src=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/gwd_bigsq.jpg\" width=\"115\" height=\"83\"></a></p>\n'),(89,6391,'2001-12-13 14:04:32',5352,'http://www.realalien.com','Real Alien,: This is a 13.5\" finely detailed scupture of what is commonly called the \"Grey Alien\". It comes professionaly packaged in a black box encased in custom formed foam .'),(88,5956,'2001-08-07 15:49:05',5305,'mrc','<CENTER>\r\n<FONT SIZE=\"+5\"><B>Dark Carnival Models</B></FONT><BR>\r\n<FONT SIZE=\"+3\">Presents</FONT><BR CLEAR=\"RIGHT\">\r\n<IMG SRC=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/mrc4.gif\" WIDTH=478 HEIGHT=116><BR>\r\nBy <A HREF=\"mailto:wecreation@yahoo.com\">Walter J. Evans</A><BR>\r\n</CENTER>\r\n\r\n<P>The classic monsters are among my favorite creations and this version of\r\nFrankensteins monster is no exception. I liked the version Geometric released,\r\nbut Id have to say, I like this one the most. I think the pose is cool and the\r\nbase that comes with this figure complements it nicely. Sculptor Brian Hamilton\r\nhas done and excellent job in capturing Mr. Karloffs likeness. He has given\r\nthis figure a great deal of character. I feel this kit conveys the monsters\r\ntortured soul and the pain that hes endeared. I really enjoy seeing different\r\nversions of characters released, even if it is of the same actor. I know why\r\nanother Frankenstein. Well to me there is enough room in this hobby for a\r\nrepeat of  characters, especially if its one of this caliber. If youre a fan of\r\nBoris Karloff or the film its self this is defiantly a must have. Brian\r\nHamilton is definitely a name we need to keep and eye out for.</P>\r\n\r\n<P>If interested please check out\r\n<A HREF=\"Http://www.darkcarnivalmodels.com\">www.darkcarnivalmodels.com</A>\r\nthis kit retails for only a hundred bucks so its definitely priced to sell.\r\nPlus youll be able to checkout their other great garage kits. The Son of\r\nFrankenstein comes in seven clean cast resin pieces; the head, two hands and\r\narms, full body and base. The resin\r\n<A HREF=\"Http://www.darkcarnivalmodels.com\">www.darkcarnivalmodels.com</A> uses\r\nis heaver than most resins on the market. As I said before this is one\r\nexcellent sculpture, the hands and head can be positioned in many different\r\npositions. Which can make everyones kit unique.\r\n<P>Now on to the painting my friends, I just got finished watching Son of\r\nFrankenstein and thought I\'d do something different with this figure. I\'ve seen\r\na few figures painted as if they are in a Black and White movie. So I thought\r\nthis would be a neat figure kit to paint this way. With this in mind I still\r\nwanted color so my finial painted scheme is a mixture of dark and light colors.\r\nThe colors where kept almost Black or White. But you can see the use of color\r\nin the figure. The head and hands received the classic greenish tint as well.\r\n<CENTER>\r\n<IMG SRC=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/mrc16.jpg\" WIDTH=255 HEIGHT=343>\r\n<IMG SRC=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/mrc17.jpg\" WIDTH=257 HEIGHT=344>\r\n</CENTER>\r\n<P>The figure was washed in hot soapy water and left to dry over night. The\r\nMonster was then premiered. The pants where painted flat Black then dry brushed\r\nwith a dark Gray. \r\n<P>Highlights where added with two more progressively lighter shades of Gray.\r\nThis was achieved by mixing the Dark Gray with White. Franks boots where then\r\nbase coated in Black. Then dry brushed with Burnt Umber to give them a leathery\r\nlook. Once this was complete the lower body receive a light wash of Mossy Moor\r\nBrown. This was done to darken the colors a little. Badger Airbrush Company\r\nmanufactures this color in their Freak Flex line. Its a nice Greenish Brown\r\ncolor; it was also used as the base coat for the vest Mr. Karloff is wearing.\r\nOnce base coated, the vest was then dry brushed with progressively lighter\r\nshades of a whitish cream color. This was achieved by mixing white with a\r\nvery small amount of burnt umber. Then more white was added to lighten the\r\ncolor for each progressively lighter  application. Three lighter shades where\r\nadded to give the fur that much more depth. His shirt was base coated with a\r\nmixture of Mars Black and Dark Gray. Then White was added to the base coat to\r\nachieve the lighter highlight color. This lighter shade of gray was dry brushed\r\nover the high points of the figures shirt.\r\n<CENTER>\r\n<IMG SRC=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/mrc21.jpg\" WIDTH=327 HEIGHT=245>\r\n<IMG SRC=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/mrc19.jpg\" WIDTH=183 HEIGHT=243>\r\n<IMG SRC=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/mrc22.jpg\" WIDTH=504 HEIGHT=378>\r\n<IMG SRC=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/mrc23.jpg\" WIDTH=505 HEIGHT=378>\r\n<IMG SRC=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/mrc24.jpg\" WIDTH=505 HEIGHT=379>\r\n</CENTER>\r\n<P>Mr. Karloffs head and hands received a coat of Freak Flexs Suntan Flesh.\r\nNext the head and hands where then lightly misted with Freak Flex Greenzilla.\r\nThis was lightly applied to the flesh areas. The Greenzilla was then lightened\r\nwith White then applied to the top of the brow, nose, cheekbones and the\r\nknuckles and muscles of the hands. Once complete the shadow areas where added\r\nwith a dark brown made with black and Burnt Umber. His eyes and brow area,\r\nhallows of the cheeks, fingertips and between them where lightly shadowed. Once\r\nagain the original highlight color was again lightened with White and applied\r\nto the same highlight areas but with a lighter touch. This combined the colors\r\nfor a nice Greenish flesh tone. Once complete the figure was sprayed with\r\nTestors Dull Coat.  Next all his little extras where taken care of the\r\nwounds on his head and hands where first airbrushed with Hammer Horror Red-Dry.\r\nThis was applied to the wounds then lightly feathered outward. This was to give\r\nthe surrounding area a reddish infected look. Then the Red was mixed with a\r\ntouch of White and applied to the inside of the wound. Next his head clamps\r\nreceived a basecoat Black then painted Silver. I then painted the figure with\r\nTestors Dull-Coat to seal the kit. When I received the figure in the mail I\r\ndropped the head and broke off the neck bolts. So to fix this little mess-up\r\nI drilled a hole in each section that had the bolts, then inserted a nail and\r\ncut the end off with my Dremal.\r\n<CENTER>\r\n<IMG SRC=\"http://www.gremlins.com/walter_evans/mrc27.jpg\" WIDTH=326 HEIGHT=434>\r\n</CENTER>\r\n<P>This completes the painting of the classic figure, as I said, before its\r\na must have for fans of Boris Karloffs and his portrayals of the Frankenstein\r\nmonster. Plus thisfigure is licensed through the Karloff estate. This is the\r\nfirst sculpture of Brian Hamilton for www.Darkcarnivalmodels.com but it\'s not\r\nthe last keep and eye out for his Thing kitfrom the classic sci-fi movie.\r\nPlus www.Darkcarnivalmodels.com has and abundance of new and exciting kit\'s\r\ncoming out so keep checking there web site for any updates.\r\nUntil next time happy modeling.'),(90,5933,'2002-02-24 13:35:50',5134,'',''),(91,5933,'2002-02-26 15:59:55',5381,'',''),(92,5633,'2003-03-14 19:30:12',4019,'',''),(93,5575,'2006-01-24 04:29:14',3965,'dark1','It has been a while, but back at it :)');
